ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,1,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,2,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 1 General remarks 2 2 Specific remarks 4 2.1 Effectiveness of the securitisation framework 4 2.2 Securitisation platform 8 2.3 Due diligence requirements 9 2.4 Transparency requirements and definition of public securitisation 10 2.5 Supervision 12 2.6 Significant risk transfer process 13 2.7 Prudential and liquidity treatment of securitisation for banks 14 2.8 Scope of application of the Securitisation Regulation 17 ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 1
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,1 General remarks The European Central Bank (ECB) welcomes the Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,A wellfunctioning European securitisation market is an important element of the capital markets union (CMU) agenda.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The ECB’s Governing Council recently concluded that further progress at the EU level was needed to ensure that the EU securitisation market could play a meaningful role in transferring risks away from banks so that they can lend more to the real economy, while creating opportunities for capital market investors.1 Improving the EU securitisation market can help deepen the EU’s capital markets, provide lending to the real economy, and hence contribute to the CMU agenda."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Policy action should promote the sustainable growth of the securitisation market, considering the interplay with other debt and equity instruments, and incentivise the transfer of risks outside of the banking sector to those actors best placed to carry them."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Policy changes should promote simple and transparent transactions that could support financial stability and market functioning.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While securitisations can play a role to free up capacities in the banking sector and increase financing to the economy, policy changes should also target specific market segments through for example securitisation platforms, to help increase the financial means available for key policy priorities in Europe, particularly the green and digital transitions."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The ECB has long supported the EU’s objective to revive the European securitisation market, while maintaining a sound prudential framework."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The asset-backed securities (ABS) purchased programme launched in October 2014 helped to improve market transparency and to support simple ABS.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This support to simple and transparent securitisations also extended through the joint discussion paper from the ECB and the Bank of England, also in 2014, which was a seminal contribution to the development of a regulatory framework for simple, transparent and standardised securitisations."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"More recently, the ECB has been actively supporting the industry’s efforts to further standardise and simplify securitisations, with a view to allow for a streamlined and fast-track supervisory significant risk transfer (SRT) assessment process, the securitisation market would benefit from further initiatives to enhance standardisation and scaling up of the market."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The adoption of the Securitisation Regulation in 2017 and the implementation of the Basel III framework for securitisation, effective 1 January 2019, aimed to strike the right balance between the need to revive the European securitisation market by making the securitisation framework more attractive to both issuers and investors, and the need to preserve the prudential nature of the regulatory 1 See Statement by the ECB Governing Council on advancing the Capital Markets Union."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,3,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 2
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This process has been complemented by subsequent amendments to the Securitisation Regulation, which most notably expanded the scope of simple, transparent and standardised (STS) securitisations to synthetic transactions3."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Against this backdrop, the ECB response indicates areas considered most promising to enhance the framework for securitisation."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"It gives suggestions for action so that the market can develop in a sustainable manner, including by addressing issues related to the lack of demand."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,It argues that initiatives to support market development should ideally be developed at the EU level.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This would allow to achieve economies of scale in the development of such products, facilitate the upscaling of the market and support the integration of EU markets, which would broadly support the CMU."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In addition, it notes that – while the ECB is looking forward to the outcome of the consultation and concrete proposals on securitisation – this can only be seen as a first step towards a more comprehensive set of actions to deliver CMU."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Progress is needed on other challenging and open dossiers4.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Securitisation would equally benefit from the harmonisation of corporate insolvency rules, accounting frameworks, and securities law, as well as better disclosure of the financial information by EU corporates."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"These issues are key to deliver the necessary harmonisation for European markets – including for securitisation, to scale up throughout the Single Market."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"To reach progress in these areas, alternatives methods could be envisaged such as the introduction of a 28th regime as an intermediate step towards further harmonisation within the EU."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,This could be particularly useful in the areas of contract law where national differences are one aspect that limit the creation of homogeneous asset pools with underlying assets from several EU Member States.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,A 28th regime in the area of contract law could be designed as an optional instrument for contracting parties wishing to use it – whilst national laws would remain in place.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Similarly, fragmentation of insolvency laws deters investors from investing in securitisations with underlying assets across EU Member States because the lack of equal safeguards and predictability with regards to the potential losses of cross-border claims prevent them from properly assessing the risks and their chances for recovery in case of default."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"2 See Opinion of the European Central Bank of 11 March 2016 on (a) a proposal for a regulation laying down common rules on securitisation and creating a European framework for simple, transparent and standardised securitization; and (b) a proposal for a regulation amending Regulation (EU) No 575/2013 on prudential requirements for credit institutions and investment firms (CON/2016/11)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,3 See Opinion of the European Central Bank of 23 September 2020 on proposals for regulations amending the Union securitisation framework in response to the COVID-19 pandemic (CON/2020/22).
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,4,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"4 “Follow the money: channelling savings into investment and innovation in Europe”, speech by Christine Lagarde, President of the ECB, at the 34th European Banking Congress: ""Out of the Comfort Zone: Europe and the New World Order"", Frankfurt am Main, 22 November 2024 ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 3"
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,2 Specific remarks 2.1 Effectiveness of the securitisation framework A well-functioning securitisation market can play an important role for the EU’s financial market and the economy.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Securitisation can be effective in channelling money from capital markets into projects and in redistributing risks across the entire financial system.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Banks can rely on securitisation as a strategic tool to manage both capital (through capital relief) and funding.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"To achieve capital relief, securitisation requires a positive significant risk transfer (SRT) assessment from the competent authority, acknowledging that risks have been actually transferred and will not be re-assumed by originators during the life of the securitisation."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While only cash (true sale) securitisation can be used for funding purposes, SRT transactions can be carried out using either cash or synthetic structures."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The objectives of policy measures for securitisation should be to foster a more active and sustainable market development within a sound prudential framework: • Enabling a genuine transfer of risk outside the banking sector to a diversified investor base that is able to manage those risks appropriately. •
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Promoting sustainable growth of securitisation volumes, avoiding any unintended or excessive build-up of risks. •"
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Promoting simple and standardised products to buttress financial stability and attract new market players. •
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Targeting reforms to use securitisation as a strategic tool to finance the real economy and support European competitiveness, especially the green and digital transition."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Judging by the significant structural shifts that have taken place since the global financial crisis, banks have changed the way they use securitisation to meet their funding and regulatory capital needs."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While the European true sale securitisation market has decreased and stabilised at lower levels when compared with the peak during the global financial crisis, issuance volumes of synthetic securitisation and covered bonds have grown significantly in recent years."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Euro area banks use covered bonds for secured funding and synthetic securitisations for risk transfer and regulatory capital release.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Importantly, synthetic securitisations have overtaken true sale structures as the vehicle of choice for risk transfer among banks."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"When considered holistically, European securitisation issuance therefore appears to be more dynamic than when assessed only for traditional, true sale securitisations (see Chart 1, which shows the trend in traditional, synthetic securitisations and covered bonds issued in the euro area)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,5,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 4
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Chart 1 Euro area securitisation and covered bond issuance (EUR billions) RMBS Auto SME ABS Other ABS CMBS CLOs Synthetics True sale market-placed (incl.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"CLOs) Covered bonds 600 500 Revised Basel securitisation framework enters into force in the EU 400 300 200 100 - 2005 2006 2007 2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 Sources: JP Morgan, Bloomberg Finance L.P., ECB Banking Supervision, European Covered Bond Council and ECB calculations."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Notes: CLO issuance refers to EU ex United Kingdom.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Synthetic securitisation issuance refers to issuance by significant institutions supervised by the ECB; available data start in 2018.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Banks have adapted their use of both securitisation and covered bonds to best suit their needs.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Securitisation and covered bonds complement each other, sharing certain similarities but also with some important structural differences."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While securitisation enables the tranching of risk, exposing the investors in senior tranches to losses only after the more junior tranches have been depleted, covered bonds are debt securities where all investors are ranked equally."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Unlike securitisation, which is backed only by the securitised underlying assets, covered bonds provide investors with dual recourse against both the issuer and the cover assets."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Furthermore, covered bonds only provide funding benefits, making them more comparable to cash securitisations than to synthetic securitisations, which do not provide funding benefits."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In terms of remuneration, however, covered bonds are cheaper than cash securitisation, as investors are exposed to more limited risks."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Overall, the reduction in the use of cash deals after the global financial crisis and the increased use of synthetics and covered bonds show that banks have optimised their strategies to draw capital and funding benefits."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The significant increase in synthetic securitisation – done predominantly in private markets for capital relief purposes, impacting less the relationship between the bank and the client – resulted in high retention levels among banks of their own SRT securitisations (see Chart 2).5 This is particularly the case for the most senior tranches of these transactions, for which market placement is rather limited, while riskier tranches are protected by external investors."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"From a prudential perspective the high retention raises attention since in case of an unexpected credit risk deterioration in the underlying exposures, losses may be 5 Since banks keep the underlying exposures in synthetic transactions on their balance sheet, these transactions have no impact on the direct relationship between banks and their clients."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,6,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 5
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ultimately carried also by the holder of senior tranches (in this case the originating bank).
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Synthetic transactions are usually private and bilateral in nature, which implies an absence of a secondary market, impacting negatively the liquidity of the securitisation market."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In addition, in case synthetic transactions are used for capital optimisation purposes rather than for new lending, they may not contribute to the CMU objectives."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Conversely, true sale securitisation has failed to garner the same level of interest as its synthetic counterpart."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"More precisely, there has been a sharp decline in the issuance of residential mortgage-backed securities (RMBS) in the EU following the global financial crisis, while issuance of other asset classes has been subdued (see Chart 1)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Chart 2 Evolution of the capital relief market: transactions with performing loans (EUR billions)6 Source: ECB Banking Supervision.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"All in all, the evolution of EU securitisation markets has been more complex than meets the eye."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Against this backdrop, any assessment of the need to further support securitisation markets should take a holistic view that considers both supply and demand factors."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"For instance, for the time being, the investor base remains quite limited and concentrated among a few non-bank financial institutions, rendering very difficult the placement of the senior tranches by originators."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The ECB is in favour of a comprehensive analysis of the limiting factors affecting investors’ appetite to invest in securitisation tranches.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The regulatory framework should promote the development of an active and sustainable market.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Potential risks that could impair market functioning should be avoided, such as a small and homogenous investor base, unhealthy volumes, or opaque and complex structures that might pose financial stability risks."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Authorities also need to be able to monitor interlinkages between investors and the banking 6 The capital relief market includes SRT and full deduction transactions (1250%) (i.e. where all retained tranches are risk-weighted by 12.50 or deducted from CET1) on performing exposures originated by SSM significant institutions.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,7,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 6
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"sector via an enhanced supervisory framework, embedding also better statistics, in particular for the NBFI sector."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In a sustainable securitisation market, banks will channel funding and lending to the economy throughout the cycle."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Drawing on the lessons of the global financial crisis, when opaque and complex securitisations led to excessive risk-taking by originators and prevented investors from running proper due diligence, we would do well to ensure that securitisation does not create excessive leverage in the financial system by fuelling asset bubbles and hiding risks on bank balance sheets."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Incentivising simple transactions with clear and transparent objectives would help to avoid renewed stigma effect for the securitisation market.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,These features would alleviate procyclicality by minimising the risk of deleveraging and tightening of credit conditions if the economic cycle turns.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Well-functioning securitisation markets instead could potentially foster crossborder activity among banks and make banks more competitive.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In a more integrated pan-European capital market, banks could exploit economies of scale and offer more attractive products and services in several countries."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This in turn would foster intra-EU competition in the banking sector, although the exact magnitude of this effect should be further evaluated."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Achieving a pan-European securitisation market would be facilitated by ensuring closer harmonisation of insolvency regimes and better disclosure of the financial information by EU corporates, which would ultimately foster standardised securitisation of cross-border pools."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Lowering bank capital requirements would not provide further incentive to transfer risks out of the banking sector and would come at the cost of further deviations from international standards.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The ECB considers, in line with the European Supervisory Authorities (ESAs)7, that the securitisation prudential framework for banks – namely capital and liquidity regimeis not a major obstacle to the task of strengthening EU securitisation markets."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In any case, making changes to capital requirements should not leave pockets of risk unaddressed and should be compatible with international standards."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The most impactful measure for securitisation and the CMU more broadly would be to increase standardisation for example by means of an EU platform.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Enhanced standardisation beyond the STS standard and simpler processes and practices in the securitisation market would be beneficial.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Simpler and robust structures, ideally based on fully harmonised industry practices comparable with other financial products, would provide further comfort to investors while safeguarding the prudential position of the originator."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Furthermore, highly standardised structures could be effective in reducing costs related to issuance and investment and facilitate due diligence by investors."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,16,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In this way, a deeper and more liquid market could be achieved, thus facilitating higher market placement."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,17,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In the meantime elements of the regulatory framework, such as due diligence, transparency and supervision, should be reviewed to address certain supply and demand factors relevant for the market."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,18,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,This should not prevent a more 7 As included in the 2022 joint advice to the EU Commission on the review of the securitisation prudential framework.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,8,19,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 7
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,comprehensive review of demand and supply related factors that goes beyond the regulatory landscape.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"2.2 Securitisation platform Establishing a securitisation platform could bring further standardisation that would benefit uptake, making it worth exploring."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Standardisation through harmonised issuance could potentially support targeted segments of securitisation, such as green securitisation to support the climate transition, one of the key priorities of the EU."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,A European platform for green securitisation might play a catalytic role as both an issuer and a standard-setting agent.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Such a platform could potentially foster standardisation by incentivising banks to adapt the contractual terms of their loan portfolios to fit uniform criteria set by the platform.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Standardised structures issued through a single platform for pan-EU issuance could be effective in reducing market fragmentation and costs, which hold back issuance."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,They would also foster standardisation of securitisation by facilitating investor-side due diligence.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"By promoting a high degree of standardisation across European countries, the EU platform could help to increase both the supply and demand for securitisation, ultimately improving market liquidity."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"An EU structure might also be effective in lowering transaction costs for sponsors and originators and reducing information asymmetries between sponsors, originators and investors, with a targeted approach focusing on specific segments to strike the right balance between impact and technical feasibility."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,A platform targeting a broad scope of loans would offer the most potential but might be challenging to set up due to the current lack of standardisation of underlying loans.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Opting instead for a targeted approach focusing on a market segment such as green securitisation might be easier to achieve.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"However, given that green securitisation can also cover a wide variety of underlying asset classes, such harmonisation may not be that easy to implement in practice and would require further consideration."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Issues such as the ongoing involvement of originators and servicers, whether it is possible to ensure the insolvency remoteness of the issuing entity and its compliance with requirements such as risk retention obligations, would all need to be clarified."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The creation of an EU platform should not imply a trade-off in terms of financial stability.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"For example, key questions include the EU platform’s liability towards investors, especially if it assumed the role of “sponsor” (as per the SECR) or issuer liable for all repayments under the notes."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Furthermore, the platforms should be subject to adequate prudential requirements."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,16,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The European Investment Bank (EIB) provides examples of how private capital can be channelled towards common EU policies using securitisation.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,9,17,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The EIB has provided financing to companies as well as climate initiatives building on ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 8
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"securitised products, attracting both private and public investors, such as insurance corporations and national promotional institutions."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While an EU platform would provide benefits without the use of public guarantees, a guarantee programme could be designed to enhance the potential of securitisation for targeted segments such as financing the green transition, as suggested by the March 2024 Governing Council statement on CMU."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Home renovation loans or other types of green assets, such as SME loans, might be suitable candidates for this purpose."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"A public guarantee focused on a particular market segment would ensure that public support is appropriately targeted, not just to promote market development, but also to contribute towards the EU’s climate transition objectives."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,It could also limit the need for public resources compared to direct public investment and could potentially leverage on existing funds such as InvestEU and the European Investment Fund (EIF).
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Such a programme should preferably be implemented at EU level in order to foster harmonisation, risksharing and pan-EU issuance."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"2.3 Due diligence requirements Investors in securitisations need to conduct their own due diligence when they invest in securitisation, in order to ensure that they fully understand the risks and the characteristics of securitisation structures."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"However, overly prescriptive due diligence might deter new investors from entering the market, and they could also generally limit secondary market activity."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Due diligence requirements could be made proportional to lower the burden on investors, though they should remain detailed and prescriptive in the interests of legal certainty."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"To properly assess the risk of any securitisation transaction, investors are currently required to assess its risk characteristics and structural features, covering the areas described in Article 5(3) of the SECR."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"However, due diligence requirements could be made proportional with respect to compliance with the STS criteria, as currently set out in Article 5(3c) of the SECR."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The ECB supports the work that the joint committee of the ESAs is currently undertaking to define due diligence proportionality under Article 5.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Introducing proportionality increases reliance on existing obligations among the parties notifying a securitisation as STS-compliant.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,This would require enhanced supervision of STS compliance and trends by ESMA and the joint committee of the ESAs.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Additionally, the due diligence obligations of investors under Article 5 of the SECR in respect of risk characteristics and structural features should be streamlined to capture more explicitly the following areas: 1."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Analysis of the credit quality of counterparties who are involved in the transaction on an ongoing basis after issuance (such as originators, original lenders, servicer, swap provider(s), issuer account bank or liquidity facility providers)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,10,16,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 9
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Analysis of interest rate risk (and where relevant currency risk), including hedges, as well as liquidity risk."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"However, institutional investors should not differentiate their due diligence analysis based on whether the transaction is public or private, as doing so would not distinctively affect the intrinsic risk characteristics or structural features of the transaction."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In the case of repeated transactions, the due diligence process could leverage on previously gathered information and analysis."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Repeated or similar transactions typically belong to an existing ABS programme and present the same underlying asset type, as well as the same sell side parties and creditgranting criteria."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Therefore, the due diligence process could focus only on those aspects that diverge from previous issuance, while usefully relying on the due diligence analysis of previous transactions."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"However, for this approach it would be helpful to have a clear notion of what qualifies as a “repeated transaction”, as well as a declaration from the issuer clearly identifying those securitisation aspects that are fully repeated from those that are not."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Meanwhile, investors should be given a relatively long period of time following the investment in which to document and demonstrate compliance with the verification requirements of the due diligence process, such as 45 days, which should be enough to comply with these requirements."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,2.4 Transparency requirements and definition of public securitisation The transparency framework is a central building block for the development of securitisation markets in the EU.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The introduction of standardised loan-level templates and transaction disclosure requirements in the aftermath of the global financial crisis was key to promote a high level of transparency in the EU securitisation market.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The current transparency regime is geared towards ensuring that investors in a securitisation have all the information they need for their due diligence processes.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"It also allows users of standardised data, such as that obtained from credit rating agencies, research and analytical service providers, to assess the risk characteristics of securitisation portfolios over time."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The Eurosystem is also a key user of this information for its collateral framework and its due diligence in the context of its ABS outright purchase programme.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The current transparency regime is also conducive to the standardisation of information gathering during the origination process of securitised exposures.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The goal of past Eurosystem-initiated initiatives was to foster a sufficiently high degree of transparency in the securitisation market.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Hence, the ECB’s views expressed in this reply are based on the lessons learned from the introduction of loan-level data requirements for asset-backed securities eligible as collateral for Eurosystem credit operations and the subsequent adoption of the ESMA templates – replacing the ECB templates – as reporting templates for the loan-level data requirements."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,11,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 10
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The ECB sees significant value in maintaining the current standardised templates, albeit with some modifications to make them more usable by investors."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Standardised templates allow for data comparability, which is essential to achieve transparency and critical for investors who are looking to invest in securitisations."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This standardisation would be lost when using unprescribed templates, which would adversely affect future market development due to the proliferation of different transparency standards."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,This lack of transparency could even lead to financial instability.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Moreover, the processing and handling of the data would expose investors to increased operational risk when performing their risk analysis."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"However, the ECB also sees merits in exploring the possibility of adjusting the transparency framework and the required templates, especially based on whether the securitisation transactions are public or private, with the latter following a more flexible approach in terms of format and the level of detail required in the templates, among other options."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"If this approach is ultimately taken, the ECB would suggest defining public transactions as broadly as possible so that all currently eligible Eurosystem securitisations (ABS fulfilling both the general criteria for marketable assets and specific eligibility criteria for ABS set out in Guideline ECB/2014/608) remain subject to detailed standardised template data requirements as stated in Annex VIII of Guideline ECB/2014/60."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Securitisation repositories (SRs) should continue to play a role in collecting and distributing securitisation information.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"To reduce the level of opacity in some segments of the securitisation market, all securitisations (public and private) could be notified to SRs and, as a bare minimum, SRs should collect standardised data templates for public transactions and simplified notification templates for private transactions."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The principle-based approach for investors (as proposed in question 5.5 of the Commission’s consultation document) cannot be fully evaluated given that no details of its design and functioning are available to date in the public domain.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"If a principle-based approach is pursued further, it would require additional work and significant in-depth analysis."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,It would be useful to examine the potential ramifications and emerging risk areas (climate-related) with the goal of presenting a more comprehensive proposal.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,A prudent and diligent analysis of the risks associated with a securitisation transaction is heavily dependent on what information is available.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Thus, the proposal to streamline the current templates for public securitisation should not preclude reporting only the most significant metrics needed to assess the relevant financial risks and valuations of securitisations."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The current templates have some room for improvement to achieve a higher degree of usability and ease the burden on the reporting entities, while still allowing them to disclose critically significant information."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Additionally, any review of the existing disclosure templates should incorporate new climate-related indicators and assess certain data quality concerns (e.g. excessive misuse of no data options, particularly ND5, which compromises the 8 Guideline (EU) 2015/510 of the European Central Bank of 19 December 2014 on the implementation of the Eurosystem monetary policy framework (General Documentation Guideline) (ECB/2014/60) (OJ L 091, 2.4.2015, p. 3)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,12,16,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 11
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,completeness and representativeness of loan-level data) to effectively enforce data quality.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Securitisation repositories, as approved market data infrastructures, could play a role in supporting any process of adapting or modifying the existing templates."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Access to climate-related data is needed to adequately assess the increase in transition and physical risks arising from climate change.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Including risk indicators related to climate change can give investors the information they need to assess the impact and exposures of risks arising from climate change.9 Introducing a minimum number of data metrics, aligning with other EU regulatory criteria, such as the EU taxonomy or Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR) disclosure requirements, would allow for a more rigorous assessment of the associated climate-related risks without unduly burdening the reporting agents or requiring unreasonable efforts to capture the necessary data."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Attempt to streamline or modify the templates would benefit from a prior consultation process.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This would benefit from the active participation of market participants (i.e. reporting entities, investors, credit rating agencies (CRAs), central banks, legal firms, etc.)"
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,in the design of the revamped templates under ESMA’s supervision and oversight.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"ESMA, also coordinating with the Joint Committee of the ESAs, should have the final say on the adoption of the new templates to ensure that sufficient levels of transparency and standardisation are maintained."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB Banking Supervision does not anticipate any significant impact on supervisory costs if this option is ultimately pursued.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"As part of the streamlining exercise, the question should be asked as to whether a transition to clustered information from granular data is justifiable in some cases."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"For credit card receivables, moving away from loan-level data would make sense on account of the exposure of these revolving assets being highly dynamic and not easily captured by loan-level data, and also because of their short duration (the information is already obsolete when published)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Disclosing information in the case of intra-group transactions and securitisations below a certain threshold could create problems.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In particular, it should be ensured that any securitisations retained by Eurosystem counterparties should not fall under the definition of intragroup transactions."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"2.5 Supervision The ECB welcomes the proposal of a unified or greatly coordinated centralised supervision, considering the successful experience of the SSM securitisation hub.10 The SSM Hub has been created as a new form of cooperation between the ECB and the national competent authorities (NCAs)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,This allows the SSM to pool 9 Please refer to the joint statement by the ECB and the European Supervisory Authorities published in March 2023.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"10 For more details, see, for instance, “Supervisory priorities and securitisation”, keynote speech by Elizabeth McCaul, Member of the Supervisory Board of the ECB, at the 26th Annual Global ABS Conference, Barcelona, 14 June 2022."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,13,16,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 12
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,resources and be more effective and consistent in the supervision of the requirements laid down in the Securitisation Regulation.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,It should aim to ensure a level playing field of high-quality regulation and supervision without regulatory arbitrage or a race to the bottom among Member States.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,One of the ESAs could coordinate such a team and the Joint Committee of the ESAs could oversee it.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Potentially, a joint supervisory hub of supervisors or a joint pan-European oversight network, along the lines of the model established under the Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA), with ESAs and competent authorities pooling common resources and expertise, could be viable models meriting further analysis."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"ECB staff recommend the creation of a dedicated task force to define in more detail the scope, supervisory model, governance and required resources."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"To maximise the benefits in terms of enhanced coordination and lower supervisory costs per average transaction, the scope should cover all securitisations and compliance with the SECR, including STS requirements.11 Additionally, the new structure could support the development of the securitisation market by removing inconsistencies in how the SECR is implemented across the EU by various national supervisors and by introducing a single-entry point for market participants."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,2.6 Significant risk transfer process The ECB Is actively contributing to increase standardisation in the market jointly with the industry with the aim to streamline the process for assessing significant credit risk transfer via securitisation.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Regarding the significant risk transfer (SRT) process, the ECB generally distinguishes between (i) simple, standardised and repeat deals; and (ii) complex and innovative transactions, for which the supervisor should have room to run the comprehensive assessment of the SRT."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"For complex transactions, an in-depth supervisory review of securitisations with difficult features is needed to assess SRT robustness, which would enhance the financial resilience of supervised banks."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,An industry-led initiative to simplify securitisation under the current applicable framework was launched earlier in 2024.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"From a supervisory perspective, the issuance of such simple and non-complex securitisations (which may or may not qualify as STS transactions) would allow for a streamlined and fast-track SRT assessment process, on which the ECB is currently working."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The fast-track process would involve the following key steps: • Creating a standardised process and template to speed up the supervisory assessment of SRT, especially for non-complex transactions with more standardised features, based on pre-agreed criteria."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"11 For more details, please refer to the ESAs’ Consultation Paper on the harmonisation of conditions enabling the conduct of the oversight activities under Article 41(1)(c) of Regulation (EU) 2022/2554."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,14,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 13
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"• Reducing the approval process time, thus allowing for faster time to market. •"
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Shifting the focus from an ex-ante review of individual transactions to riskbased bank-level supervision of securitisation activities.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"We believe that the current regulatory framework offers enough flexibility to increase the efficiency of the SRT process, which can be exploited both by supervisors and industry."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"If this simplification process is successful, the results could feed into further work and guidance on a fast-track SRT assessment by the EBA at EU level."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Lastly, the CRR mechanical tests for SRT (Articles 244(2) and 245(2)) present certain constraints, notably regarding the lack of control over the right thickness of transferred securitisation tranches, which have been acknowledged also by the EBA.12 Going forward, we see merit in further exploring alternative, more prudent methodologies that would ensure that banks transfer to third parties a sufficient share of the estimated credit losses arising from the securitised exposures."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In this context, further guidance on technical aspects in the form of Level 2 measures related to the assessment of significant risk transfer and the related quantitative criteria could be developed more broadly to ensure consistent treatment across the EU, which would also benefit originators."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"2.7 Prudential and liquidity treatment of securitisation for banks The current regulatory framework, which took effect in the EU in 2019, reflects the Basel III standards agreed to by the Basel Committee in 2014 to address shortcomings in previous regulations that came to light during the global financial crisis."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The Financial Stability Board noted recently, in its draft evaluation report on securitisation, that the reforms of the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision (BCBS) and the International Organization of Securities Commissions (IOSCO) have helped to make the securitisation market more resilient, without compelling evidence of material negative side effects on financing to the economy.13 The EU framework was amended in 2021 to include new elements that go beyond Basel standards, such as the extension of the preferential treatment for STS securitisations to include synthetic transactions."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Generally, the ECB is of the view that prudential requirements should be commensurate with the risks embedded in securitisation structures."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Overall, the view held by ECB staff is aligned with the conclusion set out in the 2022 European Supervisory Authorities’ joint advice on securitisation."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The ESAs proposed to improve the consistency and risk sensitivity of the capital 12 See EBA report on significant risk transfer in securitisation under Articles 244(6) and 245(6) of the Capital Requirements Regulation.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"13 See Evaluation of the Effects of the G20 Financial Regulatory Reforms on Securitisation: Consultation report, Financial Stability Board, July 2024."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,15,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 14
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"framework for banks, but that re-calibrating the securitisation prudential framework would not be a solution that in itself would ensure the revival of the securitisation market."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,It is unlikely to lead to significantly higher true sale issuance and can even be said to raise certain prudential concerns.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Any potential changes to the regulatory framework should be assessed against the objective of transferring risk outside of the banking sector, to widen the investor base for securitisations and to support a sustainable and healthy market."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"It should not promote complex or non-transparent transactions, as the benefits of those transactions for financing the real economy are limited and risks for banks and investors are high."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"As identified by the ESAs, the current prudential regulatory framework in the EU might benefit from targeted improvements in the framework’s risk sensitivity, to further enhance the differentiation between the actual risk profile of underlying asset pools, structural features and model and agency risks."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Relative to other financial products like covered bonds, securitisations are more complex due to the tranching of credit risk and do not exhibit the same features."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This requires a more prudent approach, while also exploring potential changes to the regulatory regime."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Many technical and structural features can affect securitisation performance and, in turn, the capital position of originators and the risk held by investors."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,A more risk-sensitive prudential treatment would do a better job at reflecting risks and thus allow for a closer calibration of capital requirements applicable to banks.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In this regard, any targeted measures to lower requirements should be strictly limited to securitisation positions of a very high quality."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"To maintain a prudent approach, it is essential to consider the fundamental role played by safeguards in securitisation structures, as proposed by the ESAs."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Any targeted changes in the regulatory treatment should be accompanied by a comprehensive set of safeguards to ensure resilience in securitisation throughout the economic cycle.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While further analysis is needed on the impact of the p-factor, it seems appropriate to preserve proper capital non-neutrality since securitisations entail significant model and agency risk."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This would prevent cliff effects that might be triggered by inadequate reductions of capital charges for mezzanine and junior tranches, which inherently carry the highest model risk."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The prudential framework is key to avoid pockets of risks being unaddressed, which could generate ripple effects across the financial system, amplifying vulnerabilities."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Potential changes to the prudential rules should be considered very carefully, and should not be reached in isolation, but taking into account the entire framework and based on a thorough impact assessment."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,16,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Given this complexity, the ECB would support first having discussions at the Basel level."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,17,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"These would encompass also other shortcomings with the current framework that were identified by the ESAs, and only then changing the framework."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,18,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Any shortcomings identified should then be assessed in terms of their materiality.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,19,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Considering the structural changes in the European securitisation markets, any further change should also consider how banks use securitisation at present."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,16,20,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 15
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,2.7.1 Output floor The effects of targeted regulatory changes for securitisations falling under the internal model-based approach for securitisation (SEC-IRBA) – which accounts for the lion’s share of SRT transactions – need to be carefully assessed.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The output floor should be implemented gradually from 2025 onwards due to increasing adjustment factors, coinciding with the potential introduction of regulatory changes (if adopted by EU legislators)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Any further changes to the existing framework should acknowledge that the temporary exemption granted by EU legislators14 for calculating the output floor application represents a significant regulatory support factor.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In this regard, the impact of potential further changes on the difference in requirements between internal model-based and the standardised approaches should be considered."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Failing to do so might lead to further deviations from the Basel framework.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"For prudential reasons, we deem it appropriate to secure alignment with the Basel framework and avoid turning the temporary arrangement into a permanent one."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Estimated total losses in the underlying portfolio are a key parameter in the capital structure of a securitisation, chiefly affecting the carving up and size of the tranches placed with external investors in order to achieve capital relief."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Estimated total losses are also a key input in determining capital requirements on retained tranches, and the capital benefit is generally higher under SEC-IRBA than under the standardised approach (SEC-SA)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Acknowledging that the output floor will have an impact on the economic decision of banks on whether to enter into a securitisation, the ECB would support further analysis of the issue in order to find a prudentially sound, long-term solution."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Total estimated losses related to the underlying pool shape the capital structure and magnitude of the tranches sold to external investors in SRT securitisations.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Ensuring a proper calibration of such estimated total losses, in line with generally conservative requirements applied to internal models used outside the securitisation framework, would render securitisation structures more robust."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,This would incentivise banks to sell thicker credit-protected tranches.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In contrast, a more lenient regulatory treatment for internal models used in securitisations compared to the broader use of models for credit risk, would render the SRT structures more fragile, potentially inducing further model and agency risks in securitisation."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"In turn, this might affect the due diligence process carried by external investors, who would rely on an underestimated loss of the underlying pool that would be disclosed by the originator bank."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"2.7.2 Liquidity treatment It is still unclear whether and if so, to what extent, the current the treatment of securitisations in the liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) treatment has been an 14 i.e. reduction of the p-factor under SEC-SA for the purpose of calculating floored risk-weighted exposure amounts (see Article 465(13) CRR3)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,17,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 16
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,explicit determining factor in securitisation issuance and investment activity among banks and for the liquidity of the securitisation market in the EU.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"As regards significant institutions under the direct supervision of the ECB, the share of securitisation to total LCR liquidity appears negligible overall."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"As of 30 September 2024, and looking at significant institutions at the highest consolidated level in the euro area, the weighted average share of securitisations of total liquid assets before applying haircuts and caps is 0.6% (the number gets even smaller when factoring in LCR haircuts)."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"At individual level, 29 out of 109 significant institutions report securitisation exposure in their LCR buffer."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"It is rarely the case that significant institutions are constrained by the caps on liquid assets, including the 15% cap for Level 2B assets."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"What this means is that they could theoretically hold more Level 2B assets, including securitisations, before being constrained by the cap."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This could indicate that the LCR categorisation as well as the haircuts applied for securitisations are not, on a stand-alone basis, a limiting factor for securitisation activities."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,7,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Overall, it appears premature to consider upgrading the LCR treatment of securitisations at this stage."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,8,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Instead, the ECB deems it necessary to conduct an analysis of liquidity risk related to securitisation positions before concluding in favour of a change in the LCR treatment of securitisations."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,9,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Such analysis should provide evidence that securitisations have a proven record as a reliable source of liquidity, even during stressed market conditions."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,10,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While the ECB acknowledges the benefits provided by securitisations for the purposes of risk management in general, even in terms of diversification, securitisations have not been sufficiently tested over the past decade, embedding also stress events that are consistent with the scenarios referred to in Article 5 of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2015/61."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,11,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This is further emphasised in the 2022 joint response to the call for advice to the EU Commission on the review of the securitisation prudential framework, according to which no LCR stress period in the banking system has been observed in recent years, including the period spanning the COVID-19 pandemic."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,12,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"The ECB would call for inserting in Level 1 a mandate for the EBA to perform an in-depth scenario analysis of securitisation liquidity, with a view to reassessing the liquidity characteristics of securitisations, including during periods of idiosyncratic and market-wide stress."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,13,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"Changes in market practices that improve the standardisation and simplicity of products, the diversification of the investor base and ultimately market depth could also be considered positive factors in this regard."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,14,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,2.8 Scope of application of the Securitisation Regulation The ECB would welcome further clarifications regarding the scope of application of the Securitisation regulation.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,18,15,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The ECB welcomed the Opinion of the Joint Committee (JC) of the ESAs on the Jurisdictional Scope of Application of the European Securitisation Regulation (SECR) and clarifications made by the ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 17
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,19,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Commission in the 2022 report from the Commission to the European Parliament and the Council on the functioning of the Securitisation Regulation.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,19,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"However, the ECB has called for further clarity as to whether the SECR applies to any securitisation where at least one party (whether on the sell side, such as originators, original lenders, securitisation special purpose entities (SSPEs) or sponsors, or buy side, such as investors) is based, or authorised to operate, in the EU."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,19,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,EU-based and EU-authorised entities should be responsible for fulfilling the relevant SECR provisions assigned to those entities.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,19,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,The ECB would welcome an expansion of the definition of eligible sponsors.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,19,4,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"While the current SECR definition of a securitisation transaction as a transaction that involves tranching of credit risk is well understood by the market and supervisors and does not require modification, the definition of eligible sponsors could be expanded to include alternative investment funds established in the EU, since these entities are able to fulfil this role in the current marketplace."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,19,5,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"This type of entity should meet the SECR conditions applicable to sponsors, such as risk retention or disclosure requirements."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,19,6,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 18
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,20,0,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,"© European Central Bank, 2024 Postal address 60640 Frankfurt am Main, Germany Telephone +49 69 1344 0 Website www.ecb.europa.eu All rights reserved."
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,20,1,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,Reproduction for educational and non-commercial purposes is permitted provided that the source is acknowledged.
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,20,2,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,For specific terminology please refer to the ECB glossary (available in English only).
ecb.conresp202412.en.pdf,20,3,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework,European Central Bank,D:20241203083104+01'00',D:20241203082500+01'00',,ECB staff contribution to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the functioning of the EU securitisation framework 19
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,1,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets June 2025
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,2,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Contents 1 General remarks 2 2 Specific remarks 6 2.1 Simplification and burden reduction 6 2.2 Trading 8 2.3 Post-trading 10 2.4 Horizontal barriers to trade and post-trade infrastructures 16 2.5 Asset management and funds 22 2.6 Topics for consultation on supervision 27 2.7 Horizontal questions on the supervisory framework 41 ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Contents 1
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"1 General remarks The European System of Central Banks (ESCB) welcomes the European Commission’s targeted consultation on the integration of EU capital markets, recognising it as an important step in fostering a more unified and resilient financial landscape across the European Union.1 Deep and well-developed capital markets supported by a harmonised regulatory and supervisory framework are essential to enhancing cross-border investments across EU Member States, improving businesses’ access to finance, boosting competitiveness and ultimately supporting sustainable economic growth throughout Europe."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB is committed to contributing to this consultation process by offering insights and expertise to help shape policies that will foster deeper integration and greater efficiency in our capital markets.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,We look forward to continued collaboration with the Commission and other stakeholders in this important endeavour.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Harmonising regulations and removing national divergences are crucial to simplifying the regulatory framework and creating a single, resilient market capable of withstanding economic shocks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Simplifying the regulatory framework by transforming directives into regulations can reduce implementation delays, complexity and costs, fostering consistent implementation of rules across the Single Market."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Harmonising rules, reducing undue complexity and doing away with duplicative requirements should be guiding principles in this process."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Addressing differences in insolvency regimes and ensuring robust regulations are essential to creating a level playing field and enhancing market attractiveness.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB also advocates for a consistent data-sharing framework to reduce reporting burdens and enhance financial stability.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Establishing a central supervisory reporting data hub led by the European Securities and Markets Authority (ESMA) could improve data accessibility and usability, as it would enhance data access to national and European authorities while also streamlining reporting obligations for the industry and avoiding duplication and overlap."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In the area of trading, the ESCB supports the European Commission’s efforts to remove barriers to EU capital market integration and reap digital technology benefits."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Deep, integrated and liquid markets can better withstand economic shocks, and cross-border equity markets should be prioritised for their role in risk sharing and innovation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB advocates removing barriers to European cross-border operations and liquidity aggregation.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It welcomes initiatives to streamline the pre-trade transparency regime and reduce complexity while emphasising the need to calibrate transparency against potential negative effects, such as detection of orders due to information leakage."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Introducing a consolidated tape will already contribute to achieving these objectives, contingent on timely and high-quality data delivery."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Digital technology is also a means to help integrate liquidity pools across the EU, overcoming fragmentation caused by non-interoperable systems."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB suggests carefully evaluating the extension of trading hours to manage liquidity risks and maintain 1 The National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg highlight that parts of the topics covered in this ESCB reply are outside their respective and direct areas of responsibility.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,3,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – General remarks 2
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,market attractiveness to international investors while considering the impact on post-trade infrastructures.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Despite recent progress, integration of securities post-trade services in the EU continues to be hindered by many of the barriers already highlighted in the Giovannini and the European Post Trade Forum (EPTF)2 reports."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The most significant remaining barriers are the legal uncertainties stemming from fundamental differences in national securities and corporate laws with regard to rights in book-entry securities, as well as in taxation and corporate event procedures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Beyond these areas, barriers also remain in registration, shareholder identification and, to a lesser extent, settlement."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, barriers stem not only from national laws and regulatory or supervisory practices but also from legacy market practices and market participants’ behaviour."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A coordinated effort towards harmonisation and standardisation, alongside adjustments to legal, fiscal and collateral frameworks, is essential to advancing the capital markets union (CMU) and leveraging new technologies efficiently."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The deployment of distributed ledger technology (DLT) in post-trade services presents opportunities for harmonisation and efficiency.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, it also brings challenges, as it requires new technologies and new entrants to be accommodated in the ecosystem and regulatory framework without creating fragmentation or an unlevel playing field."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The ESCB plans to continue its role in fostering these developments, working with public bodies and market stakeholders at both European and international levels."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Regarding asset management and funds, the ESCB strongly supports the Commission’s focus on removing barriers to the European cross-border operation and marketing of investment funds that affect costs and accessibility for EU citizens and promoting EU investor participation in capital markets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Removing barriers within the European fund market is key to promoting retail investor participation with low-cost products and financing EU investment priorities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Currently, barriers prevent the distribution of funds across the EU, implying lower scale and higher costs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The overall goal should be to reduce discrepancies in the implementation of existing rules, as well as simplifying processes for the authorisation and functioning of passporting regimes."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, some amendments could be considered to increase specialised investors’ participation in specific fund segments (such as venture capital) without reducing protection for less experienced investors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Targeted flexibility in portfolio rules could improve fund attractiveness without undermining financial stability and investor protection.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In line with the 2024 Governing Council statement3, the ESCB supports integrated supervision of EU capital markets, which could be achieved gradually and considering specific sectoral features."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The 2024 Governing Council statement called for direct EU supervision for the most systemic cross-border capital market actors – in cooperation with their national supervisors.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In the short term, strategically important sectors with systemic/European relevance should be prioritised for EU supervision in order to manage cross-border risks in the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Different models could be explored to promote integrated supervision, with varying levels of ambition 2 A temporary market body set up by the European Commission, the EPTF delivered a comprehensive report on barriers to securities post-trade services in 2017."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"3 Statement by the ECB Governing Council on advancing the Capital Markets Union, 7 March 2024."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,4,20,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – General remarks 3
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,also reflecting different views within the Eurosystem and the European System of Central Banks.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A first option could be a two-tier model, where significant players would be directly supervised by a European authority, in cooperation with national supervisors, while less significant players would remain under the supervision of national authorities, which would therefore retain a strong role."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The criteria to determine the significance of supervised entities could, at least initially, be designed in such a way that only the most systemic, European cross-border players would be subject to direct EU-level supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A second option could be a gradual shift of selected supervisory powers to a European authority.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This approach could benefit from a predefined timeline, creating certainty on the concrete next steps towards stronger integration of supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Integrating European supervision should be prioritised for sectors and market actors whose activities have a European cross-border footprint and particular systemic importance, such as market infrastructures and crypto-asset service providers (CASPs)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This is particularly relevant for EU central counterparties (CCPs), at least for those with significant European cross-border relevance, which could be supervised directly by ESMA, possibly in cooperation with the relevant national competent authorities (NCAs)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Similarly, steps towards integrated supervision should be taken for central securities depositories (CSDs) with important European cross-border activities or that belong to groups where market integration could benefit from common supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"EU-level supervision of CASPs that display higher risks due to their large size, high amount of cross-border activity or their being part of a large, globally operating CASP would reduce regulatory arbitrage and improve efficiency, with ESMA playing a key role in overseeing significant CASPs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Additionally, more integrated supervision of asset management and funds – for example by entrusting ESMA with the supervision of asset managers and funds with significant European cross-border activities or by creating joint supervisory teams (JSTs) – would support financial stability and the integration of this sector, given the growing size of these markets and their inherent cross-border nature."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It would also help remove barriers within the European fund market, which is key to promoting retail investor participation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Embedding a macroprudential perspective into fund and asset manager supervision is also critical to safeguarding financial stability.4 In each case, the scope of entities subject to direct EU-level supervision should be based on clear and objective criteria, including size and cross-border activity across the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"An effective, integrated EU supervisory framework for capital markets could offer significant benefits, including cost reductions, elimination of infrastructure duplication and enhanced market confidence."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, drawing on lessons from the banking union, a two-tier model would involve direct EU-level supervision for major entities and harmonised national oversight for smaller firms."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This model would support market integration and cross-border activities across the EU, while maintaining flexibility for domestic markets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Governance of the EU supervisor should ensure independence and Europe-centric decision-making, potentially through an Executive Board of independent members complemented by a Supervisory Board that includes national authority representatives."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Voting rules 4 See Eurosystem response to EU Commission’s consultation on macroprudential policies for non-bank financial intermediation (NBFI), published in November 2024."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,5,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – General remarks 4
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"should prevent national vetoes, especially for sectors with a strong European dimension."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,These governance arrangements would require some targeted changes relative to the current set-up of the European Supervisory Authorities (ESAs).
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The long-term efficiencies and economies of scale of an integrated system will outweigh short-term initial costs that will occur in the transition, such as set-up costs and investments."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Additionally, changes in supervisory responsibilities and governance should be matched by adequate funding to ensure independence and operational effectiveness."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Overall, a tailored governance and funding model is essential to guaranteeing effective supervision and to advancing capital market integration in Europe."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The Czech National Bank, the Central Bank of Ireland, the National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg, the latter highlighting that it is not entrusted with direct supervisory responsibilities in this field, judged that integrated supervision could ultimately be achieved in different ways."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In particular, while the Czech National Bank, the Central Bank of Ireland, the National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg concur that it is critical to deliver a more integrated and efficient approach to supervision within the Single Market, they view that this could be better achieved through greater supervisory convergence, rather than direct EU supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,6,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – General remarks 5
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2 Specific remarks 2.1 Simplification and burden reduction The ESCB supports the Commission’s initiatives to remove barriers to the integration of EU capital markets.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Eliminating obstacles to market integration would support the creation of a single, deep and liquid capital market across the EU, enhancing cross-border investment opportunities as well as increasing its attractiveness, facilitating more efficient capital allocation and increasing funding options for businesses."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Harmonising European rules and practices across the Member States would also help simplify the European regulatory framework.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Harmonising regulations and removing national divergences would allow the emergence of a truly single capital market that can better withstand economic shocks and support sustainable economic development across the EU.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It would also reduce undue complexity and costs associated with cross-border financial activities in the EU, making it easier for investors and issuers to participate in the European market."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The complexity observed within the European regulatory landscape is in large part driven by national fragmentation.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, differences in insolvency regimes can constitute a significant obstacle to further capital market integration.5 Uncertainty regarding recovery rates and creditor ranking hinder the EU markets for cross-border debt claims and securitisations."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A uniform and robust regulatory framework is key to ensuring Europe’s stability and attractiveness as an investment destination.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Having a harmonised regulatory and supervisory framework that adheres to international standards would make EU capital markets more attractive: an uneven playing field does not foster competitiveness among globally active entities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Rather, a robust regulatory framework is essential to ensuring investor protection and enhancing the attractiveness of European capital markets for European and international investors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This would in turn foster greater investment inflows and support economic resilience.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The benefits of having uniform regulation at European level can far exceed the costs associated with implementing such regulation.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While the discourse often highlights the costs of regulation, it is equally important to consider the significant costs of non-regulation.6 These include societal costs arising from the compliance burdens of navigating disparate national rules and financial crises."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Harmonising regulations within the EU would alleviate these issues, as adhering to a single European framework is simpler than complying with 27 different national frameworks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,5 The Eurosystem has previously highlighted that a common definition of insolvency as well as harmonisation of the ranking of insolvency claims across countries could further foster cross-border capital market transactions.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The relevance of insolvency regimes for cross-border investments is documented in Kliatskova, T., Savatier, L.B. and Schmidt, M. (2023), “Insolvency regimes and cross-border investment decisions”, Journal of International Money and Finance, Vol."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"6 See, for example, an ECB analysis assessing the economic costs and benefits of the Basel III finalisation package for the euro area, which shows that the transitory costs of the reform are outweighed by its permanent long-term benefits."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,7,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 6
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A more integrated single rulebook would ensure more consistent rule implementation across the Single Market.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Transforming directives into regulations, for instance, can be a strategic step towards harmonising rule implementation within the Single Market and enhancing its transparency and predictability, provided due consideration is given to proportionality and subsidiarity."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This harmonisation would reduce regulatory arbitrage, where financial entities might exploit differences in regulations to engage in riskier behaviour."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Full harmonisation also removes national regulatory barriers to the free movement of market operators and capital by creating a level playing field, reducing compliance and transaction costs for corporations and intermediaries and allowing them to compete freely."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, it is important to establish a clear, predictable and proportionate regulatory framework to avoid undue costs from overregulation (if the fundamental objectives are preserved) and to prevent duplication and internal inconsistencies."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This approach, alongside effective supervision, would facilitate a more uniform and therefore efficient regulatory environment, enhancing the overall integration of the EU financial system (and, as a consequence, its stability)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Another strategic step towards the creation of a single rulebook would be to streamline and consolidate regulatory frameworks.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, in the asset management sector, the current complexity of the framework reflects the temporal stratification of rules, built on the two main directives for undertakings for collective investment in transferable securities (UCITS) and alternative investment fund managers (AIFMs), followed by several product-specific Level 1 legislative acts (such as the European venture capital fund (EuVECA), European social entrepreneurship fund (EuSEF), European long-term investment fund (ELTIF) and money market fund (MMF) regulations), numerous Level 2 legislative products (delegated regulations and directives) and Level 3 guidelines and questions and answers designed to clarify specific aspects of those rules."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this context, streamlining the whole EU framework by grouping rules on asset managers in a single piece of regulation could be explored."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Regarding possible adjustments to regulatory thresholds, it is crucial to not unduly limit authorities’ ability to monitor and identify potential financial stability risks from smaller funds."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The consultation document asks whether the AIFM Directive’s threshold for sub-threshold AIFMs should be amended to take into consideration market evolution and/or cumulated inflation.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Such considerations can usefully be assessed as part of regular reviews of legislation to ensure they do not overly constrain market activity or ignore broader developments (such as inflation).
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At the same time, it is important for authorities to be able to identify any emerging risks to financial stability that could arise from the collective behaviour of cohorts of smaller funds, for example due to similar exposures or correlated redemption risk."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Any adjustments to the thresholds should therefore ensure that comprehensive supervisory coverage will be maintained.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A more consistent framework for data sharing would reduce the reporting burden for entities and increase financial stability.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Eliminating duplicative reporting requirements under national and EU legislation can help reduce the reporting burden – and therefore costs – for entities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,8,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"From a financial stability perspective, the ESCB as a whole and its members – under the monetary and ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 7"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"financial stability mandate – do not have direct access to entity-by-entity supervisory data already reported under the AIFM and UCITS Directives, the Money Market Fund Regulation, Solvency II and Markets in Financial Instruments Directive (MiFID)/Markets in Financial Instruments Regulation (MiFIR).7 Similarly, supervisory authorities do not necessarily have direct access to granular data collected by the ESCB for statistical purposes."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ECB aims to address this in a forthcoming ECB recommendation amending Council Regulation (EC) No 2533/98.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At present, there is no harmonised framework for sharing granular data on non-bank financial intermediation (NBFI) across jurisdictions, thus limiting authorities’ and central banks’ capacity to understand the flow of capital within the EU, assess potential vulnerabilities stemming from cross-border NBFI activities across the EU and monitor the effectiveness of policy measures that could be subject to cross-border leakages."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This is a major obstacle that hampers the ability of central banks and supervisory authorities to safeguard financial stability and needs to be addressed.8 Among other benefits, enhancing data access and data sharing can help streamline reporting obligations for industry and avoid duplication and overlap."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this regard, the ESMA Task Force on Integrated Reporting is examining how to improve efficiency, consistency and accuracy across existing reporting frameworks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, the establishment of comprehensive data-sharing and access mechanisms – such as designating ESMA as a central data hub – would be highly beneficial and merits further exploration."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Such a hub could help improve data accessibility, interoperability and usability, while also enabling national supervisory market authorities and European authorities as well as central banks to work on the same data in a unified manner, without duplication."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.2 Trading Developing cross-border capital markets across the EU, in particular for equity, has been identified as a policy priority given that equity is considered to be especially conducive to risk sharing and innovation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This is especially the case during financial stress periods, when financial markets are likely to suffer from abrupt tightening."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Instruments that are contingent on the financial situation of the borrower and could be interrupted during downturns are particularly desirable from a risk-sharing perspective.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, equity securities are beneficial in this respect, as they entail state-contingent payoffs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While equity holders receive compensation depending on the financial situation of the borrower, they also share the downside risks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For these reasons, evidence shows that international cross-border holdings of equity may be better able to provide resilient capital flows and guarantee a higher degree of long-term risk sharing than debt securities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Equity markets are also more conducive to facilitating innovation.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The reasons for this stem from the propensity of equity investors to also fund innovative sectors rich in intangible projects, and their typically longer-term investment horizon."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Irrespective of the benefits of developing equity markets, greater integration of fixed income markets would imply deeper and 7 See Eurosystem response to EU Commission’s consultation on macroprudential policies for non-bank financial intermediation (NBFI), published in November 2024."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,9,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 8
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,more liquid markets for lending and borrowing and could help facilitate portfolio diversification and risk reduction.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As a consequence, the ESCB supports further investigation of barriers to cross-border operations in the EU and regulatory and non-regulatory barriers to liquidity aggregation, including those on a cross-border basis."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, regarding the pre-trade transparency regime, the ESCB generally welcomes initiatives that streamline the regime for equities with a view to reducing complexity.9 At the same time, changes to the pre-trade transparency regime must be carefully calibrated to balance the trade-off between more transparency and market functioning."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Although simplification generally makes the monitoring of dark trading levels less complex, it could also lead to orders being detected and frontloaded."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, streamlining or abolition of waivers should be weighed against potential negative price effects from trading on the lit market."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Instead, thresholds on the minimum trading size for using specific waivers could be (re)assessed."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In any case, the impact of any amendments should be assessed before introduction given the complex interaction of different waivers, just like market functioning should be reassessed after introduction."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB looks forward to the introduction of a consolidated tape.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To ensure the quality of the data produced, the prompt delivery of the data provided to it by market data contributors (investment firms, trading venues, approved publication arrangements and systematic internalisers) will be essential."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB believes digital technology can support the integration and connection of liquidity pools across the EU.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In fact, non-interoperable technological ecosystems in each country – shaped by diverging national regulatory regimes – have created siloed pools of asset liquidity, further entrenching fragmentation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, recent advancements in digital technology offer an opportunity to create an integrated European capital market for digital assets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In particular, developing a new financial market infrastructure based on tokenisation, DLT and new technologies for central bank money settlement could integrate Europe’s fragmented financial market, contribute to the objectives of the savings and investments union (SIU), strengthen Europe’s position and secure the euro’s relevance internationally."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Efficiency gains would enhance the competitiveness of the European financial market and support economic growth.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB believes that the extension of trading hours over the short term needs to be carefully evaluated.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"On the one hand, longer operating hours could enable more agile liquidity management as they would allow market participants to adjust liquidity positions more effectively and react promptly to liquidity shortages, thereby reducing liquidity risk."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Sufficiently long trading hours are also a key element in keeping EU capital markets attractive to international investors, given the time zones European markets span."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At the same time, liquidity risks may also increase if longer operating hours lead to extensive financial activity outside normal business hours."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In particular, if certain traditional funding sources like repo markets or central bank 9 See the ECB opinion on the Proposal for a Regulation of the European Parliament and of the Council amending Regulation (EU) 600/2014 as regards enhancing market data transparency, removing obstacles to the emergence of a consolidated tape, optimising trading obligations and prohibiting receiving payments for forwarding client orders."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,10,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 9
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"lending remain unavailable during these times, high outflows may lead to liquidity shortages and financial stress, which need to be mitigated."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Finally, the impact of a significant extension of trading hours on the mainstream European post-trade/settlement infrastructure over the short term is a complex challenge as it has implications for all stakeholders throughout the transaction value chain (e.g. pre-trade and post-trade functions, back-office operations, CCPs, CSDs) as well as ancillary ecosystems (e.g. repo markets)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this vein, it is worth noting that the Eurosystem has launched a public consultation on T2 operating hours."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.3 Post-trading 2.3.1 Barriers to cross-border settlement and other CSD services The Central Securities Depositories Regulation (CSDR) has significantly advanced European integration by establishing common definitions and fundamental rules for core CSD services, which include the settlement of securities transactions and the safekeeping of securities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, the CSDR has failed to achieve its vision of full freedom of choice for issuers and a competitive and efficient CSD landscape in the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Despite the common rules in the CSDR, CSD and post-trade services remain subject to some idiosyncratic national legal requirements as well as to legacy national market practices."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These cover, among other things, services provided to issuers (beyond the core notary function, e.g. related to idiosyncratic corporate events, or services related to regulatory compliance by issuers), tax-related services, regulatory reporting, registration requirements and restrictions on forms of securities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Article 49 of the CSD Regulation has not been implemented according to the original intent of the lawmaker and does not achieve freedom of choice of CSD for issuers.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Article 49 subjects issuers’ freedom of choice of CSD to existing national corporate and securities laws, which Member States have interpreted to mean that the CSDR requirement is to be subordinated to their existing civil, securities and corporate law frameworks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, not all relevant idiosyncratic provisions from national securities, corporate or other relevant laws have been removed, hindering the free choice of issuance location and contradicting the first sentence of Article 49."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This also affects the implementation of CSD passporting rules, which are subject to the same idiosyncratic national corporate and securities law obligations and supervisory requirements."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The list compiled by Member States on the key provisions of corporate or similar laws under Article 49 may not be a useful analytical tool for stakeholders, as most Member States only report the article numbers of their respective national acts or copy the text in their official language(s)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,11,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Instead of simple references to relevant provisions, Member States could be invited to prepare a thorough, English-language analysis (considering all relevant provisions) on whether and how (i) a foreign issuer can issue securities in the CSD(s) within their jurisdiction; and (ii) an issuer resident in their ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 10"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"jurisdiction can issue securities in foreign CSDs within the EU, focusing on what provisions would prevent issuers from doing so and why."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The EU lacks a harmonised securities and corporate law framework, which has led to legal uncertainties regarding the ownership rights attached to book-entry securities in the cross-border holding of such securities within the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As highlighted by the Giovannini and EPTF reports and elaborated by the reports of the Commission’s Legal Certainty Group, the lack of harmonisation in fundamental national securities and corporate laws gives rise to uncertainty about their application across borders within the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,One of these uncertainties stems from the fact that the rights of securities owners are not (or not fully) recognised by national laws if certain national idiosyncratic rules on holding chains or account service providers are not followed.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, if an investor – for the purpose of holding the securities – uses an account provider established in a different Member State to the Member State in which the securities were issued, the rights of the investor are different or restricted compared with investors who hold the securities via an account provider established in the same Member State."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Accordingly, key definitions such as bondholders and shareholders differ across jurisdictions, which also introduces uncertainties in the implementation of existing and future EU acts, such as the Shareholder Rights Directive."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Furthermore, the EU has only partially established common rules and lacks an overarching conflict of law rule framework for rights attached to book-entry securities (similar to global initiatives such as the Hague convention)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These uncertainties are even more pronounced for DLT-based securities, as national DLT securities laws were adopted by Member States building on their existing securities laws and without considering or harmonising where existing conflict of law rules can be applied."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The Place of the Relevant Intermediary Approach used in most relevant EU and national conflict of law rules is often not applicable in a DLT context.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"When developing harmonised securities laws, or possibly a 28th regime, for DLT-based securities, the EU could build on efforts by UNIDROIT, which in 2023 adopted the Principles on Digital Assets and Private Law."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In asset servicing, compliance with existing market standards (e.g. TARGET2-Securities (T2S), Single Collateral Management Rulebook for Europe (SCoRE)10, shareholder identification and corporate action market standards) remains insufficient and continues to be deprioritised by several market stakeholders."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Although T2S and the Eurosystem Collateral Management System (as major European infrastructure initiatives) helped increase awareness of and, to some extent, compliance with common market standards in settlement and asset servicing, overall compliance with the relevant European standards remains insufficient.11 This is primarily due to a lack of collective action (insufficient incentives for individual market stakeholders to devote resources to changing their processes to ensure compliance with harmonised standards) and to residual conflicting local regulatory requirements stemming from corporate or tax laws."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,10 A set of standards endorsed by AMI-SeCo.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"11 See the most recent AMI-SeCo compliance reports on progress with T2S harmonisation standards, on corporate events and shareholder identification standards and on SCoRE standards."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,12,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 11
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Some of the barriers to free and seamless cross-CSD settlement, previously deemed dismantled, remain, despite two-thirds of EU CSDs now using T2S. The possibility to settle, in central bank money, any security issued in the EU through a single CSD relationship has been the core proposition of T2S and one of the key indicators of a true CMU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Today, for reasons not limited to national laws and regulations, that proposition is out of reach for most investors, issuers and intermediaries due to12: • persisting uncertainty around national legal requirements on the place of settlement; • the fact that the network of CSD links is far from complete, especially with regard to smaller markets and CSDs; • the continued restriction by several stakeholders (issuers, CCPs, trading parties) on the location of settlement, requiring that their counterparts maintain securities accounts with the issuer CSD or elsewhere; • frictions in settlement between T2S and non-T2S CSDs; • lack of demand-side awareness of the possibility of cross-CSD settlement; • inertia or inability to collectively move to common market practices, resulting in issues such as different messaging, improper use of certain messaging elements, not updating or not consistently using standing settlement instructions, or hardcoding required settlement locations in internal systems, which perpetuate fragmentation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While the number of links between EU CSDs has increased in the last decade, the use of these links remains low across the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This is shown by (i) the low share of cross-CSD settlement compared with the total volume processed, and (ii) the low overall share of most CSDs’ securities holdings with other CSDs in T2S. There is a significant discrepancy between larger and smaller CSDs in the EU with regard to number of links."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Smaller CSDs tend to have no or only a limited number of links, both as investor and/or issuer."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Although a single link can potentially be used to access multiple markets through relayed connections (with T2S automatically and seamlessly handling such relayed links at the technical level), this capability is not utilised to any significant extent."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Nevertheless, stakeholders can also use channels other than cross-CSD settlement for cross-border transactions by relying on custodian networks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Thus, the use of CSD links is not a prerequisite for increased cross-border settlement activity."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The insufficient availability of CSD links limits the efficiency of the ecosystem.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The reluctance by CSDs to set up links to each other cannot be justified by technological barriers, because T2S provides a common pathway for most CSDs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Establishing links involves considering the business case, which is influenced by varying local market practices."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Most CSDs cite differing asset servicing (corporate 12 Based on feedback from the survey conducted by AMI-SeCo in February 2024 and recent industry reports, such as by AFME in 2023."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,13,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 12
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"events, tax and other regulatory) requirements in foreign markets as a reason for not establishing links to other CSDs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At a technical level, T2S offers seamless and fully automated operation of CSD links for settlement."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, CSD links are not only used for settlement but also for asset servicing of the securities that are held via such links."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, promoting the availability and use of CSD links as well as the efficiency of cross-border investments in the post-trade domain requires the underlying fragmentation and barriers in asset servicing to be addressed."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Creating a pan-European post-trade environment where links between CSDs are easy to set up and use and where cross-border custody services are cheap and efficient requires ambitious measures on a legal and regulatory basis for asset servicing as well as the development of common market practices and standards.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Local rules and specific features regarding corporate event processing, including the lack of compliance with European corporate event standards, differing withholding tax processing requirements, differing registration requirements and other idiosyncratic national requirements or associated legal uncertainties should be eliminated to the extent possible."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,T2S was built as a settlement platform enabling seamless intraand cross-CSD settlement against central bank money.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In terms of technical functionalities, T2S fully supports cross-CSD settlement and, where CSD links are available and set up on the platform, removes any technical impediments hampering cross-CSD settlement by making it as straightforward as intra-CSD settlement."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While T2S offers direct advantages for settlement between CSD participants, its benefits in harmonising related processes also translate to transactions which are not settled at the level of CSDs by offering the same technical services to intermediaries (settlement agents or custodians) across all participating CSDs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, the share of cross-CSD settlement on T2S remains low, which is due to several factors related to the way T2S stakeholders use the platform."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition to the barriers highlighted above regarding CSD links, administrative and behavioural restrictions on the location of settlement by key stakeholders, such as issuers and CCPs, also limit the use of cross-CSD settlement even where CSD links are available."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Furthermore, inertia is observed as a result of legacy practices (e.g. settlement at issuer CSD hardcoded in back-office systems) of market participants and a lack of awareness of the possibility of cross-CSD settlement."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These barriers need to be removed by reviewing market practices, by further increasing awareness among market stakeholders and by collectively following a common roadmap, supported by the public sector."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,T2S is a multi-currency settlement platform and hence is technically capable of serving central bank money settlement in any currency.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The T2S framework has the necessary standing contractual and governance elements to onboard and serve additional currencies (in addition to EUR and DKK, which are served today)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, whether new currencies are onboarded to T2S remains a decision of the central banks of those currencies."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,14,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 13
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The current functional scope of T2S covers securities settlement only and does not extend to other activities or areas of post-trade services (e.g. issuance, asset servicing or pre-settlement transaction services such as allocation or trade confirmation)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The current T2S scope is influenced not only by initial agreements in the T2S project by the Eurosystem with participating CSDs but also by a lack of harmonisation and differing national requirements for issuance and asset servicing.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The current fragmentation in this domain would make it very challenging for a single platform to cover all functionalities and user needs (in the extreme, 27 different sets of procedures and rules) stemming from asset servicing and issuance."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, a common European platform to serve these CSD services could only be feasible if national requirements (stemming from both national law and national supervisory requirements) were harmonised under a common rulebook of issuance and asset servicing."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The EU’s Settlement Finality Directive (SFD) has been crucial in harmonising settlement finality rules and providing protection against risks stemming from insolvency of system participants.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"New, innovative settlement platforms should also benefit from protection equivalent to that offered by the SFD framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,There is a clear need for a well-defined settlement finality regime for DLT-based transactions.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Participants in Eurosystem exploratory work on new technologies for wholesale financial transactions tested a settlement model based on interoperability between central bank and external DLT platforms.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Atomicity, in the ESCB’s understanding, is not only a legal concept but also refers to operationally and technically ensuring “all or nothing settlement” of delivery versus payment (DvP) or payment versus payment (PvP) transactions on a gross basis as simultaneously as possible."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The exploratory work highlighted that specific technical mechanisms and procedures on DLT can ensure “atomic settlement” of transactions to reduce counterparty and operational risk.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, in the absence of a well-defined settlement finality legal regime, there remains a legal risk that the atomic transaction must be unwound should one of the parties become insolvent."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, SFD protection is also important for settlement systems or mechanisms based on DLT that settle transactions atomically."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, novel practices are introduced in DLT ecosystems (e.g. interlinking central bank money and commercial bank money transfers at a technical level, which could be offered by systems not designated under the SFD)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This is indicative of a possible restructuring of the current ecosystem and intermediation with the expected further accelerating adoption of DLT, which should be taken into account when reviewing relevant SFD provisions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, emerging innovative service providers (e.g. those offering DLT) may not be able to apply the SFD framework as the SFD provides an exhaustive list of types of institutions that are eligible to be participants in a system designated under the SFD."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, it should be investigated whether removing undue restrictions on types of participants in designated systems in the SFD could be feasible, without endangering the protections to systems regarding settlement finality."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This could help extend the benefits of settlement finality protection to additional emerging innovative systems.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,15,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 14
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.3.2 Uneven/inefficient market practices and disproportionate compliance costs As regards information sharing between CSDs and authorities under the CSDR, it would be to a large extent premature to assess the impact of the changes introduced under CSDR Refit."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Indeed, the related regulatory and implementing technical standards, in particular those pertaining to the review and evaluation process and the criteria for the establishment of supervisory colleges, have not yet been adopted and implemented."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB continues to welcome the forthcoming establishment of supervisory colleges under the CSDR as a way to enhance information sharing between competent and relevant authorities and to promote supervisory convergence.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The ESCB would also support any technical solution that further facilitates the exchange of information pertaining to the authorisation and the review and evaluation of CSDs, including a central database or platform operated by ESMA."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"If appropriately designed, in combination with regulatory provisions clarifying the authorities’ access to information, such a platform could allow CSDs to provide the required documentation in a standardised manner and allow relevant authorities to access without delay and in a harmonised manner the information relevant to their tasks and their role under the CSDR."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The information requested by authorities under the CSDR does not entail duplication and is proportionate to the related activities and regulatory requirements.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The ESCB notes that there is by design no duplication possible under the CSDR of the information requested by relevant authorities and the information required by competent authorities, as relevant authorities only receive all or a subset of the information provided by the CSD to the competent authority for the purpose of the authorisation or the review and evaluation.13 The ESCB generally considers that the reporting requirements for CSDs are commensurate with the importance of the functions they perform for the smooth functioning of securities markets and with the information needs of competent and relevant authorities to assess their compliance with the CSDR and other regulatory requirements."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The timeframes for authorisation procedures under the CSDR are already very constrained.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The ESCB notes that the current timeframes for relevant authorities to deliver opinions under Articles 17(4) and 55(5) CSDR (three months and two months respectively) are already very short, taking into account the need to carry out the related assessments of the applicant’s compliance."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It should also be noted that the length of the authorisation procedure typically depends largely on the time needed for the applicant to develop a complete application, given the need to provide documentation covering a wide range of regulatory requirements."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The proposal to allow market participants to provide core CSD services independently under the CSDR should be studied further to clarify its potential benefits – also in view of the actual market demand for such a possibility – and 13 Overseers may have additional powers under national law to collect information to discharge their responsibilities, but this is not foreseen by the CSDR."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,16,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 15
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Notably, most CSDR provisions apply horizontally to a CSD regardless of the functions it performs, including most organisational requirements (governance, record-keeping, outsourcing), conduct-of-business rules (transparency, communication procedures) and prudential requirements (covering legal risks, general business risks, operational risks and capital requirements)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,They would therefore need to apply uniformly to all core service providers authorised under the CSDR.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It should thus be considered whether further fragmenting the market infrastructure value chain is advisable: many local financial market infrastructures in the EU already have small operations and limited activity, and sometimes find it more difficult than larger operators to meet high standards of financial and operational resilience."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, provisions that apply to two or more core services would need to be clarified to clearly allocate responsibilities in case they are provided separately (e.g. provisions on the integrity of the issue)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Finally, maintaining interoperability and open access across these various infrastructure service providers could prove challenging and would require strong safeguards and enforcement to prevent further fragmentation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It should however also be noted that technological change may have a profound impact on the current ecosystem, market structures and possibly the distribution of roles in capital and payments markets, to the extent allowed by (future) regulation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Intermediaries are already actively investigating changing the current scope of their services with the adoption of DLT.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This should be taken into account when further studying the above-mentioned proposal.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.4 Horizontal barriers to trade and post-trade infrastructures 2.4.1 EPTF barriers The EPTF barriers have not been addressed on a significant scale by lawmakers and regulators or the industry.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Based on recent detailed investigations by the Eurosystem’s Advisory Group on Market Infrastructures for Securities and Collateral (AMI-SeCo), as well as from its own experiences, the ESCB believes that most of the barriers reported by the EPTF in 2017 remain in place."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While significant steps were taken under the Commission’s CMU action plans in shareholder identification and in tax processing, these have yet to bear fruit in terms of integration benefits."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To give an example, AMI-SeCo recently ran a survey on settlement restrictions at EU sovereign issuers where it was confirmed that EPTF Watch List Barrier 1 is still in place."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, it should be noted that many of the barriers highlighted both in the EPTF report and, more recently, by AMI-SeCo based on its survey of 202414 do not stem from lack of regulatory action but from practices by market stakeholders where adaptations could be made without changes in underlying laws or regulation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,17,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,14 AMI-SeCo survey on remaining barriers to integration in securities post-trade services ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 16
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.4.2 Issuance Beyond the barriers noted above, which generally affect cross-border securities services, issuance processes specifically are subject to inefficiencies due to the lack of a commonly used data model, the lack of transmission of machine-readable reference and transaction data and divergence in the use of market conventions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Based on the report by the ECB’s Debt Issuance Market Contact Group, there are three sources of inefficiencies and an absence of integration in European securities issuance processes: • lack of a single, trusted “golden source” for security reference and corporate event data, which hinders efficient regulatory reporting and processing of corporate events; • frictions in exchanging standardised, machine-readable data to allow the settlement of primary market transactions in an efficient and timely manner (including International Securities Identification Number allocation and propagation in the issuance process); • use of legacy market conventions (business day rules and calendar, interest/coupon conventions, rounding rules, etc.),"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"which cause frictions or media breaks in trade and post-trade processing; despite significant progress in the convergence of practices in euro area bond markets, legacy local conventions persist, which make both trade and post-trade operational processes inefficient and cumbersome without bringing any economic benefits."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Most of the issues relate to the lack of adoption of a common data model for issuance (starting with representation of term sheets and data elements of prospectuses).
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"If market incentives prove insufficient in creating momentum towards the adoption of such a common data model, EU-wide regulatory action should be considered by the lawmakers."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.4.3 Innovation – DLTPR and asset tokenisation The ESCB supports the continuation of the DLT Pilot Regime (DLTPR) and its adaptation to better enable the development of DLT-based market infrastructures, while mitigating the related risks and maintaining a level playing field with traditional infrastructures.15 These conclusions are based in part on the experience gained by the Eurosystem through its role as relevant authority under the DLTPR, as well as the trials and experiments on new technologies for central bank money settlement16 and the extensive and structured engagement with market stakeholders in this context."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The DLTPR and the permissions granted under it should be extended into the long term or made indefinite, until a pathway to the regular 15 Please note that this section also covers some of the questions raised under Section 3.2 “Barriers to the application of new technology and new market practices” of the consultation document."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,16 The Eurosystem ran trials and experiments by providing three distinct DLT-based services to all eligible market stakeholders in order to settle in central bank money the euro cash leg of wholesale transactions conducted using DLT.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The trials and experiments were conducted between March and November 2024.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For further details, see the ECB’s website."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,18,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 17
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"regulatory framework is defined, in order to provide applicants with sufficient planning certainty."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A possible way forward could be to introduce a two-stage approach in the DLTPR, maintaining the current approach for new entrants and experiments under the existing thresholds while introducing a second level for more mature firms with more limited exemptions and more prescriptive requirements (see below) as an intermediate step."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Over the longer term, the pilot regime should allow for convergence with the ordinary regulatory framework, with the necessary adaptations to allow for the coexistence of traditional and new technologies, also in view of a level playing field between newcomers and incumbents."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESCB would for the time being advise against a principle-based approach.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A principle-based approach would base supervision on general regulatory principles that can be adapted to each entity authorised under the framework, as opposed to a more prescriptive rules-based approach detailing the specific requirements regulated entities must meet."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"First, such an approach would be nearly impossible to apply consistently across the EU, unless the supervision of DLT market infrastructures is transferred to a single EU-level authority."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Second, the EU legal framework does not allow for the full delegation of rule-making powers granted to EU institutions under the Treaties in a way that would make a principle-based approach legally workable."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Third, the gap between a principle-based approach and the highly detailed and prescriptive requirements of the CSDR and its technical standards would further deepen the discrepancy and unlevel playing field between the DLTPR and the regular framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,It would also make bridging this discrepancy in the future even more difficult.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As an alternative, the DLTPR could be further reviewed to examine if certain prescriptive requirements, especially those set out in technical standards or guidelines, constitute obstacles to the development of new entrants and could be disapplied for entities that remain below the existing DLTPR thresholds."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The limits on the value of financial instruments traded or recorded on DLT market infrastructures should be reviewed, while ensuring that regulatory requirements remain proportionate to the activity."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Increasing the DLTPR thresholds should be considered in order to provide some headroom in case of an uptake in activity.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This should go together with a thorough review to ensure that the regulatory framework is sufficiently clear and prescriptive and ensures a level playing field: as DLT market infrastructures are allowed to grow, the framework should converge towards a similar level of prescriptiveness as for traditional infrastructures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A careful balance should be struck to ensure adequate safety standards for more developed infrastructures, while preserving the capacity of new entrants to innovate and scale up to some degree."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It could be considered whether possible exemptions remain appropriate for larger DLT market infrastructures: for instance, it may not be appropriate to exempt them from CSDR requirements relating to outsourcing, participation criteria, transparency or the protection of client assets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"DLT-specific requirements (e.g. Article 7 DLTPR), which are currently very high-level, could also be further specified, for instance through ESMA technical standards or guidelines developed in consultation with the ESCB."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,19,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The DLTPR should continue to be aimed primarily at new entrants, or at giving incumbent financial institutions the possibility to experiment with DLT up to a ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 18"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For new entrants, DLTPR thresholds may not be the primary issue, although clarity on their potential to scale may still be important for their future planning, and consequently also for current investments."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The main issue is the time needed to develop a mature solution for the infrastructure and the requisite risk management processes and documentation to meet regulatory requirements.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To this end, further clarity in the requirements and guidance on how to implement them may be helpful for new entities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Accordingly, mandating ESMA to develop guidelines, in consultation with the ESCB, could constitute a flexible approach and utilise the expertise gained by ESMA in the application of the DLTPR."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Conversely, established financial institutions aiming to use DLT to provide large-scale market infrastructure services should do so under the regular legislative framework, including the CSDR and the SFD, in which case they would not be subject to DLTPR thresholds (see below)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As regards the possibility to provide CSD core services separately from settlement, please refer to Section 2.3.2 above."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Extending the scope of the financial instruments allowed in DLT market infrastructures should be considered, while taking into account the necessary implications for the regulatory framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The current restrictions on the scope of financial instruments and deviations from MiFID definitions may make customer acquisition difficult for new entrants.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Still, the scope should in any case remain limited to the scope of financial instruments defined by MiFID, to maintain the level playing field with traditional trading venues and financial market infrastructures, and the distinction with MiCAR-authorised entities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The articulation with national legal frameworks for digital financial instruments should also be taken into consideration.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"If repos and especially derivatives are considered for inclusion in the scope of the DLTPR, this will raise the question of how to integrate central clearing in the processing of these instruments."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This could also require compliance with the European Market Infrastructure Regulation (EMIR) requirements.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This should be further studied, also in the context of a wider analysis of the evolving role of intermediaries."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To enable established financial institutions to provide large-scale market infrastructure services based on DLT, the primary lever should be a progressive adaptation of the regular framework to enable DLT-based services alongside traditional infrastructures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As a first step, CSDR definitions (“dematerialised form”, “transfer order”, “securities account” and “book-entry form”) could be adapted to enable the use of DLT-based and token-based solutions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Further steps would entail changes to provisions on settlement discipline, settlement finality, communication procedures and links between infrastructures, but these may be premature, as the experience with DLT-based infrastructures is currently limited."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The provisions of the current legal framework are sufficiently general to mitigate and control the risks arising from using a permissioned DLT in a securities settlement system (SSS).
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,20,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This is the case if: (i) the technical rules governing the functioning of the DLT exclude decentralised validation and a decentralised governance structure; (ii) the services provided via the DLT are limited to those offered by the SSS and do not deviate from those performed by an SSS not based on DLT; and (iii) either the CSD operates the underlying DLT infrastructure or ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 19
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"the scope of “outsourcing” is interpreted as in the current legal framework, and not as in the interpretation set forth in Recital 31 DLTR (i.e. it also encompasses the delegation of tasks related to the operation of a DLT SSS or the use of DLT to perform settlement)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In such arrangements, additional technical standards may prove beneficial in specialising general requirements to address DLT-specific operational and custody risks (for example, risks related to smart contract failures and custody failures)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Conversely, settlement systems that utilise permissionless DLTs must take into account additional governance risks (such as those associated with contentious forks), settlement process risks (chain reorganisations, double spending, availability incidents and maximal extractable value exploitation) and legal risks (particularly those stemming from varying interpretations of outsourcing)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The DLTPR requirements on cash settlement should be reviewed to provide better articulation with the regular framework and further clarity in their application.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To promote the use of central bank money and avoid excessive risk-taking in this essential component of securities settlement, Article 5(8) DLTPR should be reviewed, considering the following principles. •"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Settlement in central bank money should continue to be required for DLT market infrastructures whenever it is practical and available.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This would help promote a transition to central bank-operated settlement solutions, in the currencies for which such solutions are developed, as soon as they become available. •"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Settlement in commercial bank money, including in tokenised form, should remain permitted without compliance with Title IV CSDR only below the current DLTPR thresholds – including if the DLTPR thresholds are increased."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Activity above these thresholds should be subject to Title IV CSDR, including the possibility of using a commercial bank that does not meet the requirements of Title IV under specific thresholds foreseen under Article 54(5) CSDR. •"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Settlement in e-money tokens issued by a single e-money institution should only be permitted under low thresholds such as those currently in force in the DLTPR.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Above these thresholds, cash settlement should rely on more robust solutions towards which DLT market infrastructures should transition, which could include reliance on multiple e-money institutions to ensure risk diversification, provided this can be done in a safe and efficient manner. •"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Cash settlement through the accounts of the CSD (or, equivalently, cash-equivalent tokens issued by the CSD on the DLT trading and settlement system (TSS) for the benefit of their members, participants and clients) should be permitted in compliance with Title IV CSDR."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It should also be clarified whether Title IV applies when a DLT TSS is operated by an investment firm authorised under a national transposition of MiFID, in case the cash available for settlement on the DLT TSS is fully prefunded into an omnibus account held by the investment firm at a credit institution and protected only by MiFID prudential and risk management requirements."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,21,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The deployment of DLT to provide financial services, including post-trade services, should preferably rely on permissioned platforms – whether public or ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 20"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,private – while the use of permissionless DLT should be carefully regulated.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Permissionless blockchains are publicly accessible platforms operated by a distributed network of entities which cannot be regulated (and usually not even identified), called validators."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In principle, permissionless blockchains may offer several benefits: (i) by operating without a central authority, they could act as a neutral platform with a global reach, with the ability to operate across jurisdictions; (ii) by employing distributed consensus among multiple entities, they may avoid single points of failure, enhancing security and resiliency; (iii) their open-source nature fosters innovation and the development of a diverse ecosystem of applications and services, which contributes to the growth of the platform; (iv) they benefit from strong network effects, given the multiple assets available for settlement and the diverse range of financial protocols deployable as smart contracts."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, several risks (in addition to those also present in permissioned platforms) should be considered and, where possible, mitigated by the regulatory framework: (i) the role of unregulated validators exposes DLT users to risks associated with the settlement process (related to decentralised consensus failures such as chain reorganisations, double spending and maximal extractable value exploitation); (ii) co-locating regulated financial services with other services on the same public DLT can expose participants of the financial market infrastructure using DLT to additional operational risks, including cyber threats and scalability issues; (iii) failures in decentralised governance may result in contentious DLT forks, where a single DLT infrastructure splits into two distinct infrastructures with different properties, such as divergent sets of validators and governance structures; (iv) the security of permissionless DLTs hinges on a sufficient degree of decentralisation, regarding both governance structures and validators."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, there is no widely accepted definition of a decentralised DLT."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Some DLTs may have centralisation points – for instance, a centralised entity holding a de facto majority in DLT consensus – which are challenging to assess."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Others may have centralisation drivers, such as high capital requirements to become a validator, and could eventually evolve into centralised platforms."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Employing permissionless DLTs that are, in effect, operated by one or more central entities exposes users to the same risks as a centrally managed platform, albeit without equivalent legal protections, as validators remain unidentifiable and unregulated."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These risks must be properly mitigated by the regulatory framework: for instance, the use of permissionless DLTs could be subject to relatively low limits such as those currently applied in the DLTPR, to ensure that they do not pose a systemic risk, as well as to enhanced investor protection requirements."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, infrastructure operators using this type of technology could be required to develop a plan for addressing settlement, governance and operational failures and to demonstrate that the permissionless DLT they use is subject to adequate financial incentives, in order to ensure its resilience with a high degree of confidence and its suitability to safely provide these services."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition to regulatory changes, a dedicated EU legal framework that encompasses both private law and conflict of law rules is warranted to enable the development of European DLT-based markets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The ESCB is of the opinion that an enabling legal environment which provides legal certainty is missing at EU level and that this is one of several factors currently preventing wider use of tokenised securities and the development, at scale, of DLT-based trade and post-trade structures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,22,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Such a framework should include private law rules on how tokenised ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 21
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"securities can be issued, how they can be acquired and how they can be transferred."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The framework could potentially be introduced as a 28th regime.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Inspiration for this legal framework could be taken from national laws and the UNIDROIT Principles on Digital Assets and Private Law.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Such a comprehensive framework could in the longer run also contribute to the eligibility of DLT-based assets for Eurosystem credit operations.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Fragmentation of DLT applications and a lack of interoperability in a broader sense hinder the development of liquid and efficient DLT-based markets, with investors and issuers facing high costs for connecting to multiple platforms at once."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The Eurosystem will continue to act as a catalyst for harmonisation and standardisation and provide coordination to the extent possible in this domain.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This also limits the possible benefits of DLT.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Eurosystem exploratory work on settling DLT-based transactions in central bank money identified how interoperability could be established between a diverse range of market DLT platforms for the purpose of safe and atomic settlement, including private permissioned, public permissionless and shared platforms between different market stakeholders."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, the exploratory work showed how each market DLT platform operates on its choice of technology and under its own practices, often under idiosyncratic national legal frameworks, with a lack of industry-wide market practices and standards."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The DLT ecosystem would benefit from less divergence of technologies, market practices and operating models, and from the application of technical standards, which could improve scaling."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Harmonisation and standardisation require coordination across market players and market segments and ultimately the adoption of harmonised market practices and standards.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The Eurosystem will continue to promote coordination and act as a catalyst for harmonisation and standardisation in this domain with the vision of a future ecosystem of fully interoperable ledgers or a shared ledger.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,It is important to note that DLT’s scope of application goes beyond current financial market infrastructures and affects all parts of the value chain.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, adjusting legal, fiscal and collateral frameworks at a pan-European level for DLT will be important to scale and reap the expected benefits."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The Eurosystem will undertake detailed analysis of all these aspects in conjunction with the relevant public sector bodies and liaising with market stakeholders, both at European and international levels."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.5 Asset management and funds The ESCB welcomes the Commission’s focus on removing barriers to the cross-border operation and marketing of investment funds in the EU that affect costs and accessibility for EU citizens.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A single, simple and consistent implementation of the Single Rulebook is ultimately needed to avoid fragmentation and address gaps and differences in supervisory approaches."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Despite the harmonisation reached with Directive 2009/65/EC (UCITS Directive) and Directive 2011/61/EU (AIFM Directive), which allow investment funds to be marketed across the EU via a relatively simple notification procedure, divergences remain in national interpretations, supervisory practices and operational requirements, undermining the core goals of a single European capital market."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These inconsistencies generate duplication, undermine efficiency and fragment cross-border operations."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,23,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Harmonising ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 22
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,supervisory practices and recognising group-wide compliance and risk functions across Member States would strengthen the Single Market and reduce unnecessary burden.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Greater convergence, through ESMA-led guidance and binding technical standards, would enhance legal certainty and reduce market fragmentation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"More integrated market supervision, especially for large cross-border operators, is important (Section 2.6.5); in the meantime, cooperation among NCAs should be strengthened, for example by enhancing the role of supervisory colleges."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Removing regulatory barriers to cross-border investments within the EU is key to unlocking the full potential of EU capital markets for investors.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Retail and professional investors still encounter obstacles and fragmented access rules when investing across borders, including divergent national restrictions on retail investment in alternative investment funds (AIFs)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Removing the potential obstacles to retail investments in AIFs should however be accompanied by safeguards that protect the retail investor and assure the manager’s capacity to manage the associated risks.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Harmonising the tax treatment of investment products across the EU and simplifying access to cross-border funds is key to the goals of the CMU and the broadening of investor choice.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The recent agreement on safer and faster procedures to obtain double taxation relief (FASTER)17 is a stepping stone in this regard that will encourage cross-border investment.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, some impediments remain, for example the requirement for financial intermediaries to register with the competent authorities of each Member State from which income originates to be allowed to request tax relief on behalf of a registered owner."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This registration process is still subject to different application in Member States.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A key issue is the tax recognition of the various categories of investors (institutional investors, such as mutual or pension funds, or other forms of vehicle) operating within the Single Market, in order to grant them withholding tax relief and refunds."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Member States may also impose more extensive reporting obligations on transactions in order to detect potential tax abuse or fraud.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,More detailed and uniform EU rules may therefore be necessary to clearly define the scope of these obligations and the associated liabilities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Further harmonisation of corporate law at EU level on matters affecting the whole investment chain (including insolvency and taxation) should also continue to be assessed – including the potential use of a 28th regime.18 Harmonising corporate tax regimes (at least the tax base) helps reduce differences between Member States on the net return on investments, which affects the competitiveness of capital markets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, corporate taxation reforms could positively influence the development of the CMU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, the debt-equity bias reduction allowance proposal, introducing a tax allowance on increases in company equity, incentivises the use of stock markets, rewarding the capitalisation of companies, and increases, other things being equal, the return on investment in the equity of companies by reducing the cost of capital."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Creating a voluntary opt-in 17 Regulation (EU) 2019/1156 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 20 June 2019 on facilitating cross-border distribution of collective investment undertakings.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"18 See Oehmke, M. and B. Becker (2025), “Restructuring and insolvency – reform opportunities in Europe”, ASC Insights, No 4, European Systemic Risk Board, May."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,24,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 23
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,EU-wide system alternative to national ones (a “28th regime”) for innovative firms which would cover aspects of insolvency and corporate law – as proposed by the European Commission – can potentially overcome certain political difficulties in achieving harmonisation.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In light of past experience, however, its success would crucially depend on defining a comprehensive legal framework, capable of meeting the needs and interests of entrepreneurs and investors in innovative companies."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Referrals to national laws should be minimised to reduce the risk of divergences that may result from varying courts’ interpretations.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The narrow focus on innovative companies with relatively homogeneous characteristics and investor needs could facilitate the creation of an appropriate legal framework.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As harmonisation will remain a complex endeavour, continued efforts to improve the efficiency of national regimes (e.g. in insolvency law) remain of utmost importance."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.5.1 Improving authorisation procedures Harmonised and streamlined authorisation requirements and procedures are critical to achieving a true single market for asset management.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Today, management companies face inconsistent application processes, which, in addition to local requirements and procedural delays across Member States, create undue barriers to the effective operation of cross-border asset management within the Single Market.19 The ESCB supports initiatives to further harmonise and standardise authorisation requirements and procedures for management companies to increase simplification and reduce fragmentation in the EU’s asset management sector."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The establishment of a single EU-wide authorisation system for management companies, based on standardised forms and uniform timelines, would merit future consideration."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Uniform approval standards and cross-border access for fund service providers in the EU are essential to supporting market integration.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The current national variations in approving depositaries and service providers delay fund launches and complicate cross-border operations across the EU.20 Harmonising the depository regime through EU-wide passporting and a central register of approved entities would improve cross-border operations, ensuring seamless service provision across Member States and enhancing market efficiency."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Recognising group-level operations and eliminating duplicative supervision are crucial to facilitating efficient cross-border asset management within the EU.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Centralised risk management, compliance and marketing frameworks within cross-border groups could be better recognised and would be helped by having a more harmonised supervisory framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Allowing group-level authorisations and 19 See the European Court of Auditors’ special report on a single market for investment funds (2022), which notes that variations in national requirements and interpretations can create obstacles to the seamless operation of the single market for investment funds, or the European Fund and Asset Management Association (EFAMA) response to ESMA’s consultation on the Eligible Assets Directive (2024)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"20 Currently, the depositary regime is only partially harmonised at EU level: while the common rules regulate the duties and tasks of depositaries of investment funds and pension funds, the licensing and supervisory regime applicable to them is regulated at national level, thus creating divergences and providing leeway for possible regulatory arbitrage."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, the UCITS Directive explicitly forbids using non-domestic depositaries, while the latest amendments to the AIFM Directive tie it to several conditions and the discretion of NCAs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,25,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 24
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"supervisory waivers, particularly for operational functions, would enhance integration and reduce administrative barriers."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This would also lower operational costs for asset managers, which could benefit investors through lower fees and access to a broader range of investment products."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.5.2 Enhancing the functioning of the EU passport for the marketing of investment funds, management companies and depositaries Eliminating national barriers and facilitating cross-border fund distribution within the EU is necessary to ensuring the full effectiveness of the EU marketing passport."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While the legal passport exists, practical obstacles – including local approvals, fees and divergent marketing rules – continue to fragment the Internal Market."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, ESMA has highlighted that pre-approval of marketing materials is required in some cases, while in others there is a reliance on ex post checks.21 These differences can lead to diverging practices in the application of marketing rules across the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESMA’s role in centralising and harmonising marketing-related information across the EU could be strengthened in this regard.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Strengthening the effectiveness of the management company passport and introducing a depositary passport for all types of funds is necessary to supporting cross-border activities across the EU and creating a level playing field.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Substantive and procedural barriers imposed by some Member States undermine the functioning of the UCITS and AIFM Directives.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Furthermore, passporting of depositary services for UCITS is currently not allowed, while for AIFs it is subject to conditions at the discretion of NCAs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Centralising passport notifications and creating a standardised, EU-wide notification procedure under ESMA would eliminate the benefits of home state optimisation and allow management companies and depositaries to operate freely across borders without unnecessary restrictions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.5.3 Enhancing the attractiveness of EU markets as an investment destination Asset managers play a key role in the financing of the European economy and act as a transmission mechanism for effective capital allocation.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Vibrant, pan-EU capital markets are vital to securing investment funding and bolstering the EU’s productivity and competitiveness."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"By investing their clients’ savings in the markets, asset managers provide a source of stable, long-term funding to European governments, companies and infrastructure projects while providing investment opportunities for retail and institutional investors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A European savings and investment product could be used to attract more savers to capital markets and should go hand in hand with the removal of barriers within the European fund market to promote retail investor participation with low-cost products.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,It is crucial that the risk/return profile of these products is transparently communicated to retail investors.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,21 See ESMA Report on Marketing Requirements and Communications (2023).
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,26,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 25
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Allowing targeted flexibility in portfolio rules needs to be accompanied by appropriate safeguards to maintain financial stability, protect investors and support market integration."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While the UCITS framework has successfully promoted investor protection and financial stability through diversification, liquidity and risk management rules, some targeted flexibility could improve fund attractiveness without undermining these core objectives."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As an example, the rules allowing UCITS funds to benefit from increased investment limits in a single issuer could be made more flexible."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, such flexibility must be carefully calibrated and accompanied by financial stability safeguards both at microprudential and macroprudential levels."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, robust liquidity risk management, concentration controls, stress testing and enhanced supervisory oversight adjustments to portfolio requirements should be harmonised across Member States, ensuring a consistent level playing field and avoiding regulatory fragmentation that could exacerbate financial vulnerabilities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Deepening market integration through integrated supervision, harmonisation and barrier removal is also essential for a globally competitive EU asset management sector."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Shifting supervisory powers to the European level22, harmonising operational rules and simplifying cross-border fund operations would go a long way towards deepening the single market for capital."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It could also facilitate consolidation in the sector and the scaling of existing funds, which could benefit consumers through lower costs.23 Establishing a centralised EU data hub would improve financial stability oversight and reduce duplication."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These reforms would deliver a stronger, more integrated Single Market and enhance the EU’s position in global financial markets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Harmonising access conditions for high-net-worth individuals that pass relevant suitability requirements would broaden the investor base and support deeper capital market integration.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Current MiFID II categorisation rules, in combination with national divergences, limit the ability of affluent investors to access specialised fund segments such as EuVECAs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Simplifying and harmonising access conditions paired with relevant suitability requirements across Member States, particularly for investors meeting wealth and investment thresholds, would expand funding sources for venture capital and SME growth, directly contributing to the objectives of the CMU and SIU, without undermining investor protection for less specialised investors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"22 For example, supervisory colleges with strong, legally defined powers could be introduced for asset managers that operate across borders while shifting supervisory authority to ESMA for the largest cross-border asset managers."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"23 ESMA analysis shows that EU UCITS remain, on average, much smaller than US funds, which can partially explain the substantial differences in the fund cost levels observed between the EU and the United States since larger funds tend to have lower ongoing costs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,See ESMA’s report on the costs and performance of EU retail investment products in 2024.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,27,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 26
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.6 Topics for consultation on supervision 2.6.1 Effectiveness of the current framework The ESAs, i.e. ESMA, the European Banking Authority (EBA) and the European Insurance and Occupational Pensions Authority (EIOPA), play a crucial role in developing the regulatory framework and overseeing financial markets within the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESMA in particular focuses on enhancing investor protection and promoting stable and orderly financial markets.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESAs have been instrumental in implementing a single rulebook and in promoting consistent regulation across Member States.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"By standardising rules through directives and regulations like MiFID II, EMIR and the Prospectus Regulation, the Single Rulebook helps to reduce regulatory arbitrage and create a level playing field."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESAs also promote supervisory convergence and actively work towards the consistent application of EU law by issuing guidelines and opinions.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The current allocation of supervisory powers to the different participants and segments of capital markets across the EU is heterogeneous and complex.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"First, the supervisory framework is largely decentralised, which affords national authorities considerable discretion in enforcing legislation and implementing supervisory practices."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This can lead to inconsistencies and hinder true European market integration.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The governance system, which relies on decisions by the NCAs from 27 Member States, may give too much weight to national interests, sometimes resulting in suboptimal outcomes."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This concern was echoed in the Commission consultation on supervisory convergence and the Single Rulebook, where most industry respondents, as well as consumer representatives and academics, identified the need to enhance the independence of the ESAs’ deciding bodies, a sentiment corroborated by the ESAs themselves.24 Second, such heterogeneity at national level is exacerbated by the interaction with the regulatory and supervisory powers at European level.25 Under the current allocation of regulatory and supervisory powers, convergence and harmonisation are difficult to achieve due to different national rules and practices, as well as different national implementations of European rules."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Despite the progress in harmonising rules and strengthening the ESAs, more integration of the EU's supervisory framework can foster capital market integration."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Significant progress has been made in harmonising rules and establishing binding technical standards across the EU, creating a more consistent supervisory landscape."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, the incremental amendments to the supervisory framework have not established a completely level playing field or achieved comprehensive market integration."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While some centralisation of supervisory tasks for 24 This was highlighted in the Commission’s 2022 report on the operation of European Supervisory Authorities, as well as in the summary report of the 2021 targeted consultation on supervisory convergence and the Single Rulebook."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"25 For banks, the ECB is responsible for supervision, but the EBA is in charge of regulatory harmonisation via the Single Rulebook."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For capital markets, ESMA is responsible for the Single Rulebook but is also in charge of direct supervisions for certain players (e.g. rating agencies and trade repositories)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For insurance and pension funds, supervision is carried out at national level, but EIOPA is in charge of regulatory harmonisation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,28,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 27
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"cross-border players has occurred, the existing framework does not fully support supervisory convergence across the Internal Market, potentially hindering the integration of capital markets.26 Even when enforcing EU legislation, national supervisors can maintain different supervisory practices tailored to their national markets or policy preferences."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,They may also seek to protect domestic players from cross-border consolidation or competition.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Only a more integrated supervisory framework can produce truly harmonised supervisory practices that underpin a genuine single European market.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A more integrated supervisory system would offer significant benefits, including a more transparent, predictable and accessible environment for market participants."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This system would support the uniform implementation of rules, increase market confidence and promote cross-border investments across the EU, which are crucial for developing a single market for capital in Europe."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Moreover, integrated supervision would facilitate a more coordinated response to market disruptions, further contributing to financial stability."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Ultimately, integrated supervision with a single rulebook will open up markets, increase competition and benefit investors and borrowers in all jurisdictions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"An integrated framework would also facilitate the supervision of entities operating across borders in the EU and reduce compliance costs, thus supporting market integration."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The combination of the national and European level in terms of responsible authorities may create unnecessary complexity for regulated entities and supervisors alike.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"An entity active in multiple segments might need to deal with a large number of supervisors, taking into account multiple home-country authorities, multiple supervisors in other Member States, plus European regulators and supervisors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Such complexity has two important consequences.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"First, it increases the costs for market participants to comply with different rules and their interpretation when operating across borders and creates an unnecessary need to duplicate infrastructure and processes."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Second, it has the potential to create disincentives for market players to increase their scale and to expand across borders in Europe."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.6.2 Specific questions on supervisory arrangements for different sectors A stepwise approach accounting for specific sectoral features may help incrementally integrate the supervisory architecture of capital markets.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Such an approach would recognise and address the specific characteristics of different sectors within the capital market, introducing more integrated European supervision for those market players with a stronger European dimension and systemic relevance."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Different market segments operate under distinct regulatory, operational and risk management frameworks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, a gradual and tailored approach that considers the specific dynamics and regulatory requirements of each market segment could facilitate a smoother transition towards integrated supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This approach would also allow for the identification and mitigation of sector-specific risks and challenges, ensuring that the integrated supervisory framework is robust, comprehensive and adaptable."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,26 See the Commission’s 2022 report on the operation of European Supervisory Authorities and the summary report of the 2021 targeted consultation on supervisory convergence and the Single Rulebook.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,29,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 28
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Moreover, engaging with stakeholders from various sectors during this process can foster collaboration and build consensus on the integrated supervisory system."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In the short term, targeted improvements to the supervisory framework could be considered by bringing certain segments of capital market actors under EU supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These could be, for example, market segments that are strategically important and that could benefit from common supervision to achieve the required scale, or market segments where integration or intense cross-border activity across the EU could entail higher cross-border contagion risks to financial stability."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Examples of such strategic sectors include but are not limited to trading venues and CCPs of systemic EU importance, CSDs, relevant asset management companies and funds, and CASPs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Different models could be used to introduce these targeted improvements and promote supervisory integration in the short term, taking into account specific sectoral features."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Different approaches could be considered in order to achieve the objective of integrated supervision of EU capital markets – in line with the 2024 Governing Council statement.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Different models with varying levels of ambition also reflect different views within the Eurosystem and the European System of Central Banks.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In particular, two options could be explored."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A first option could be a “two-tier” model (akin to the Single Supervisory Mechanism (SSM)) where only the most significant actors in a given market would fall under direct EU-level supervision, in cooperation with national supervisors, while the primary responsibility for smaller and less significant players would remain with national supervisors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The scope of significant entities under direct EU-level supervision could be determined based on a range of different factors, such as their size, EU relevance, cross-border activities across the EU, etc."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"These criteria could, at least initially, be designed in such a way that only the most systemic, cross-border EU players would be subject to direct EU-level supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Under this model, the EU-level supervisor would retain ultimate responsibility for the overall good functioning of the supervisory framework and could also directly intervene at the level of smaller players where required."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"National authorities would retain a strong role, by keeping supervisory powers on the less significant entities and by cooperating with the EU supervisor for the oversight of the most significant players."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A second possible option could be a progressive and gradual shift of selected supervisory powers to a European authority.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Under this option, national supervisors would over time transfer more powers over certain capital market sectors to the EU-level supervisor, while still retaining some supervisory powers at national level."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This option could benefit from an ex-ante predefined timeline to ensure that the gradual shift of some supervisory powers to the European level will actually take place.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This would help create some certainty about the next steps while still moving towards integration of EU capital markets supervision at EU level.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The options presented above, which need to be further explored, would offer the clear benefit of moving towards the goal of integrated European supervision of capital markets, although with different levels of ambition."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,30,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The creation of regional hubs with the participation of national supervisors or EU centres of supervisory expertise by sector, which are options included in the consultation, could ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 29"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"also be interesting avenues to explore: they would likely be less helpful to move towards integrated supervision in the short run, but could offer useful support when implementing any of the two proposed options."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, the design and tasks of such hubs and centres would be key to understanding and assessing whether and to what extent they could provide substantial net benefits in view of the goal of integrated supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Last but not least, it should be stressed that the options proposed above require further in-depth analysis in terms of design and implementation, as well as potential benefits and possible drawbacks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The Czech National Bank, the Central Bank of Ireland, the National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg, the latter highlighting that it is not entrusted with direct supervisory responsibilities in this field, judged that integrated supervision could ultimately be achieved in different ways."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In particular, while the Czech National Bank, the Central Bank of Ireland, the National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg concur that it is critical to deliver a more integrated and efficient approach to supervision within the Single Market, they view that this could be better achieved through greater supervisory convergence, rather than direct EU supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"That can be brought about via increased use of supervisory colleges, peer reviews, common supervisory actions (CSAs), streamlined Q&A tools of the ESAs and real-time information and knowledge sharing."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The Central Bank of Ireland, the National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg’s view is that the costs and benefits of proposals around direct EU supervision need to be considered carefully, including from the perspective of efficiency."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Ultimately, given the breadth of entities in the capital markets ecosystem, the Central Bank of Ireland, the National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg consider that a “case-by-case” approach is preferable when determining the optimal supervisory framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.6.3 Questions on the supervision of EU CSDs The ESCB sees several benefits to more integrated supervision of EU CSDs in terms of efficiency and convergence of supervisory practices as well as further integration in the post-trade landscape.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"First, a number of CSDs – especially international CSDs – provide systemically important services to securities issuers and market participants in multiple Member States, and in some cases to non-EU countries."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The cross-border systemic importance of the largest CSDs is due to the critical functions they provide for multiple national securities markets, the high volumes of transactions they settle for market participants in different countries and their interconnectedness with other CSDs and market infrastructures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For these CSDs, an EU-level supervisor would align their supervision with their systemic importance, the interests of the various markets they serve and the cross-border risks their activities pose."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Second, more integrated supervision of EU CSDs would allow for more efficient and convergent supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The current supervisory architecture poses challenges: NCAs typically supervise only one CSD – or in rare cases, two."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,31,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Especially when the CSD is a small entity serving only the local market, the competent authority may accordingly, in some cases, have limited resources itself to conduct a thorough supervision of this single CSD, which can limit its ability to induce necessary ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 30"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,improvements in its risk management.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, supervising only one or two entities does not allow for comparability across CSDs and the benchmarking of their risk management practices."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The establishment of colleges under CSDR Refit could bring some benefits in this regard, which should be assessed."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Still, without ESMA’s involvement in the authorisation or the review and evaluation of CSDs27, its ability to ensure supervisory convergence is constrained."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Only the Eurosystem, as a relevant authority, has an overview of the risk management practices across the significant number of CSDs on which it is consulted and endeavours to promote high standards of compliance in the areas within the scope of its assessment – however, this relies on the cooperation of NCAs, which is not consistently provided."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, more integrated supervision of EU CSDs would allow for more efficient supervision by concentrating and further developing expertise in an EU-level authority."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It would also allow for greater comparability across CSDs, thus leading to further supervisory consistency."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"More integrated supervision of EU CSDs should consider the implications for the supervision of CSDs which provide banking-type ancillary services and are authorised as credit institutions accordingly, to ensure appropriate coordination and integration between the supervision of CSDs under the CSDR and under banking regulations."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Second, integrated supervision could in turn support integration in the post-trade landscape, especially for cross-border groups."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"National supervisory practices can limit the ability of CSD groups to further integrate their infrastructure, for instance by consolidating entities in a single Member State or by designating a single SSS for multiple Member States."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, national supervisors can interpret regulatory provisions on outsourcing strictly in order to place limits on intragroup outsourcing, which would require a change of supervisory approach, as it would be difficult to address in the regulatory text without undermining the purpose of these provisions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Targeted changes to CSDR provisions – which are currently focused on CSDs as individual entities – could allow for further intragroup integration while ensuring an effective cross-border assessment of risks.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, the Commission should assess whether an EU-level supervisory architecture for cross-border CSD groups28 could facilitate the integration of their activities and technical infrastructure, with efficiency gains and benefits for EU securities markets overall."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It could also allow these groups to have a single point of contact for their supervision, increasing the efficiency of the supervisory process, for instance by avoiding duplicative or overlapping supervision of group policies and procedures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The EU supervisory architecture for CSDs could evolve towards further integration along different pathways.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A first path would be to set up a two-tier supervisory model, with centralised supervision limited to (i) CSDs of substantial importance for the securities markets of multiple Member States, or the whole EU; 27 Under the CSDR, ESMA does not provide an opinion during the review and evaluation process."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Following CSDR Refit, ESMA would be part of the supervisory college for the CSDs where one is established, and the college could collectively adopt ex post non-binding opinions on issues identified in the review and evaluation process."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, this form of involvement is rather weak and limited to only a few CSDs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,28 This could be achieved by ensuring that an EU-level supervisor supervises all the EU CSDs which are part of a same group within the same supervisory unit.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"If a group also includes non-EU CSDs, this could be accommodated through supervisory cooperation between the EU-level supervisory authority and the third-country supervisory authority."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,32,20,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 31
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,and/or (ii) CSDs which are part of a cross-border group of CSDs.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The criteria for substantial importance could be drawn from the draft regulatory standards already prepared by ESMA29.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,These criteria could be complemented with others to reflect (i) the importance of a CSD for the whole EU securities markets and (ii) the fact that CSDs belong to cross-border groups.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At the same time, national CSDs which are less interconnected and represent lower risk profiles would remain under national supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The organisation of centralised supervision could take the form of JSTs comprised of ESMA as lead supervisor and NCAs, and involving relevant authorities (e.g. the national central bank responsible for the oversight of the SSS operated by the CSD, and the relevant central banks of issue, CBIs)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The involvement of national authorities would allow the EU-level supervisor to draw on their knowledge of national securities law and regulations.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For CSDs under centralised supervision, these JSTs could then replace supervisory colleges in the interest of simplification, if the number of authorities involved is manageable, and thus colleges could remain only for the most interconnected CSDs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A more gradual evolution towards more harmonised supervision could take the form of intermediate steps, building on the improvements brought by CSDR Refit."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Notably, it has not yet been possible to assess the benefits of establishing colleges under CSDR Refit."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition to ESMA’s participation in CSD colleges as foreseen by CSDR Refit, ESMA could participate in the authorisation and the review and evaluation processes for CSDs, with the possibility to make recommendations, whether binding or on a comply-or-explain basis."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Another option that could be further investigated would be, in addition to or in combination with the supervisory colleges for individual CSDs foreseen by CSDR Refit, the establishment of supervisory colleges for CSD groups, chaired by ESMA, with the aim of harmonising supervisory approaches for CSDs with group policies and procedures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Relevant authorities, including ESCB members both as CBIs and as overseers, should remain involved in the supervisory process for CSDs, irrespective of the pathway chosen to further integrate supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The smooth operation of CSDs and the SSSs they operate is critical to central banks’ mandates, including the implementation of monetary policy and the related use of SSSs for central bank credit operations, the stability of the financial system and the smooth functioning of the payment system."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Relevant authorities should therefore remain involved in the authorisation and review and evaluation processes, including for CSDs under centralised supervision, as well as for those CSDs for which colleges will be established, although the modalities may vary in the interest of efficiency."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Should the governance of CSD supervision evolve towards further centralisation, for instance with the creation of JSTs or a supervisory committee within ESMA, relevant authorities should also be closely involved in those structures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg, the latter highlighting that it is not the CSD competent authority, do not fully share the views set out in this section."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,They consider that the assessment of potential shortcomings in CSD supervision does not appear sufficiently conclusive so as to 29 See ESMA’s final report regarding the draft RTS on the substantial importance of CSDs under Article 24a(13) CSDR.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,33,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 32
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"justify, at this stage, a significant move towards centralised CSD supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"They note that the supervisory framework for CSDs was recently reviewed under CSDR Refit, which was adopted on 13 December 2023 and entered into force in 2024 only."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The improvements introduced in that review to enhance integrated supervision, especially through mandatory supervisory colleges with the participation of ESMA (and EBA where a CSD provides banking ancillary services), are ongoing and should first be fully implemented and assessed before new proposals are made to change this framework again."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Finally, they note that for CSDs authorised as credit institutions, their direct supervision by ESMA under CSDR would break the existing synergies that stem from their supervision by a single competent authority, both as CSDs and as banks, leading to inefficiencies and additional costs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Moreover, as long as securities and company laws lack harmonisation within the EU, national fragmentation will persist, which will not be solved by centralising supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.6.4 Questions on the supervision of EU CCPs The mechanisms introduced by EMIR have only partially succeeded in harmonising supervisory practices for EU CCPs.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"First, the mechanisms in place to balance the views of NCAs could be enhanced."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The ESMA CCP Supervisory Committee allows its members to contribute to ESMA decisions and to the convergence of supervisory practices.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, NCAs continue to have sole responsibility for the supervision of EU CCPs, with only limited areas of shared competence with ESMA30."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As for EMIR colleges, they, by design, only partially allow authorities interested in the safety of EU CCPs to influence NCAs’ supervisory decisions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Colleges provide a useful platform for information exchange, and college members – including ESCB members, both as CBIs and as CCP overseers, and ECB Banking Supervision – adopt opinions on key supervisory decisions and can issue non-binding recommendations."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, college members can actively engage in supervisory procedures to influence the decision of the NCA."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In practice, however, these opinions rarely influence the outcome of NCAs’ decisions: college members are often not in a position to challenge the stance of NCAs (e.g. due to the timing or depth of the college engagement and tight deadlines, which are further shortened by EMIR 3); the tools available to express dissent are limited (e.g. abstentions or negative votes); and ultimately, most college opinions are non-binding and NCAs retain the final say (except for opinions under Article 17, which are binding if adopted by a two-thirds majority)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While ESMA’s efforts have fostered convergence through various tools, supervisory practices remain divergent across NCAs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"NCAs hold a margin of interpretation of EMIR risk management standards and are able to adopt diverging approaches regarding CCP practices – for example, on outsourcing of critical functions, access criteria or margin model changes."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, the convergence tools currently used by ESMA are not always effective."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, ESMA guidelines, 30 The validation of significant risk model changes is shared with ESMA."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, EMIR 3 extended the competences NCAs share with ESMA to the qualification of a model change as significant under Article 49a and the chairing of the college."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,It also granted to ESMA a coordination role in case of an emergency situation affecting more than one CCP (Article 24 (3)).
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,34,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 33
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"while promoting a common understanding of EMIR requirements, are not always fully followed by NCAs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Similarly, peer reviews conducted by ESMA expose divergent practices but do not consistently lead to tangible changes in NCAs’ supervisory approaches."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, the current supervisory framework could benefit from enhancements from a risk and competitiveness perspective."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"First, less conservative risk management practices could emerge due to the absence of a harmonised supervisory approach to CCPs’ risk management – albeit within the limits of EMIR (i.e. a “race to the bottom”)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Further, even if some NCAs require from CCPs higher conservativeness above the regulatory minimum standards, the varying supervisory approaches among NCAs could create an unlevel playing field between EU CCPs, potentially distorting competition within the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Second, the current supervisory framework can lead to regulatory inefficiencies."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In particular, diverging supervisory practices may incentivise EU legislators and ESMA to increase the scope and granularity of regulatory requirements."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This, in turn, may increase the burden for both authorities and EU CCPs, while not always efficiently ensuring flexible and future-proof regulatory approaches."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"One justification for national-level supervision is typically the national fiscal responsibility associated with a failure of supervision, but this is in part misleading."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Given that public funds may only be used as a last resort, the allocation of CCP losses would likely affect clearing members, including from other EU Member States, rather than domestic public funds."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"If a CCP of systemic importance for multiple Member States were to accrue losses that cannot be absorbed by the default waterfall or recovery arrangements, it is therefore likely that those losses would be allocated across several Member States in any event, rather than to the sole Member State where the CCP is established."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Such an outcome may become more likely in the future as resolution planning for CCPs matures to a point where it is feasible in practice, not just in theory."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, the Regulation on a framework for the recovery and resolution of central counterparties views extraordinary public financial support as a last resort."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Therefore, it may be beneficial to complement centralised supervision with solutions that do not rely on national fiscal responsibility, thereby addressing the issue of potentially significant losses that cannot be attributed to clearing members."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The ESCB would thus be in favour of centralising the supervision of EU CCPs, at least for those with the most significant European cross-border relevance (i.e. a two-tier model)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"First, centralisation could help better address cross-border risks, ensuring that the impact of EU CCPs on EU markets, market participants and currencies is adequately addressed by supervisors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Second, centralised supervision would be an opportunity to simplify the current supervisory framework and reduce duplicative supervisory work of ESMA and the NCAs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Finally, it would lead to greater convergence of supervisory practices and ensure a level playing field between CCPs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This benefit would be fully effective if, in the long run, centralisation is achieved for all CCPs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,An EU supervisory framework with a stronger EU-level angle would overall better reflect the cross-border dimension of EU clearing.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,35,20,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Similar to the existing approach for non-EU CCP supervision, a two-tier model could be introduced, where the most systemic EU CCPs would be supervised by ESMA, in cooperation with the ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 34"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The criteria for determining the systemic importance of CCPs could include the volume and value of central clearing activity, the scope of products, the geographical scope of EU-based clearing participants and the EU-based trading venues connected."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Another feature of this two-tier approach is that less systemic EU CCPs would be transitioned to this centralised set-up through a phased-in process based on risk, following a predetermined schedule."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESMA appears to be the obvious institution where the powers for the supervision of EU CCPs could be centralised.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESMA has built up significant experience and expertise over the past decade and is well positioned to ensure that cross-border risks are adequately monitored and managed.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Different solutions could be considered to ensure an appropriate level of involvement for NCAs.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,One potential approach to fostering cooperation in ongoing supervisory activities is the establishment of JSTs.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"They should not become a supplementary layer of supervision, but rather a way to accommodate some centralisation of supervision and simplification of the current framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The supervisory processes for EU CCPs would have to be adapted to better incorporate the views of other authorities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Considering the potential need for CCPs to use central bank deposit and credit facilities, ESCB members, both as CBIs and as CCP overseers, need to be closely involved in the (ongoing) supervisory processes of relevant EU CCPs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As explained above, it is often challenging for the Eurosystem as the central bank of issue (CBI) and the relevant members of the ESCB responsible for the oversight of CCPs to meaningfully engage in these supervisory processes through EMIR colleges."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Furthermore, while the ESMA CCP Supervisory Committee facilitates good dialogue among NCAs supervising CCPs, ESMA’s Board of Supervisors (BoS) is ultimately the decision-making body for key ESMA decisions on CCP supervisory matters."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,It would therefore be useful for those ESCB members acting as CBIs or CCP overseers to be represented as observers when the BoS discusses CCP matters.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"If ESMA were to take on central supervisory responsibilities with respect to EU CCPs, existing supervisory processes (e.g. consultations with EMIR colleges and ESMA committees) would need to be streamlined and options to ensure appropriate involvement of CBIs would need to be explored, preferably via JSTs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For CCPs that remain under national supervision and for which the existing college and ESMA committee procedures are maintained, these procedures should be revised to ensure early, close and meaningful engagement of CBIs in supervisory matters falling within their remit (as defined by Article 24b EMIR)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Furthermore, relevant prudential supervisors, including ECB Banking Supervision, should be given a permanent observer role in the CCP Supervisory Committee."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Given the interlinkages between institutions acting as clearing members and CCPs, discussions in the CCP Supervisory Committee are relevant for the risks these institutions may incur due to their exposures to CCPs, as reflected in the fact that ECB Banking Supervision is a member of the EMIR CCP colleges, as well as of the Joint Monitoring Mechanism established under EMIR 3."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Thus, the prudential supervisors of the institutions with the three largest contributions to the default fund of EU CCPs should participate in the ESMA CCP Supervisory Committee meetings as non-voting members."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,36,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this way, supervisors will be informed in a timely manner about possible risks affecting individual institutions and will be able to address them, if and as needed, as part of ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 35"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Such membership should be made permanent, rather than subject to an ad hoc invitation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.6.5 Questions on the supervision of funds and asset managers31 The ESCB sees benefits to more integrated supervision to monitor and address Europe-wide risks or where markets provide pan-European services.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A more integrated supervisory ecosystem would enhance financial stability and integration, increasingly important issues which need to be addressed given the growing size of these markets and their inherently cross-border nature.32 Non-banks’ total assets have more than doubled since 2008, growing from €23 trillion to €58 trillion by 2024 and are now comparable to 80% of banking sector assets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In the euro area, the role of NBFI entities in financing the real economy has become more important over the past decade, despite a decline in their share of total credit granted since 2022."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"NBFI entities accounted for 29% of outstanding credit to non-financial corporations in 2024, up from 15% in 2009."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Reflecting the continued expansion of the fund sector, domestic and cross-border holdings of euro area investment funds have almost quadrupled since 2008."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Investment funds’ total assets have grown from €5.4 trillion in 2008 to €19.7 trillion at the end of 2024.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Cross-border holdings in particular have expanded more rapidly, resulting in a predominant allocation by the majority of euro area funds towards foreign assets."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In total, the share of domestic assets in funds’ portfolios has fallen from 37% in 2008 to 27% in 2024, while investments in assets of other EU countries have also declined from 38% to 27%."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"By contrast, investments in non-EU assets have increased substantially from 25% of all holdings in 2008 to 46% in 2024."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"National supervisors may not fully account for spillover effects, and there is a risk of regulatory arbitrage or supervisory shopping, which could undermine financial stability across the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,An EU-level supervisory framework can increase financial integration by enhancing the level playing field.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This aligns with efforts to address barriers in the cross-border distribution of funds across the EU, where diverging supervisory practices and inconsistent rule implementation have been identified as obstacles."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This would also support the objective of promoting retail investor participation and facilitate the distribution and access of European savings and investment products.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Moreover, integrated supervision – including supervision by ESMA at EU level – could lead to greater efficiency."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"By consolidating resources, the EU can achieve cost-effectiveness and streamline processes."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, establishing an EU repository or procedure for the registration of funds could simplify operations for market actors, making the financial system more accessible and coherent."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Improving the EU supervisory framework for asset management and funds towards integrated supervision can take different forms.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Ultimately, elevating 31 The National Bank of Belgium and the Banque centrale du Luxembourg highlight that topics covered in this section are outside their respective and direct areas of responsibility."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"32 See Section 4.1.3 of ECB (2024), Financial Integration and Structure in the Euro Area, June."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,37,20,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 36
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,supervision at EU level within a single supervisory authority would be in line with the goals of having a single market for capital with an institutional framework most likely to protect financial stability and promote a level playing field.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Similar to the model chosen for banking supervision, this could mean a two-tier model, entrusting ESMA with the supervision of asset managers and funds with significant European cross-border activities, while NCAs would supervise asset managers and funds with limited or no cross-border activity."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"It is important to develop a model that is legally sound but also avoid an institutional set-up that would result in a more complex framework, with duplicative responsibilities at national and European level adding an extra layer and costs compared with the current set-up."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this context, establishing JSTs under ESMA’s leadership would be a powerful tool to achieve harmonised, efficient supervision of asset managers and funds."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,JSTs composed of national experts and ESMA staff would preserve local knowledge while ensuring centralised decision-making.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This would enhance supervisory consistency, deepen cooperation and deliver efficiency gains across the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this sense, they should not become a supplementary layer of supervision, but rather a way to accommodate some integration of supervision and simplification of the current framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To improve consistency in the meantime, joint supervisory work could be conducted by ESMA and national authorities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This could take the form of mandatory supervisory colleges, chaired by ESMA."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Here too, a two-tier approach could be applied: colleges would take joint decisions for the supervision of asset managers and funds with significant European cross-border activities, while NCAs would supervise asset managers and funds with limited or no cross-border activity."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While these colleges are already possible within the existing framework on a voluntary basis, making them mandatory would enhance the predictability of the framework and enhance the level playing field."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Mandatory supervisory colleges’ decisions could cover both supervisory functions (including authorisation, targeted intervention powers and investigation powers) as well as coordination responsibilities (including peer reviews)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Colleges with purely consultative and non-binding roles appear less effective.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Clear and objective criteria, including size and European cross-border activity, should determine which actors fall under EU-level supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To ensure a coherent and proportionate supervisory framework, asset managers and funds subject to EU supervision should be selected based on factors including the amount of assets under management, cross-border activities, cross-border investor base and the systemic importance of their combined activities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The exact designation criteria would need to be carefully calibrated in a separate, evidence-based quantitative exercise."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This approach would target supervisory resources efficiently at entities whose activities are most relevant for single market integration and financial stability, without imposing unnecessary burdens on smaller, purely domestic actors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At the same time, the European supervisory authority would have the power to assume jurisdiction over entities that, even if they do not meet the relevant criteria, could create risks for the system."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Delegation of tasks could be an integral part of the EU supervisory model to strengthen risk management and operational resilience.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,38,19,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Delegation of operational tasks to national authorities under the coordination and responsibility of ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 37
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"ESMA could support operational efficiency and preserve local proximity, where beneficial, without fragmenting supervisory authority."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While maintaining some local proximity and access to national expertise is important, we should be mindful that establishing regional hubs or fragmented centres of expertise does not add an additional layer of complexity and create coordination challenges along with a risk of perpetuating fragmentation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Proximity could be maintained through supervisory colleges or JSTs under European leadership, ensuring consistent supervisory methodologies and unified decision-making."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Embedding a macroprudential perspective into fund and asset manager supervision is critical to safeguarding financial stability.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Efforts towards an EU supervision approach of these actors should dovetail with swift progress on initiatives aimed at building a macroprudential framework for NBFI to guard against the build-up of systemic risk.33 Investment funds are increasingly interconnected with other parts of the financial system, and their liquidity mismatches, leverage and procyclical behaviours can amplify market-wide shocks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Previous stress episodes highlighted vulnerabilities in the NBFI sector, which contributed to and amplified market disruptions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In some cases, extraordinary central bank interventions were required to restore market functioning and safeguard financial stability."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A macroprudential approach to supervision should be developed alongside microprudential oversight, enabling sector-wide monitoring, forward-looking risk assessments and, where necessary, the activation of preventive measures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"More integrated supervision would allow for a more consistent application of macroprudential tools across the Single Market, reducing the risk of regulatory arbitrage."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"A stronger link between microprudential supervision and the EU macroprudential framework is necessary to close existing gaps and ensure financial stability.34 To obtain the benefits of the CMU, capital markets should be a resilient and sustainable source of financing, especially in times of stress."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Avoiding regulatory arbitrage and sectoral blind spots requires supervisory frameworks that are capable of detecting and addressing emerging systemic risks across the entire financial system.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This calls for expanded supervision of capital markets at EU level, with a much stronger mandate to address macroprudential concerns and more integrated supervision and policies that ensure stability in the NBFI sector."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"More integrated supervision of funds and managers should be closely connected to the broader EU macroprudential oversight architecture, including interaction with the European Systemic Risk Board."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Strengthening ESMA’s role with macroprudential functions should be accompanied by adequate governance structures and sufficient resources to ensure that macroprudential perspectives are fully embedded within ESMA’s risk identification, policy and supervisory approaches."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,These should include dedicated internal structures and sufficient resources to assess and respond to system-wide risks.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At the same time, it is essential that all national competent and macroprudential authorities are adequately represented in relevant decision-making processes at ESMA to ensure diverse supervisory perspectives and maintain 33 See Eurosystem response to EU Commission’s consultation on macroprudential policies for non-bank financial intermediation (NBFI), published in November 2024."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,39,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 38
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,This would increase the coherence between fund supervision and the broader EU financial stability objectives.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.6.6 Questions on the supervision of EU CASPs The ESCB sees benefits in EU-level supervision of CASPs that could exhibit higher risks for example due to their large size, high amount of cross-border activity or their being part of a large, globally operating CASP."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The borderless and global nature of crypto-assets due to the technology used also enables CASPs to provide their services across borders.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,EU-level supervision would ensure a comprehensive overview of risks and coordination of supervisory actions while avoiding regulatory arbitrage and reducing compliance costs for CASPs operating in several Member States.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"National supervisors may not fully account for spillover effects, which could undermine financial stability across the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For small CASPs, on the other hand, national supervision may provide a better balance in terms of benefits and risks."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Developing supervisory capacity directly at EU level and pooling expertise may entail lower costs compared with building up expertise at each NCA: given the newness of this field, NCAS generally have limited experience of supervising CASPs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The current supervisory set-up does not take into account higher risks from significant CASPs operating across borders.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Under the Markets in Crypto-assets Regulation (MiCAR) NCAs are in charge of the authorisation and supervision of CASPs, regardless of their size."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,MiCAR defines significant CASPs as those having 15 million active users in the EU on average per year.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, reaching this threshold only implies that the competent authority must report supervisory developments for significant CASPs to ESMA (Article 85 (3) MiCAR))."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This is in stark contrast to the treatment of significant e-money token or asset-referenced token issuers, where the EBA has supervisory powers to take account of the higher risks these issuers pose to financial stability and establish supervisory colleges once issuers are classified as significant to facilitate coordination of supervisory activities."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESMA has intervention powers if a type of activity or practice related to crypto-assets poses a significant investor protection concern or a threat to the orderly functioning and integrity of crypto-asset markets or the stability of the EU’s financial system.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In such a case, ESMA can address these concerns at aggregated level by temporarily prohibiting or restricting this activity or practice (Article 103 MiCAR)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, ESMA does not have any powers in relation to individual CASPs."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In general, crypto-asset services are provided mainly on a cross-border basis, and an NCA’s authorisation enables CASPs to offer their services throughout the EU (Article 59 MiCAR)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Currently, most of the CASPs listed in ESMA’s MiCAR register (around 80%) intend to provide their authorised service in at least five other Member States, and half of all CASPs in all Member States.35 This would call for a harmonised approach across the EU, in particular for larger and more complex providers."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The complex structure of global CASPs and their oligopolistic position in the market may entail additional risks that could be more efficiently and effectively 35 According to ESMA’s Interim MiCAR Register (as at 22 April 2025), 22 providers have been officially authorised as CASPs in the EU."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,40,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 39
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The Financial Stability Board has highlighted the vulnerabilities of multifunction crypto-asset intermediaries (MCIs), which are individual firms, or groups of affiliated firms, that combine a broad range of crypto-asset services, products and functions typically centred around the operation of a trading platform.36 Many MCIs operate their platforms primarily through a single global entry point, but MCIs often have affiliated entities and subsidiaries in several countries, including offshore financial centres."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The combinations of certain functions MCIs provide could exacerbate their vulnerabilities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As generally these MCIs are not transparent regarding their corporate structure and governance, it would be beneficial to deal with authorisations and supervision of MCIs and their subsidiaries at EU level, including by adopting a consolidated approach."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, under MiCAR, competent authorities may refuse the authorisation of CASPs if close links with non-EU firms could prevent effective supervision (Article 63 (7)(8) MiCAR)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Given the structure of certain global CASPs, analysing these aspects may be more efficiently done at EU level by pooling resources to understand the structure of these large players and also to prevent regulatory arbitrage."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"An example of the oligopolistic structure is the spot and derivatives trading market for crypto-assets, where the top five crypto trading platforms account for 50% of spot trading and for 90% of derivatives trading.37 EU-level supervision of significant CASPs would improve alignment with oversight responsibilities related to pan-EU payment schemes and arrangements and with SSM supervision of CASPs as part of credit institutions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Some CASP activities are relevant to the ESCB’s task of ensuring the smooth operation of the payment system.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The Eurosystem oversight framework for electronic payment instruments, schemes and arrangements (PISA) applies to crypto-related payment schemes and to crypto-related payment arrangements."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,CASP services might qualify as a pan-EU payment scheme or arrangement and therefore fall under the scope of the PISA framework.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As this oversight is conducted at Eurosystem level, EU-level supervision of significant CASPs would facilitate cooperation between oversight and CASP supervisory authorities in line with Responsibility E under “cooperation with other authorities” in the principles for financial market infrastructures issued by the Committee on Payments and Market Infrastructures and the International Organization of Securities Commissions."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In addition, CASPs may be part of a credit institution group, or credit institutions could directly offer crypto-asset services.38 Thus, there could be an interplay with the banking supervision tasks conferred upon the ECB by the SSM Regulation."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,These interplays give rise to significant coordination costs as they require interaction between several competent authorities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,EU-level supervision of significant CASPs would add an additional layer of supervision.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A coordinated supervisory approach between the EU authority and the other competent authorities would thus be needed to mitigate any additional coordination costs.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Nonetheless, integrated supervision of significant CASPs may 36 See FSB (2023), “The Financial Stability Implications of Multifunction Crypto-asset Intermediaries”, November."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"37 Of the top five crypto-asset trading platforms, three appear in the top five for spot and for derivatives trading."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"See CoinDesk Data Exchange Review, March 2025."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,38 Crypto-asset services are included in the list of activities subject to mutual recognition as established in Annex I of Directive 2013/36/EU on access to the activity of credit institutions and the prudential supervision of credit institutions.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,41,18,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 40
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,also be beneficial as it would bring supervisory convergence and reduce costs arising from divergences in supervision across Member States.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,There is important merit in analysing potential criteria and thresholds to determine which CASPs could fall under EU-level supervision.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While MiCAR defines significant CASPs solely on the basis of an active user criterion, it needs to be assessed whether this criterion sufficiently captures the higher risks outlined above that would call for EU-level supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,The active users within the EU could be an indication of how many EU clients are affected.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"However, for certain crypto-asset services this needs to be complemented by other criteria that reflect how the vulnerabilities of these activities and potential implications increase with size."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In particular, for activities such as operating a trading platform for crypto-assets and providing custody and administration of crypto-assets on behalf of clients, the risk of these activities is driven by the value and types of transactions and amount of assets under management rather than the mere number of clients."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Other potential criteria to determine significant CASPs could take into account the additional risks from combining activities and be aligned with those for significant issuers.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"As providing certain crypto-asset services in combination with being part of a MCI may amplify certain risks, even if the MCI conducts only part of its operations in the EU, a suitable criterion for determining which CASPs to supervise at EU level could be whether they are part of a group that operates in non-EU countries."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The significance decision could also be based on the assessment of a combination of criteria, as, for example, is the case for significant e-money token and asset-referenced token issuers."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For the sake of consistency, it could be appropriate to classify significant issuers also as significant CASPs if they offer any of the services listed under Article 3.1(16) MiCAR, in particular if they offer custody and administration of crypto-assets on behalf of clients, operate a trading platform for crypto-assets, offer exchange of crypto-assets for funds or for other crypto-assets, or provide transfer services for crypto-assets on behalf of clients."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this connection, the interplay between the significance regimes for CASPs and e-money or asset-referenced token issuers for the same entity should be duly assessed to avoid any excessive burden for both entities and supervisors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For instance, a single college of supervisors could be established to cater for both the classification as significant CASP and significant issuer."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"2.7 Horizontal questions on the supervisory framework 2.7.1 New direct supervisory mandates and governance models A more integrated EU supervisory framework could bring significant net benefits, including reduced costs for market participants, elimination of infrastructure duplication and more effective supervision."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,42,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Lessons from the banking union, with direct supervision for the largest credit institutions and an emphasis on harmonising practices for the supervision of smaller institutions by national authorities, show the potential advantages of similar frameworks in capital ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 41"
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"markets.39 This approach could enhance market confidence, ensure regulatory predictability and facilitate cross-border activities across the EU through an integrated supervisory framework."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"This model supports market integration while maintaining proportionality and flexibility for smaller firms, balancing the advantages of integration with the practicalities of national oversight."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"EU-level supervision, characterised by a common authority with centralised powers alongside national supervision, is crucial to achieving the uniformity necessary for the development of pan-European markets and advancing capital market integration."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"While transitioning to an integrated system could involve initial costs, such as restructuring existing supervisory frameworks and investing in new systems or processes, the long-term savings and efficiencies should outweigh them thanks to economies of scale and scope and to the common approach to supervision and enforcement."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Irrespective of the model of integration that is ultimately chosen, the governance of the EU supervisor would have to reflect its increased responsibility, ensuring independence and the European nature of the decision-making process."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,5,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"To this end, the governance of the ESAs should be strengthened to facilitate a larger role for decision-making at European level and to ensure that they can act fully independently."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,6,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In this context, several options could be explored such as those outlined in the consultation document."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,7,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"In principle, governance models including an Executive Board composed of independent members present strong benefits, as already shown by experiences with other institutions (e.g. the ECB, the Single Resolution Board and more recently the Authority for Anti-Money Laundering and Countering the Financing of Terrorism)."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,8,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"At the same time, national authorities should still play a key role, as they could offer vast expertise and proximity to local markets and investors."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,9,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,A possible approach could be to establish a Supervisory Board comprising both Executive Board members and representatives of national authorities.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,10,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Voting rules should however be designed in such a way that national supervisors would not have in principle veto powers – this could help to facilitate the European nature of the decision-making process.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,11,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The decision-making process could be articulated taking into account the differences across sectors of the capital markets, while avoiding excessive complexity."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,12,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, in capital market segments with a predominantly domestic nature, national supervisors could be assigned more weight in the voting mechanisms, such as via qualified majority procedures."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,13,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For sectors with a stronger European dimension, the voting rules could instead not allow Member States to block decisions and the Executive Board could retain ultimate responsibility and powers."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,14,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"When designing this possible tailored voting mechanism, it would also be important to ensure that the process of reaching a final outcome is not excessively cumbersome or complex."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,15,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"39 Research has shown, for example, that the creation of the SSM has produced positive effects on bank governance, bank lending and bank profitability."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,16,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"For example, see Chiarella, C., Cuadros-Solas, P.J. and Rossi, L. (2025), “Supranational Supervision and Bank Governance”, March; Altavilla, C., Boucinha, M., Peydró, J.L., Jasova, M. and Smets, F. (2024), “Supranational Banking Supervision, Credit Supply and Risk-Taking: European Evidence from Multi-Country Credit Registers”; and Raunig, B. and Sigmund, M. (2024), “Restoring Confidence in Systemically Important Banks: SSM Effects on Bank Performance”."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,43,17,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 42
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,44,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,2.7.2 Funding Changes in responsibilities and governance should be complemented by changes in revenues to ensure supervisors have sufficient funding to conduct their duties.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,44,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Regardless of what (mix of) funding source(s) is chosen, it is crucial that ESAs’ funding safeguards a degree of independence and is sufficient to fulfil its mandate."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,44,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"Independence should be ensured vis-à-vis both the political sphere and the industry, which could also be achieved via an appropriate mix and diversification of the sources of funding."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,44,3,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"The amount of available financial resources will also be critical to ensuring that an EU capital market supervisor is able to perform its tasks: in this respect, the magnitude of the funding should probably be significantly higher than the current budget available to ESMA."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,44,4,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets – Specific remarks 43
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,45,0,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,"© European Central Bank, 2025 Postal address 60640 Frankfurt am Main, Germany Telephone +49 69 1344 0 Website www.ecb.europa.eu All rights reserved."
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,45,1,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,Reproduction for educational and non-commercial purposes is permitted provided that the source is acknowledged.
ecb.conresp202506.en.pdf,45,2,ESCB reply to the European Commission’s targeted consultation on integration of EU capital markets,European Central Bank,D:20250610171244+02'00',D:20250610171205+02'00',,For specific terminology please refer to the ECB glossary (available in English only).
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,1,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED ERPB engagement on digital euro fit in the payment ecosystem Forward Looking Conclusions 16 July 2025 Digital euro project
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,2,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Engagement on fit in the ecosystem with market stakeholders started in October 2024 All stakeholders were invited to participate and contribute to all sessions.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,2,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"8 Payment Service Providers 3 Core theme sessions on Competition, Synergies and Business Model; 3 Outcome Sessions1 ERPB Fit in the Ecosystem Technical Sessions Merchants 1 Core theme session2 on Competition, Synergies and Business Model 1 Consumers 1 Core theme session2 on Digital financial inclusion, Privacy fraud prevention and resilience, and Competition 2-day in-person workshop was organized to discuss the key takeaways from the workstream 1."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,2,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Competition and Synergies outcome sessions were held on the same day.;
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,2,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Merchants and Consumers sessions were held on the same day www.ecb.europa.eu © 2
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,3,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"ECB-UNRESTRICTED Process leading to today's discussion Discussion initiated and presented based on internal, qualitative analysis Based also on previous bilateral engagement Written feedback on the discussions provided by market participants Contributions always published on ECB website Outcome sessions to identify areas of widespread agreement and to focus on further in-depth explorations Ad-hoc outcome session held only for PSPs Workshop conducted to delve deeper into areas identified by the ECB and the market as requiring further exploration Forward-looking conclusions document summarizing the main outcomes and the way forward for the workstream 29 Value drivers presented and discussed (16 PSPs, 4 Merchants, 9 Consumers) www.ecb.europa.eu © 3"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,5,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"ECB-UNRESTRICTED Competition – version shared • There is -on a conceptual levelbroad understanding that a digital euro with mandatory acceptance and without scheme-fees for intermediaries could give intermediaries and merchants a better negotiation position vis-à-vis International Card Schemes, especially for those markets where no domestic or regional card scheme is present of where they for now have limited penetration. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,5,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,To counter the perceived risk of disintermediation of European private sector initiatives “cobranding/co-badging” of digital euro with domestic cards solutions or digital wallets could be a promising way out. •
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,5,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,This would also take away the need for expensive or complex initiatives of interoperability between domestic/regional solutions.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,5,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,This would reduce the dependency on ICS and create reach across the eurozone. www.ecb.europa.eu © 5
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,6,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Competition – proposed version after feedback ECB-UNRESTRICTED Note: Only key changes will be highlighted here and in the following • There is - on a conceptual level - broad an understanding that a digital euro with mandatory acceptance and without scheme-fees for intermediaries may make intermediaries and merchants better off: This could be the case if benefits of a potentially better negotiation position vis-à-vis International Card Schemes (indirect channel) and the benefits of the compensation model (direct channel) would outweigh the downsides of potentially lower ICS volumes.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,6,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Both benefits could apply especially for those markets where no domestic or regional card scheme is present or where they for now have limited penetration. •
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,6,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"To counter the perceived risk of disintermediation of European private sector initiatives, voluntary “cobranding/co-badging” of digital euro with domestic cards solutions or digital wallets could be pursued."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,6,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,In that scenario the private sector schemes could be their preferred brand wherever they are accepted […] • This could provide an alternative to expensive or complex initiatives of interoperability between domestic/regional solutions.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,6,4,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"It would reduce the dependency on ICS, essentially requiring them only for nonEU payments and/or as premium product, and create reach across the eurozone."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,6,5,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"More work is needed how this “co-branding/co-badging” can work with a logic as described above, while allowing for merchant preference and maintaining final consumer choice www.ecb.europa.eu © 6"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,7,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Synergies – version shared • The main point of synergy identified in this domain is the creation of what is dubbed a “common acceptance layer” for A2A/push payments for PoS and e-commerce transactions. •
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,7,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Main components of this common acceptance layer: a ‘free to use’, scheme-agnostic contactless (NFC) kernel with push-payments (terminal-host) protocol, QR-code standard, (open banking) API-standards and several other components currently under evaluation. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,7,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Next to that we will aim to identify in follow up sessions further opportunities to align on data definitions, standards and processes already widely adopted in order to create further synergies and cost advantages […] www.ecb.europa.eu © 7"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,8,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Synergies – proposed version after feedback • The main point of synergy identified in this domain is the creation of what is dubbed a “common acceptance layer” for A2A/push payments for PoS and e-commerce transactions. [
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,8,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"no change] • Main components of this common acceptance layer: open standards based on a ‘free to use’, scheme-agnostic contactless (NFC, first priority especially for POS and ATM) kernel with pushpayments (terminal-host) protocol, open QR-code standard (second priority), (open banking) API-standards and several other components currently under evaluation. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,8,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Next to that we will aim to identify in follow up sessions further opportunities to align on data definitions, standards and processes already widely adopted in order to create further synergies and cost advantages […] [no change] www.ecb.europa.eu © 8"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,9,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Business Model – version shared • As the compensation model and other aspects of the business model are part of the Digital Euro Regulation (DER) which is a remit of the co-legislators.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,9,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Nevertheless, we aim to share conclusions from the ERPB session on the requirements for a “fair for all” business model that ensures maximum value of lowest possible risks for actors in this space • Next to that the joint wish is to work on a staggered roll-out approach for digital euro that spreads cost over time and ensures the most important basic use cases, that ensure adoption and are most crucial for resilience purposes, are prioritised over use cases that can be introduced in a staggered approach. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,9,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Next to that we will more clearly identify where the space will be for the private sector to develop on top of digital euro as a “value added service” for which they can consider charging. •
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,9,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Another way to reduce the total investment costs for the market is to investigate how this development (and operational) cost can be mutualised. www.ecb.europa.eu © 9
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,10,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Business Model – proposed version after feedback • Stakeholder views on the proposed digital euro compensation model diverge and are mutually incompatible.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,10,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,As the compensation model and other aspects of the business model are part of the Digital Euro Regulation (DER) which is a remit of the co-legislators.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,10,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Nevertheless, we aim to share conclusions from the ERPB session on the requirements for a “fair for all” business model that ensures maximum value of lowest possible risks for actors in this space […] • Next to that the joint wish is to work on a staggered, roll-out approach for digital euro that spreads cost over time and ensures the most important basic use cases that ensure adoption and are most crucial for resilience purposes, are prioritised over use cases that can be introduced in a staggered at a later stage. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,10,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Next to that we will more clearly identify where the space will be for the private sector to develop on top of digital euro as a “value added service” for which they can consider charging.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,10,4,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"While so far stakeholders view opportunities from value added services as limited, this work may build on opportunities highlighted in the concurrent innovation workstream. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,10,5,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Another way to reduce the total investment costs for the market is to investigate how this development (and operational) cost can be mutualised. [
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,10,6,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,no change; reuse of existing infrastructure where possible is a given] www.ecb.europa.eu © 10
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,11,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"ECB-UNRESTRICTED Resilience and Transparency – version shared • With current reduction of cash for retail payments and further reduction expected there is the risk that it can no longer credibly function as a credible-fall-back in case of outages of cards systems, especially at PoS. • Digital euro will also ensure a contribution to resilience in general."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,11,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"With increased dependence on digital payments, especially at the physical PoS where often only one type of (cards) transaction is available, supplementing this with a means of payment that works on totally separate rails creates a much-needed alternative in case of disruptions. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,11,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Furthermore, offline digital euro also creates the option to pay if the internet connectivity of the merchant is lost. www.ecb.europa.eu © 11"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,12,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"ECB-UNRESTRICTED Resilience and Transparency – proposed version after feedback • With current reduction of cash for retail payments and further reduction expected there is a non-remote risk that it can no longer credibly function as a credible-fall-back in case of outages of cards systems, especially at PoS.* • Digital euro will also ensure a contribution to resilience in general."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,12,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"With increased dependence on digital payments, especially at the physical PoS where often only one type of (cards) transaction is available today, supplementing this with a European, publicly-supplied means of payment that works on totally separate rails creates a much-needed alternative in case of disruptions. •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,12,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Furthermore, offline digital euro also creates the option to pay if the internet connectivity of the merchant and/or the consumer is lost. ["
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,12,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"no further change; offline digital may also partially address electricity outage] *See e.g., Bundesbank cash foresight study: https://www.bundesbank.de/en/press/contributions/exploring-the-future-of-cashin-germany-a-foresight-study-942886 www.ecb.europa.eu © 12"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,13,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"ECB-UNRESTRICTED Digital & Financial inclusion, privacy and fraud prevention – version shared • As many new payment methods are offering “digital-only” solutions, digital euro can complement these by a solution available to people that are not willing/able to pay with a smartphone, do not have a bank account or want to benefit from the cash-like anonymity of an offline digital euro as foreseen in the draft Digital Euro Regulation. www.ecb.europa.eu © 13"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,14,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"ECB-UNRESTRICTED Digital & Financial inclusion, privacy and fraud prevention – proposed version after feedback • As many new payment methods are offering “digital-only” solutions, digital euro can complement these by a solution available to people that are not willing/able to pay with a smartphone, do not have or want to open a regular bank account or want to benefit from the cash-like anonymity privacy of an offline digital euro as foreseen in the draft Digital Euro Regulation. www.ecb.europa.eu © 14"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,16,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Proposed prioritised actions and in-depth analyses (1/4): 1.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,16,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Launch a joint effort with all private sector European cards and A2A schemes to work out concretely how these schemes and the digital euro propositions will coexist and strengthen each other and leverage on existing infrastructure where possible.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,16,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,This will involve: • Building on RDG work on following existing standards or mandating new/upgraded standards that benefit also private sector schemes. •
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,16,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"On a voluntary basis, work out how co-badging/tri-badging – if to be pursued – could concretely work, addressing concerns mentioned in the context of the ERPB work: collaborate with stakeholders to explore user experience (UX), branding, and business logic solutions (e.g., prioritising domestic solutions or offering the digital euro as an alternative). •"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,16,4,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Organize deep dive session on leveraging existing processes, also to take note of any misalignment of principles, rules and thresholds with the existing regulatory landscape: maximize the reuse of existing processes such as AML/KYC, fraud risk management, and onboarding/offboarding for the digital euro."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,16,5,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Address industry-wide roadblocks as needed. www.ecb.europa.eu © 16
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,17,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Proposed prioritised actions and in-depth analyses (2/4): 2.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,17,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Work together with co-legislators to achieve a “fair-for-all” compensation model that reduces risk of unintended consequences and ensures fair distribution of liabilities and value between intermediaries and consumers/merchants.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,17,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ERPB can help the co-legislators through joint positions on outstanding topics.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,17,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Main topics to be addressed here are (non-exhaustive): • Open funding (balancing openness versus sovereignty as well as operational and user experience complexity) • Compensation Model (fee caps, inter-PSP fees) • Access to OEM secure element (contractual, pricing) • Role of X-Pay wallets in digital euro ecosystem while balancing liabilities in light of the compensation model www.ecb.europa.eu © 17"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,18,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Proposed prioritised actions and in-depth analyses (3/4): 3.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,18,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,All parties agree on the need to ensure the potential issuing of a digital euro is as cost-efficient as possible.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,18,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Therefore ECB is open for a joint effort with all stakeholders on detailing out digital euro roll-out strategy to optimise speed to market, adoption and costs for participants – ensuring costs are as low as possible and smartly spread out over time without endangering policy objectives."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,18,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"This effort could include assessing a “one form factor per use case” approach or the later implementation of specific functionalities (e.g., recurring payments)."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,18,4,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Address technical roadblocks (if any) on “digital euro as a service” and clarify two-tier access to DESP: identify and address technical challenges to implementing ""digital euro as a service"" and clarify two-tier access to DESP within the rulebook development group and during draft reviews."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,18,5,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,This could include workshops with potential providers of such a service.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,18,6,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"Finally address in the rulebook fee reporting obligations for acquirers: involve stakeholders, especially merchants, in shaping rulebook requirements for reporting obligations for acquirers. www.ecb.europa.eu © 18"
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,19,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Proposed prioritised actions and in-depth analyses (4/4): 4.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,19,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Organise a deep dive on Digital Euro fraud risk management (tentative; subject to alignment with rulebook development group workstreams): Jointly work on the fraud management aspects to ensure fraud can be addressed by intermediaries across all payments forms and digital euro is -to the extent possiblefraud-resistant by design.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,19,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Organise a deep dive on offline digital euro [note: upon specific request of merchant associations] www.ecb.europa.eu © 19
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,20,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"ECB-UNRESTRICTED Next Steps - Documents Document Objective Timeline Minutes of the Fit in the Ecosystem concluding workshop held on 19- 20 May, aiming to report the full discussion including ECB statements Concluding Workshop - Minutes WP closed High level potential benefits and concerns related to the design of the digital euro identified during the Fit in the Ecosystem workstream and its concluding workshop on May 19-20 Forward looking conclusions WP closed (will feed into full report) Detailed forward-looking conclusions, including information on interactions with stakeholders throughout the entire workstream and during the concluding workshop Annex 1 - Forward looking conclusions WP closed (will feed into full report) Document serving as the basis for both informing internal and external stakeholders on the outcomes, conclusions, opportunities, concerns and proposed next steps."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,20,1,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,"After publication of the report the planning for the agreed follow-up actions will start, prioritised by both urgency and expected value."
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,20,2,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,The report will also indicate on a high level to which extent conclusions were supported by all or part of the stakeholders in the session.
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,20,3,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,Sharing 48hrs in advance for fatal flow check; Publication on ECB website tbd and also subject to today’s discussion Full FitE report www.ecb.europa.eu © 20
ecb.deprep250820_erpb_16thtechnicalsession_presentation.en.pdf,21,0,16th ERPB technical session on digital euro – presentation,European Central Bank,D:20250820104831+02'00',D:20250724102330+02'00',,ECB-UNRESTRICTED Thank you for your attention
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,1,0,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Discussion Paper Series The climate and the economy Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens Disclaimer: This paper should not be reported as representing the views of the European Central Bank (ECB)."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,1,1,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The views expressed are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect those of the ECB.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,2,0,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",ECB Discussion Paper Series No 22 1
