ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,0,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",Non-technical summary Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,1,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","In this Discussion Paper, we draw on economic research to identify some key mediumand long-run economic implications of these developments."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,2,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Climate change presents not just a threat to life as we know it, but also an opportunity to reinvent the global economy."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,3,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","We provide an educated speculation about how the world will likely change, based on our reading of the academic literature and on reasonable assumptions regarding the evolution of the climate and of public policy."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,4,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","We argue that in the near future, climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,5,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","While serious, these challenges appear manageable for EU member states that on average stand to gain from rising temperatures."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,6,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",This is especially true under a scenario of early and orderly climate transition.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,7,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to two types of uncertainty."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,8,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","First, the effect of increasing emissions on the climate is intrinsically uncertain."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,9,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","This means that for a given increase in the concentration of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere, there is a wide range of climate – and from there, economic – outcomes, ranging from the benign to the catastrophic."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,10,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Second, the path of the economy depends on the type, scope, and speed of implementation of climate policies by governments."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,11,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The cost to both the real and the financial sector is lowest when climate policies are introduced in a foreseeable and gradual way.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,12,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","In contrast, the ongoing aggression by the Russian Federation in Ukraine may precipitate abrupt changes in world energy markets, as well as in climate policy."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,13,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Both will plausibly accelerate the transition to green energy, but also increase the risk of a disorderly transition."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,14,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","An effective and smooth transition towards a net-zero economy requires a large-scale, coordinated response between fiscal authorities, central banks, regulators, and supervisors."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,15,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The dual objective should be to transition to a green economy while mitigating as much as possible the adverse economic effects.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,16,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The ECB’s optimal monetary policy in response to the challenges posed by climate change and the policy response thereto is one of inflation forecast targeting.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,4,17,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Beyond that, the ECB is expected to take actions, within its mandate, to support the green transition, but the nature of the challenge significantly exceeds the competencies and policy tools of the central bank. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 22 3"
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,5,0,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",“A change in the weather is sufficient to recreate the world and ourselves.”
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,5,1,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",Introduction Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,5,2,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",This note draws on economic research to identify some key mediumand long-run economic implications of these developments.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,5,3,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","As the opening quote suggests, climate change presents not just a threat to life as we know it, but also an opportunity to reinvent ourselves and our system of economic interactions."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,5,4,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","We provide an educated speculation about how the world will likely change, based on our reading of the academic literature and on reasonable assumptions regarding the evolution of the climate and of public policy."
