ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,3,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",What do we know about global warming?
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,4,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","The nature of climate change, the extent to which it is affected by humans, and its potential effects on our economy constitute complex questions."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,5,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",This complexity stems from a mixture of elements and technical details coming from the natural and the economic science.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,6,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","As a result, it can be difficult for commentators to sort between opposing views, as well as for the interested citizens to reach their own conclusions."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,7,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The global surface temperature has increased by about 1 OC relative to the pre-industrial period.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,8,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","In addition, each of the four last decades has been successively warmer than any decade that preceded it since 1850.3 A relevant question is to what extent this increase is caused by humans."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,9,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",It is well known that Earth’s temperature varies naturally for many reasons that do not depend on human behaviour.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,10,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","One factor comes from the fact that Earth’s orbit around the sun varies according to a fixed pattern, which in turn affects the average temperature."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,11,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",Another factor has to do with variations in the tilt of the Earth’s axis.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,12,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Humans can also affect the climate, for instance, by emitting greenhouse gases into the atmosphere."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,13,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The dominant greenhouse gas emitted by humans is carbon dioxide.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,14,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",It accounts for about 75 percent of total emissions of greenhouse gases.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,15,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Carbon dioxide is primarily emitted as a by-product when fossil fuels – i.e., coal, oil, and natural gas – are burnt."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,16,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",It is well accepted that the recent increase in global temperatures is largely driven by human activity.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,17,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","For example, the latest report from the UN Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), IPCC AR6 (IPCC, 2022), establishes that it is “unequivocal that human influence has warmed the atmosphere, oceans and land.”"
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,18,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",What is less clear is the extent of the anthropogenic contribution.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,19,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","According to the previous report (IPCC, 2014) it “is extremely likely that more than half of the observed increase in global average surface temperature from 1951 to 2010 was caused by the anthropogenic increase in greenhouse gas concentrations and other anthropogenic forces together.”"
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,20,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",Two effects from increasing the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere It has been known for more than 100 years now that the amount of carbon dioxide (and other greenhouse gases) in the atmosphere can influence the climate and the temperature.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,21,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","The resulting greenhouse effect is not entirely a bad thing: without greenhouse gases in the atmosphere, the average temperature of the Earth would be about -20 OC instead of the approximately 15 OC that we have right now."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,22,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The presence of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere only becomes a problem when its concentration increases beyond the long-term equilibrium value.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,6,23,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",3 IPCC AR6. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 22 5
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,0,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The total effect of emitting an additional unit of carbon dioxide can be divided into two sub-effects: the direct effect and the feedback effects.4 The two can differ in their size and direction.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,1,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","The direct effect comes from the fact that carbon dioxide in the atmosphere does not affect the inflow of energy, but it does affect the outflow."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,2,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Specifically, solar radiation passes right through the carbon dioxide, but carbon dioxide makes it more difficult for heat to radiate back into outer space."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,3,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Hence, if the climate system is initially in balance (steady state), an increase in the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere will cause the temperature to rise until the system reaches balance again.5 The effect is not particularly large but it is also uncontroversial."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,4,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","In fact, it can easily be replicated and shown to exist in a simple laboratory experiment."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,5,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","While the direct effect is known with relative certainty, the feedback effect is not, and this makes the total effect of increasing the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere highly uncertain."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,6,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Examples of feedback effects when the earth warms up include effects on cloud formation, wind and water flows, and the melting of ice sheets."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,7,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",These effects can either strengthen or weaken the direct effect and their respective effects are much harder to quantify.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,8,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Current estimates, however, suggest that the feedback effects will strengthen the direct effect (IPCC, 2022)."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,9,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","The climate sensitivity, tipping points, and threshold effects There is substantial uncertainty about how much the Earth will warm when the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere increases."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,10,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Reflecting this uncertainty, the IPCC provides an interval for the so-called equilibrium climate sensitivity."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,11,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The climate sensitivity tells us by how much the global temperature will increase if we were to double the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,12,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","In the latest report (AR6), the climate sensitivity is reported to likely be in the range between 2.5 OC to 4 OC, with a best estimate of 3 OC."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,7,13,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The climate sensitivity is very likely between 2 OC and 5 OC.6 See Figure 1.
