ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,3,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The question therefore becomes whether – and to what extent – climate policies can stimulate green innovation.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,4,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The importance of the answer is underscored by the fact that the optimal path of carbon emissions is rather different in models with static versus models with endogenous technology.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,5,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The literature has studied the relationship between energy-saving innovation and two broad factors: market forces and public policies.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,6,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",Market forces are exemplified by changes in energy prices.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,7,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Public policies are typically proxied by changes in the price of carbon, such as the imposition of carbon taxes or excise taxes on fuel."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,8,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",Studies using various methods and samples have reached the same conclusion: higher energy prices speed up innovation in energy-saving technologies.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,9,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",The first such force to study are oil and/or energy prices.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,10,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",1999) show that the energy efficiency of home appliances was strongly correlated with energy prices.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,11,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Using a time series of US patent data, Popp (2002) finds that a 10% increase in energy prices leads to 3.5% more patents in energy-saving technologies."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,12,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","2021) use aggregate data on GDP, energy, capital, and labor to compute a measure of the energy efficiency, and they show that energy efficiency has increased steadily since the oil shocks of the 1970s."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,13,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",2016) investigate how gas prices shape innovation in the car industry.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,14,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","They find that a 10% increase in fuel prices leads to 8.5% more innovation that develops alternatives to fossil fuel engines, and a 8.3% decline in innovation within the class of fossil fuel engines."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,15,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","In other words, the literature has broadly concluded that the direction of energy-saving innovation is endogenous and can be changed by market forces."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,16,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",Pricing carbon has been shown to have a similar positive effect on innovation in energy saving and low-carbon technologies.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,17,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",2016) find a similar increase in innovation in climate-friendly technologies when studying changes in fuel taxes instead of changes in fuel prices.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,18,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","The advantage of this method is that taxes are a policy instrument, therefore more exogenous to firm conditions."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,19,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","At the same time, the elasticity of innovation turns out to be lower than in the case of fuel prices."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,20,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",This is to be expected as demand is driven by the final price that consumers pay rather than the tax itself.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,21,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","Calel and Dechezlepretre (2016) compare firms that are regulated by the EU’s ETS to firms that are not and find that ETS-regulated firms increase low-carbon innovation (as measured by patent filings) by 10%, relative to non-regulated firms."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,22,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","The reaction of innovation to market forces and public policies is crucially shaped by path dependency, i.e., by the type of knowledge that firms have accumulated so far."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,23,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.",2016) compare firms that have accumulated knowledge in fossil fuel engine technologies to firms that have accumulated knowledge in alternative engine technologies.
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,12,24,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","They show that absent changes in gas prices, dirty technologies dominate clean ones, and the gap widens over time."
