ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,18,10,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","When the relative price of energy, food or other goods rises sharply because of a supply disruption or an extreme weather event, the society is worse off even if the aggregate price level remains perfectly stable."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,18,11,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","A supply disruption, global temperature rise, or an extreme weather event make the society worse off by reducing resources available for consumption."
ecb.dp22~cf428d6828.en.pdf,18,12,The climate and the economy,"Johannes Breckenfelder, Bartosz Mackowiak, David Marques, Conny Olovsson, Alexander Popov, Davide Porcellacchia, Glenn Schepens",D:20231106112458+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Climate change and the public policies to arrest it are and will continue reshaping the global economy. This Discussion Paper draws on economic research to identify some key medium- and long-run economic implications of these developments. It explores implications for growth, innovation, inflation, financial markets, fiscal policy, and several socio-economic outcomes. The main message that emerges is that climate change will cause income divergence across individuals, sectors, and regions, adjustment in energy markets, increased inflation variability, financial markets stress, intensified innovation, increased migration, and rising public debt. These challenges appear manageable for EU member states, especially under an early and orderly transition scenario. At the same time, the direction, scope, and speed of economic transformation is subject to large uncertainty due to two separate factors: the wide range of climate scenarios for a given trajectory of greenhouse gas emissions and the exact policy path governments choose, especially in the context of the ongoing Russian aggression in Ukraine.","A carbon tax is also likely to make people living today worse off for some time, because aggregate output is likely to fall temporarily as factors of productions reallocate between sectors."
