ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Despite a boost during the Corona pandemic, e-commerce in consumer markets in the euro area is still developing, except for durables and semi-durables, but the general trends are evident: Digitalisation creates room for new products and services, such as e-books or personalized items."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",It improves the efficiency of inventory flows in both stationary and online retail.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",The availability of online information seems to increase price transparency.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,4,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","The ability of online retailers to track and recognize customers, however, also increases their possibilities to price discriminate."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,5,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Personalised offers impair the comparability of prices, which also might complicate inflation measurement."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,6,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Price adjustment frictions in online retail seem lower than in stationary retail, resulting in more frequent price changes."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,7,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","If online bidding platforms gain prominence in business-to-business transactions as well, this might speed up price adjustment after cost shocks."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,8,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Information frictions play a crucial role in the provision of financial services.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,9,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Therefore, the digital revolution could be particularly disruptive for the financial sector."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,10,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In principle, the entire value-chain of the financial industry can be digitalised, and the potential for bundling financial and non-financial services has the potential to create new interconnections between the financial sector and the real economy."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,11,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","The proliferation of data (including non-financial data derived from e-commerce activities) favours financial services based on “hard” information, such as credit scores."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,12,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","While this creates efficiency gains and enables larger credit volumes, the diminishing role of long-term relationships may yield a more cyclical financial system."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,13,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Moreover, economies of scale favour concentration, which can create systemic risk."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,14,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",The rise of digital platforms poses a challenge for the integrated business model that dominates the banking sector in most advanced economies.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,15,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Specialised financial services providers are increasingly able to bypass banks’ distribution networks, which increases competition for non-core financial services and thus benefits consumers."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,16,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Moreover, digital platforms may interject themselves between banks and customers, collecting most rents and potentially monopolizing access to data."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,6,17,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In the extreme, these developments could imply a disintegration of the traditional bank business model. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 5"
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,7,0,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",The rapid rise of new technologies and business models in the financial sector poses several regulatory challenges.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,7,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","These include regulatory arbitrage, operational risks, market power, and the potential for market abuse and fraud."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,7,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Recent developments in crypto-asset markets are a case in point.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,7,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","While new players and infrastructures such as digital platforms and cloud computing promise large efficiency gains, they also create new interlinkages between the financial and non-financial sectors that are not well-understood, and possibly call for tighter supervision and regulation. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 6"
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,0,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Introduction Digitalisation already has had and will further have profound implications for economic growth, employment, wages, and consumer welfare, which will change both our economy and society.3 The technological progress that digitalisation in all its facets brings about has the potential to improve living standards and lead to new (and possibly more rewarding) jobs."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","At the same time, it is often seen critically, as a disruptive process for existing business models and leading to the disappearance of many jobs."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",This paper draws on economic research to identify some key implications of this evolution for the euro area economy and the associated challenges for policy makers.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","It surveys the evidence, highlights blank spots in current knowledge, discusses its opportunities and risks and likely future evolution in the euro area, and asks how this process can be facilitated by economic policies."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,4,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Doing so, it focuses on labour markets, inequality, e-commerce, and the financial system – topics that were covered to a lesser extent in the work stream on digitalisation (2021) or where more recent evidence is available."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,5,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","There is no strong evidence of aggregate productivity gains from digital technologies so far in the euro area, possibly due to slow adoption, among other factors."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,6,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","The limited effects on aggregate productivity in the euro area may be also due to lags in realizing productivity gains, and offsetting effects across firms amplified by concentration in market power in the hands of “superstar” firms."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,7,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Slow adoption may imply that the euro area falls behind the technological frontier, failing to reap the full benefits of digitalisation."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,8,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Artificial intelligence (AI) in particular can have adverse effects on employment of selected sectors and workers, as well as on wages and inequality."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,9,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Digitalisation enables automation of non-routine tasks (e.g., medical advice or coding), a process that will accelerate with the increasing usage of AI."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,10,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","While digitalisation does not necessarily lead to an overall loss in employment, it can lead to a displacement of workers, i.e., replace existing jobs, but might also create new jobs through reinstatement and through employment gains stemming from higher productivity."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,8,11,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","3 This paper follows the broad definition of digitalisation adopted in Work stream on digitalisation (2021), including, inter alia, a wide range of information and communication technologies, technologies enabling automation and robotisation, and technologies related to the processing and analysis of digital data, including big data, such as artificial intelligence and machine learning, and edge and quantum computing. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 7"
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,0,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Digitalisation can contribute to increasing income and wealth inequality.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Income inequality can increase inter alia to the extent that wages are affected differently across the skill distribution and because of changes in the distribution of high-wage and low-wage firms.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Wealth inequality can increase because the rise in demand for capital relative to labour increases returns to wealth, especially in the upper parts of the wealth distribution."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In addition, digitalisation lowers the price of digital products and thereby disproportionately benefits richer households, which tend to spend a higher fraction of their expenditures on digital products than low-income households."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,4,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Cushioning the effects on the labour market and inequality will require appropriate policies.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,5,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","To avoid polarisation of labour markets, it is essential to ensure training and re-training opportunities."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,6,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Active training can contribute to building the skills that are required to capture the benefits of digitalisation.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,7,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","However, increased education and a strong public safety net often are not sufficient to address some of the inclusion challenges in the labour market driven by digitalisation."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,8,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","For instance, there might be an under-provision of “good jobs”, defined as those that provide a middle-class living standard, sufficiently high benefits and security, and career prospects."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,9,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","At the same time, minimum wages can help contain wage inequality in the presence of these structural developments; however, more evidence is needed on their potential adverse effects on employment."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,10,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","The emergence of distribution platforms is especially relevant in e-commerce and fintech, with ambiguous net effects on competition."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,11,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Digitalisation affects both e-commerce and fintech in similar ways, in particular through the emergence of digital distribution platforms (such as Amazon)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,12,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",These profoundly change the market structure in both e-commerce and fintech: Higher information transparency and reduced search costs increase the competition within a platform.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,13,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","At the same time, network effects inhibit the competition between platforms, providing established platforms with comfortable monopoly rents, and giving a sizeable advantage to early innovators."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,14,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",The effects of digitalisation in the financial sector could be especially disruptive for the traditional bank business model.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,15,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","As with e-commerce, low-cost digital distribution enables specialised financial services providers to bypass banks’ distribution networks."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,16,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In addition, digitalisation reduces information-related frictions."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,17,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Financial services face considerable informational frictions which traditionally were overcome by means of relationship lending.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,9,18,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","As innovations in data collection and processing reduce information-related frictions, they favour business models based on “hard information”, which might disintegrate the business model of the traditional universal bank. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 8"
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,0,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","There are several implications for monetary policy, financial stability, and regulatory policies as well as competition policies."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Lower online price adjustment friction and online competition on offline prices can result in more flexible prices, enhancing their responsiveness to monetary policy by making the Phillips curve steeper, other things equal."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Similarly, a more competitive financial system will likely see a stronger pass-through of monetary policy into market rates and financial conditions."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",A financial system that is more reliant on hard information and is less built on long-term relationships (which facilitate the smoothing of credit terms over the cycle) is bound to become more cyclical.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,4,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","However, new technologies, business models and processes in the financial sector may not be covered by the existing regulatory framework, suggesting that the regulatory toolkit requires updating."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,5,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Finally, given the ambiguous effects of digital platforms on competition, it is important that competition policies accompany the digital evolution to ensure appropriate competition also across platform providers."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,6,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Overall, digitalisation can substantially lift living standards in the longer run, assuming appropriate economic policies."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,7,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In a 1930 essay on “Economic possibilities for our grandchildren”, John Maynard Keynes predicted that 100 years later, living standards would be four to eight times as large as at the time of writing, and the “economic problem” of scarcity and inequality would be solved."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,8,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",He made these predictions at the onset of the Great Depression when commentators feared a decline in prosperity or at least a slowdown in the rapid improvement in the standard of living.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,9,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","His understanding, in contrast, was that “the increase of technical efficiency has been taking place faster than we can deal with the problem of labour absorption”, that the “unemployment due to our discovery of means of economising the use of labour” was “outrunning the pace at which we can find new uses for labour”."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,10,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",He reasoned that this was only a temporary phase of “maladjustment”.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,11,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Keynes’ predictions were remarkably close to the actual development – not with regard to jobs and inequality, but with regard to income."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,12,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In a similar vein, if our institutions are able to solve the challenges due to the risk of “maladjustment” and labour market polarisation, the technological progress that is brought about by digitalisation certainly has the potential to boost economic growth and support the transformation to an environmentally sustainable economy, for the benefits not only of the current, but also of the coming generations."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,13,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",The paper is structured by thematic areas.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,14,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Section 2 focuses on the effects of automation on labour markets and inequality.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,10,15,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Section 3 explores the implications of a few other prominent aspects of ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 9
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,11,0,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","digitalisation, such as the centralisation of the provision of digital goods and services by a handful of online platforms, with a focus on the retail (e-commerce) and financial sectors (fintech)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,11,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Effects of automation on labour markets and inequality 2.1 Aggregate effects on productivity and living standards Figure 1: Total factor productivity growth, 1955-2019, percent Source: Authors’ calculations on World Bank data Despite the development of digitalisation and artificial intelligence, aggregate productivity growth has been subdued since the mid-2000s."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,11,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","After the protracted productivity slowdown starting in the mid-1970s in most advanced economies, since the mid-1990s productivity growth in the US has been stronger than in the euro area, at least until the Global Financial Crisis (Figure 1)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,11,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",This fact raises the question whether more advanced digitalisation and larger investment in intangibles in the US could have contributed to this difference.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,11,4,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","For instance, research has linked robot adoption with larger labour productivity growth.4 However, these developments have also been linked to the decline in business dynamism and the rise of market power in the US economy."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,11,5,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In contrast to the US, in the euro area 4 Graetz and Michaels, 2018 ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 10"
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,0,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","market power has remained quite stable over the last 15 years or so, especially in manufacturing.5 Some research has tried to reconcile the paradox of the optimism about the potential of AI technologies contrasting with the poor productivity growth measured in the data by alluding to implementation lags: For general purpose technologies (which can in principle affect the entire economy) to raise aggregate productivity, it may take years or decades for new capital stock to accumulate and for complementary assets to be invented, built and installed.6 Investment in new technologies boosts productivity for early adopters but may depress productivity for other firms, which may become less competitive or are deterred from entering the market."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","This phenomenon is particularly salient for intangible investment, such as software, research, and development, which has been rising steadily (as a share of GDP) since the 1980s."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","The first firms to adopt these technologies have a competitive advantage and may grow very fast, sometimes becoming superstar firms and sizably contributing to aggregate productivity (direct effect)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","However, productivity and dynamism may be reduced as non-adopters become less competitive and the high fixed costs associated with intangible investment and the increased market power of adopters might deter other firms from entering (indirect effect)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,4,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",The net effect on aggregate productivity is thus ambiguous and likely to change over time.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,5,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Technological progress has generated a steady increase in living standards, which has been unequally distributed."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,6,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In the United States, average real incomes have increased by 60% since the 1980s, but stagnated for the bottom half of the distribution, implying large improvements for the top of the income distribution.7 In contrast, in Europe while inequalities also increased in most countries, the incomes of the poorer half rose by 40% between 1980 and 2017.8 The emergence of new digital technologies with AI breakthroughs enables automation of nonroutine tasks, raising new concerns about the impact of these new technologies on labour markets and inequality."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,7,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Between 1990 and 2010, wage and job polarisation accelerated as both low-skill lowpay jobs and high-skill high-pay jobs experienced higher demand, whilst many middle-skilled workers, mostly in routine-intensive jobs, were displaced due to computerisation."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,8,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In contrast, the period since around 2010, on which we focus in this section, is characterised by the emergence of AI breakthroughs, 5 Cavalleri et al.,"
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,12,9,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","2019 6 See, e.g., Syverson, 2019 and ECB, 2021 7 Piketty, Saez and Zucman, 2018 8 Blanchet, Chancel and Gethin, 2019 ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 11"
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,0,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","including advancement in robotics, supervised and unsupervised learning, natural language processing, machine translation, or image recognition among many other activities, that enable automation of human labour in non-routine tasks in manufacturing but also services (e.g., medical advice or writing code)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,1,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",This raises new concerns about the impact of these new technologies on labour markets and inequality.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,2,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","2.2 Employment Automation, including AI, impacts overall employment through various channels."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,3,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","In general, the overall employment effect of new technological developments depends on the relative strength of three channels."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,4,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","First, new developments destroy jobs because they automate tasks (displacement effect)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,5,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Second, they might complement human labour, thereby increasing productivity and indirectly resulting in more jobs due to an increase in demand for products of some firms (productivity effect)."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,6,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","Third, a combination of both effects, with some tasks and jobs being replaced but new ones being created – the so called “reinstatement” effect."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,7,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.",Digital technologies enhance globalisation as they make it easier to trade services across borders but can adversely affect employment and wages of some workers.
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,8,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","This pre-existing trend of “globotics” (globalisation and robotics) has been compounded by the substantial expansion of work from home during the pandemic, which may persist into the future."
ecb.dp23~6b1e40acca.en.pdf,13,9,Digitalisation and the economy,"Luca Dedola, Michael Ehrmann, Peter Hoffmann, Ana Lamo, Gonzalo Paz Pardo, Jiri Slacalek, Georg Strasser",D:20231106113348+01'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Digitalisation has fundamentally changed the global economy and will continue to do so. This paper draws on economic research to identify some of its key implications for labour markets, inequality, e-commerce and the financial system. Beyond its potential to boost productivity and living standards, digitalisation: i) does not necessarily replace jobs on aggregate but changes their content; ii) tends to raise income and wealth inequality; iii) has ambiguous effects on competition; and iv) might change how the retail and financial sectors respond to monetary policy. Developing adequate (re-)training opportunities and providing a labour market, regulatory, and innovation environment which encourages the creation of “good jobs” is essential to improve productivity and equity while avoiding a polarisation of labour markets. E-commerce and fintech will likely lead to a faster transmission of monetary policy. The rise of fintech brings about new risks for regulatory arbitrage and has ramifications for financial stability.","While increased trade in services may result in aggregate productivity gains, it can adversely affect employment and wages of some groups of employees in services, such as office work, who were not much impacted by the first digital wave.9 In contrast, in manufacturing, to the extent that the introduction of robots results in a decreasing weight of wages in total costs, automation lowers the incentives to open new manufacturing plants in lowwage economies, ultimately pushing for the relocalisation of manufacturing back into high-income countries -- an issue that has gained increasing attention following the supply chain disruptions triggered by the pandemic and the war in Ukraine. 9 Baldwin, 2019 ECB Discussion Paper Series No 23 12"
