ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,1,0,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Discussion Paper Series Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini-Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux No 26 Disclaimer: This paper should not be reported as representing the views of the European Central Bank (ECB)."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,1,1,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",The views expressed are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect those of the ECB.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,2,0,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Discussion papers Discussion papers are research-based papers on policy relevant topics, offering a broader and more balanced perspective."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,2,1,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","While being partly based on original research, they place the analysis in the wider context of the literature on the topic."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,2,2,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","They also consider explicitly the policy perspective, with a view to develop a number of key policy messages."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,2,3,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Their format offers the advantage that alternative analyses and perspectives can be combined, including theoretical and empirical work."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,2,4,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",The selection and distribution of discussion papers are subject to the approval of the Director General of the Directorate General Research. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 26 1
