ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,7,7,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",Panel b) latest observation 31 May 2024.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,7,8,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","3 In December 2023, the ECB announced its intention to continue reinvesting, in full, the principal payments from maturing securities purchased under the PEPP during the first half of 2024."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,7,9,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Over the second half of the year, it intends to reduce the PEPP portfolio by €7.5 billion per month on average."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,7,10,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",The Governing Council intends to discontinue reinvestments under the PEPP at the end of 2024.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,7,11,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",See ECB press release as of 15 December 2023. ECB Discussion Paper Series No 26 6
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,0,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","On the fiscal side, euro area governments responded to the energy shock and the resulting high inflation with a set of mainly ""unconventional"" stimulus measures, aimed at containing the rise in energy prices and protecting households' purchasing power."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,1,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Prior to Russia's invasion of Ukraine, energy support in the euro area was relatively limited and confined to a few countries."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,2,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Following the surge in energy prices, fiscal support measures were adopted at a large scale in many countries, bringing the (gross) size of this stimulus to about 1.7% of GDP at the euro area aggregate level in 2022 (Chart 3)."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,3,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","The measures mainly took the form of reductions in indirect taxes and increases in subsidies, mostly on energy products, as well as transfers to households."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,4,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Many were extended into 2023 and new ones adopted, so that the fiscal support, albeit declining in size, is estimated to have remained large in 2023 (at 1.3 percent of GDP)."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,5,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","A sizeable part of the support, especially from 2023 onwards, consists of subsidies related to price cap measures adopted in some of the largest euro area countries."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,6,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",Around 70% of the measures in terms of budgetary impact is set to be withdrawn in 2024.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,7,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Given the profile and composition of measures, fiscal policy is expected to contribute in the shorter run (2022-23) to reducing inflation pressures."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,8,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Hence, the “unconventional” nature of many of these measures: albeit a fiscal stimulus, being mostly in the form of temporary reductions in indirect taxes and increases in subsidies, it can contribute to reducing inflation, while increasing real output, over the period of its application."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,9,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","However, as these measures are withdrawn and the positive wealth effect of the overall fiscal stimulus may still persist, a reversal of the impact (higher inflation) is expected, especially in 2024."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,10,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Compared with a no-fiscal policy change scenario, such measures are generally assessed to mitigate the effects of the energy shock and smoothen the inflation profile, with lower inflationary pressures in the short run and higher afterwards.4 Chart 3: Fiscal policy response Large temporary stimulus over 2022-23 in response to the energy crisis and high inflation (percent of GDP) Total Subsidies Capital transfers to firms Other transfers Government consumption Indirect taxes Direct taxes and social contributions 2.0 1.7 1.8 1.6 1.3 1.4 1.2 1.0 0.8 0.6 0.4 0.4 0.1 0.1 0.1 0.2 0.0 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025 2026 Source: June 2024 Eurosystem staff’s macroeconomic projections 2.3."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,11,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Large heterogeneity in fiscal positions, with very high public debt levels in some countries The monetary tightening has taken place against a large degree of fiscal heterogeneity across the euro area countries, with very high debt levels in several of them."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,12,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","In 2022, public debt ratios ranged from about 20% to 170% of GDP (Chart 4a)."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,8,13,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",Budget balances ranged from deficits close to 8% to surpluses of over 2% of GDP (Chart 4b).
