ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,23,23,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","When the real value of government debt is not too high, a moderate surplus is sufficient to maintain fiscal stability."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,23,24,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",An increase in the real value of government debt leads to an increase in the primary surplus and vice versa.
