ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,23,30,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",55 See also Sims (2023).
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,23,31,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","56 See, for instance, Davig and Leeper (2006) and Schmidt (2023). ECB Discussion Paper Series No 26 22"
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,0,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",An increase in government debt no longer leads to an increase in primary surpluses – the economy is in a fiscally-dominant regime.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,1,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","If the central bank sticked to the conventional Taylor rule, fiscal stability could be at risk, for an increase in policy rates, which raises debt servicing costs, would feed into a higher debt burden with no adjustment in surpluses."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,2,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",Such fiscal instability ultimately erodes price stability.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,3,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","As Sargent (1982) notes, under fiscal dominance “the fiscal authorities select a path or policy for government expenditures and explicit taxes implying growth rates of government indebtedness to which the monetary authority must adjust.”"
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,4,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Suppose, then, that the central bank adjusts by deviating from the conventional Taylor rule and keeping the policy rate below some upper bound."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,5,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",Schmidt (2023) analyses the macroeconomic consequences of this monetary-fiscal policy configuration in a New Keynesian rational-expectations model with sticky prices.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,6,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","In the model, the economy endogenously shifts from the orthodox regime to the fiscally-dominant regime."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,7,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",The adjustment in the monetary policy rule when the economy is in the fiscally-dominant regime helps to preserve fiscal stability.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,8,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","At the same time, the central bank generically fails to achieve its price stability goal."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,9,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.",Inflation is systematically higher than it would be if fiscal policy always ensured government debt sustainability and the central bank was solely concerned with inflation stabilisation.
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,10,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","In the model, this inflation bias increases with the real value of government debt."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,11,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Consider an economic environment akin to the one many economies faced in 2021/2022: Inflation and policy rates have been low for a long time, and government-debt-to-GDP ratios are high."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,12,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","Then, the economy is hit by a large and persistent inflationary shock."
ecb.dp26~de397a039d.en.pdf,24,13,Monetary and fiscal policy interactions: risks to price stability in times of high government debt,"Cristina Checherita-Westphal, Anna Rogantini Picco, Sebastian Schmidt, Jean-David Sigaux",D:20241003110359+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The change in macroeconomic conditions since the ECB’s strategy review in 2021 towards an environment characterised by above-target inflation, high interest rates, and renewed concerns about elevated government debt has been a vocal reminder of the intricate interdependencies between monetary and fiscal policies. Against this background, our paper reviews the literature on how central banks’ ability to maintain price stability is shaped by their interactions with fiscal policy and the state of the economy. According to standard models, a policy framework aimed at price stability requires suitable commitments from both monetary and fiscal authorities. When public debt burdens become too high, price stability may be at risk. The paper also draws lessons on how to mitigate such risks.","The central bank, in line with a conventional Taylor rule, raises the policy rate more than one-for-one with inflation."
