ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,2,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.”
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,2,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Keywords: DSGE Estimation, Inflation De-anchoring, Regime Switching JEL-Codes: D83, D84,E10 ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 1"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Non-technical summary When inflation is near its target, central banks often allow for temporary fluctuations of inflation and adjust interest rates only gradually."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This approach is common when supply shocks create a trade-off between stabilizing output and inflation, prompting a ”looking through” strategy."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","However, in times of high inflation or prolonged low inflation, there is a risk that inflation expectations may stray from the central bank’s target—a phenomenon known as de-anchoring."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In such cases, a stronger policy response is crucial to maintain credibility."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This paper proposes a framework that quantifies the risk of de-anchoring using a regime-switching model.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Unlike traditional, purely descriptive indicators from financial markets or surveys, our modelbased measure evaluates different scenarios—including forecast baselines and policy counterfactuals—in real-time."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This tool helps policymakers understand when a gradual approach is sufficient, such as in environments where inflation is close to target and the stock of credibility is high, and when aggressive action is warranted to prevent expectations from de-anchoring, as in high inflation scenarios with limited stock of credibility."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In an empirical analysis, we analyze long-run inflation expectations according to the participants of the survey of professional forecasters (SPF)."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,8,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","We show that long-run inflation expectations are heterogenous, time-varying, and partially driven by current inflation."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,9,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We then use a simple economic model to show that a more forceful or aggressive response to inflation deviations from target is optimal if the central bank has limited credibility or inflation expectations become de-anchored.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,10,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",A further increase in forcefulness is optimal if the actions of the central bank influence its credibility.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,11,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In that case, rate changes do not only affect output and inflation, but also the credibility of the central bank."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This calls for a stronger response to inflation deviations from target, once risks to the anchoring of inflation expectations start to emerge."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Finally, we provide a model-based approach to the risks of de-anchoring."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We extend a workhorse macroeconomic model with a regime switching mechanism.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,15,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In this model, agents attach a higher weight to the de-anchored regime, if the data supports de-anchoring."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,16,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Using this model we revisit the period since 2010 and identify one period of de-anchoring between 2011 and 2015.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,3,17,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The more recent period of the inflation surge is not identified as a de-anchoring event, because of the relatively short-lived increase in inflation and the strong response of the ECB. ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 2"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,4,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Taking into account uncertainty, we construct a measure of risks of de-anchoring, based on stochastic simulations of the de-anchoring model around a baseline."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,4,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","We find that the risks of de-anchoring increase if the central bank is not reacting to a deviation of inflation from target, if the central bank enters into an inflationary or deflationary period with a low stock of credibility, and if the economic environment is characterized by a high degree of uncertainty and volatility."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,4,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","We furthermore show that if the central bank is ’looking-through’ supply side driven inflation, the risks of de-anchoring can increase. ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 3"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",1 Introduction High inflation is putting central banks to the test.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The recent surge in inflation, following an extended period of low inflation and interest rates near the effective lower bound, has radically transformed the policy landscape."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","A large part of this shift is driven by supply-side factors such as energy prices, output bottlenecks, and economic scarring, which push output and inflation in opposite directions."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Unlike demand shocks, these supply shocks present more complex dilemmas for monetary policy, especially in an environment marked by persistent shocks and heightened uncertainty."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Since monetary policy cannot directly influence the price of supply factors, a restrictive policy runs the risks of contracting activity severely without reducing inflation effectively."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In contrast, an overly accommodative stance risks unmooring inflation and inflation expectations from the announced target."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","De-anchored inflation expectations can imply self-enforcing dynamics, undermining the credibility of monetary policy."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In this paper, we offer a novel framework for understanding the monetary policy implications of a potential de-anchoring of inflation expectations through a regime-switching approach."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,8,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We analyse the role of monetary policy to stabilize inflation in an environment where the decisions of the central bank influence the probability of switching between a well-anchored and a de-anchored regime.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,9,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Our setting assumes that the central bank operates with a constant inflation target, but private agents might doubt that the central bank will bring inflation back to target in the medium term."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,10,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Instead they rely on a time varying inflation target.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,11,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","By choosing an appropriate policy path, the central bank can gradually bring back inflation to target and realign the private sector’s beliefs with the actual inflation target."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The more aggressive policy contributes to avoiding a switching to the de-anchored regime or to switch back to the anchored regime.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","A stronger response to inflation, in order to align expectations, opens a trade-off between the welfare-losses of an overly hawkish stance and the welfare gains associated with an earlier convergence of expectations with the central bank target."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",To understand the interplay potential de-anchoring and monetary policy strategy we make the following steps: We describe the optimal monetary policy response to switching credibility in theory.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,15,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In particular, we derive optimal policy under discretion and under commitment in a simple three equation model where the policy maker influences the probabilities of moving from one regime to another regime."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,16,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In a further step, we apply the framework to a quantitative policy environment using a macro-economic workhorse model in the euro area."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,5,17,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We introduce regime switching and conduct stochastic simulations to ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 4
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",derive a measure of risks of de-anchoring of inflation expectations.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Finally, we revisit a key historical episode at the peak of inflation to answer the policy question if a looking through strategy would have risked credibility."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","We contribute to the literature along three dimensions: First, we extend on optimal policy considerations in regime switching models under constant regime change probabilities to the case where the probabilities are endogenous and driven by the choices of the policy maker."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Second, we take the framework of analysis to a quantitative setting in a workhorse DSGE model with regime switching (RSDSGE) to evaluate the euro area and to identify episodes of de-anchoring."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Third, we provide a metric of (risks) of inflation de-anchoring in macro models with regime-switching based on a method to conduct stochastic simulations of the RSDSGE model."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","With the analysis of optimal monetary policy under regime-switching, respectively forms of heterogeneous expectations and learning we relate to a growing literature."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Nakata and Schmidt (2022) analyse optimal monetary policy in a model with zero lower bound on interest rates and sunspot shocks driving the economy into a liquidity trap.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We apply their approach in this paper to analyse optimal policy under the constant switching parameter case.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,8,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Choi and Foerster (2021) analyse simple optimal rules in a regime switching model, finding that it is optimal to switch the parameters of the rule conditional on the prevailing regime."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,9,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Our approach gives qualitatively similar results, in a simpler, textbook setting: Rather than considering simple optimal rules, we focus on the optimality conditions in a simple three equation model."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,10,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Our results are confirming that the reaction to inflation and output should be conditional on the regime, and show that the trade-offs are more pronounced if future credibility is considered."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,11,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Similar to Gasteiger (2021) we find that the central bank needs to act more hawkish if it considers its stock of credibility.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,6,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Similar to us, Gasteiger (2021) analyses optimal monetary policy in a model with heterogeneous expectation formation processes."
