ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The most frequently used filter for regime-switching applications is the Kim-Nelson filter.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Kim (1994) extended the Hamilton filter to regime switching state space models, incorporating regime switching in both the mean and variance."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","To render regime switching models tractable Kim proposed to approximate the regime specific Gaussian-mixture, i.e. the K´alm´an filter posteriors originating from the different regimes, with a single normal distribution."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,15,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This approximation is of key importance in regimeswitching DSGE models.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,16,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Alternatives, like the interacting multiple model, where the approximation is of the current state instead of the posterior, have been shown to deliver similar filter accuracy, while being slightly more efficient, for details see (Hashimzade et al.,"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,17,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The accuracy is this approximation is of key importance, as if it is inaccurate the forward solution of the DSGE will be arbitrarily off."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,18,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This approximation is in general accurate if the switching in state variables is small, e.g. if the switching on states does not have an impact on long-run properties, such as asymptotic mean and variance."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,7,19,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In DSGE models, the forward-looking agents base their decisions on the expected future states of the world, and can therefore anticipate regime switches, which in turn can influence the current equilibrium. ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 6"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,8,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",In our framework the solutions of the two regimes are spanned by the same minimum state space (MSV).
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,8,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This is due to expectations being fundamental ( i.e. fully determined by exogenous shocks), as well as some additional properties which allow us to simplify the computations."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,8,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Note that the only parameters in our model that influence the ergodic mean are the regime transition probabilities.
