ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",nomic agents towards a notion of limited credibility of the central bank.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Under this definition of limited credibility, agents might find it preferable to deviate from the announced inflation target when the central bank fails to deliver on it’s price stability commitment."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The possibility of inflation expectations becoming de-anchored has implications for the formulation of the monetary policy strategy of central banks targeting a specific inflation rate.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In the next part, in an optimal monetary policy exercise, we derive how central banks facing a potential de-anchoring shall conduct policies."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We show that under de-anchoring risks the central bank should not only care about inflation realizations but also the medium-term inflation expectations of households and firms.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This additional consideration is calling for a more forceful reaction of the central bank to deviations of inflation from the announced target.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","2 Optimal monetary policy To analyse optimal monetary policy, we take the 3-equation NK DSGE model and augment it with a Markov switching process."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,13,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The resulting model is then composed of three key economic equations: the Phillips curve, the New Keynesian aggregate demand (IS) curve, the monetary policy rule, and the Markov-switching process."
