ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The ’stock of credibility’ gives a scenario based on higher inflation in the pre-sample and consequently lower credibility.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The ’Uncertainty’ entry shows the results for the ’ex-post’ version but shocks being scaled up by a factor of 5.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,8,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",to the entry ’ex-post data’ in graph 4.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,9,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","We assume again, that forecasters had perfect knowledge of the expost realization of data, but policy rates would stay as low as expected in ’Sept. 21’."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,10,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In monetary policy had looked fully through the inflation surge, inflation would have increased significantly and reduced the weight on the well-anchored regime, giving rise to sizable upside de-anchoring risks."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,11,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The overall de-anchoring risks increase to around 30 percent.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The high downward de-anchoring risks from ’Sept 21’ are strongly reduced and upward risks increase markedly, contributing two-thirds of total de-anchoring risks."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Stock of credibility The initial level of trust that agents place in the central bank’s ability to control inflation forms the foundation for the probability assigned to the regimes.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",A central bank with a strong track record of maintaining price stability starts with a higher stock of credibility and thus a lower probability for the de-anchored regime.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,15,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Conversely, if the central bank has previously struggled with inflation, agents will be more inclined towards the de-anchored regime ex-ante."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,16,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This initial condition reflects the accumulated credibility (or lack there of) of the central bank.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,17,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Preceding the inflation surge, the euro area went through a long period of low inflation and rates close to the ELB."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,18,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",When entering the inflation surge the credibility was reduced due to several years of inflation below target.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,33,19,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In addition to the reduced stock of credibility, the perceived target was significantly below ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 32"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The low target did not help to explain the high inflation realization, implying that the model simulations are predominantly identifying de-anchoring in the lower tail of inflation realisations, implying the high downward de-anchoring risks shown in the right columns of table (2) for ’Sept. 21’."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","We conduct a counterfactual simulation (’Stock of credibility’ in table (2) ), where we modify the data prior to the simulation horizon."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Specificially, we feed the model with positive demand shocks to engineer a demand driven inflation increase, pushing average inflation in the ’pre-sample’ (2015q1 to 2021q2) to 2.9% annual inflation."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The higher inflation drives up the perceived inflation target and reduces credibility of the central bank at the same time.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","As a result of these two factors, de-anchoring risks to the upside increase considerable (row ’Stock of credibility’) over the simulation horizon, which includes the inflation surge."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",De-anchoring risks increase if the stock of credibility is low and the perceived target deviates from 2% into the same direction as the inflation surprises.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The calibration of the evolution of the perceived target is also affecting the identification of de-anchoring risks.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The results of the paper are produced with a persistence parameter (ς in equation (35)) of 0.98.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,8,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In appendix (E) we report the sensitivity of de-anchoring risk, using the calibration of Coenen and Schmidt (2016)."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,9,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Impact of uncertainty Heightened economic uncertainty, reflected in a higher volatility of structural shocks hitting the economy, may contribute to a higher evidence for de-anchoring."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,10,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This is partially explained by the property that the de-anchored regimes has an additional state variable allowing for higher flexibility to explain volatile data.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,11,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","When agents face a turbulent environment with wide-range of potential outcomes, they are more likely to doubt the central bank’s ability to steer inflation towards its target."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This uncertainty can stem from factors like volatile energy prices, unexpected supply chain disruptions, or unexpected geopolitical events."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","On the other hand if the uncertainty is anticipated, then it can reduce the information content of the de-anchoring signals."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","When large shocks fall short of the scale they were expected to be drawn from, then heightened uncertainty can be reaffirming the central bank’s credibility by discounting evidence of de-anchoring more."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,34,15,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The row ’Uncertainty’ illustrates this property by re-simulating the ’Sept. 21’ exercise, scaling the shocks by a factor of 5. ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 33"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Impact of realized shocks Furthermore, the actual shocks that hit the economy ultimately determine the trajectory of perceived beliefs."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","If these shocks align with the baseline projections, the perceived probability of de-anchoring will likely remain unchanged."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","However, if unexpected shocks push inflation persistently away from the target, despite the central bank’s efforts, agents will update their beliefs, leading to a higher de-anchored regime probability."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","For instance, a sudden surge in energy prices not anticipated by the baseline projection could trigger such an update."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The de-anchoring probabilities are closely linked to the forecast errors.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","If the credible regime’s sequential forecast, given the realization of the shocks, implies smaller forecast errors than the ones of the de-anchored regime, the evidence for de-anchoring is reduced and the central bank increases its stock of credibility."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","If the realizations of the shocks change the relative forecast performance in favor of the de-anchored regime, the evidence for de-anchoring increases."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",It is important to note that changes to the perception of the regimes take place only gradually.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,8,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The required evidence for a regime switch needs to be sufficiently large and persistent.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,9,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Generally speaking, in situations of high uncertainty, large shocks might generate economic states inconsistent with a credible central bank, suggesting a potential loss of credibility."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,10,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",3.2.4 A counterfactual scenario: looking through external price shocks The optimal policy exercise in section 2 showed that under a mounting risk of a de-anchoring of inflation expectations the central bank should react more forcefully against deviations of inflation from target.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,11,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We illustrate this property by revisiting the exercise illustrated in figure 4.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",We change the central bank’s inflation measure from headline consumer prices to the value of domestically produced goods and services (GDP deflator).
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The central bank´s reaction function can then be described by it = ρit−1 +(1−ρ)(π∗+ bπcore t + byt)+εi,t (36) where bπcore t is the inflation gap between GDP deflator inflation and the constant inflation target π∗, byt is the output gap and εi,t is an i.i.d."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,35,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Figure (6) shows that the overall risks of de-anchoring increase for all exercises, but to a limited ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 34"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","If the central bank is looking through price pressures originating from imported goods, it is tolerating more pronounced deviations of headline inflation from target."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The deviations can lead to a higher weight on the de-anchored regime, increasing also the risks of de-anchoring."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The size of the increase in risks is however limited.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This is due to the medium-term orientation of the ECB, implying a certain degree of looking-through temporary shocks."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This medium-term orientation is implicitly captured in the estimated coefficients of the Taylor rule.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","For more persistent, foreign price shocks such as the shocks occurring after the Russian invasion of Ukraine, the transmission to domestic inflation was relatively fast and substantial."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This high degree of transmission implies that also for the case of targeting domestic price inflation, rates are increased substantially."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Figure 6: Risks of (upward and downward) inflation de-anchoring: Headline Inflation versus Inflation of domestically produced goods and services (percentages) Figure 7: Uncertainty around the September 2022 forecast inflation projections (year-on-year percentage points) 60 Headline Domestic 50 40 Risks 30 20 10 0 June 21 Sept. 21 Dec. 21 Mar. 22 June 22 Sept. 22 Dec. 22 Mar. 23 June 23 Sept. 23 Dec. 23 Mar. 24 June 24 Sept. 24 Sources: Authors’ computations.
