ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","The simulations are based on a regime-switching version of NAWM-I, where the credible regime is defined as the estimated version of the NAWM-I with a fixed inflation target, the de-anchored regime is characterized with a time-varying inflation target."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The share of de-anchoring paths is based on 5000 simulations around the forecast baseline.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,15,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Looking-through denotes a scenario where the Taylor rule responds to core inflation (GDP deflator).
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,16,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",increases for the looking-through case.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,36,17,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This increase in uncertainty is driven by the more frequent cases ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 35
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,37,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","of the de-anchored regime, which leads to more extreme paths of inflation. ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 36"
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",4 Conclusions The central bank cannot directly address the price impact of a foreign shock.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In such cases, looking through the shock can be welfare maximizing."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In practice, this is reflected in concepts such as the medium-term orientation of monetary policy or frameworks like average inflation targeting."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Both from a theoretical as well as from a practical point of view, looking-through is advisable as long as inflation expectations are firmly anchored."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In this paper, we analyze the implications of a potential de-anchoring of medium-term inflation expectations for monetary policy, both theoretically and quantitatively."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","From a normative perspective we find that the possibility that the economy might switch into a de-anchored regime, changes the optimal monetary policy prescription in comparison to the case of a model without regime switches."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",In the regime switching case the optimality criteria under commitment are extended to include the impact of the current interest rate decision on the probability of switching to another regime.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The implied rule under regime switches implies that the central bank in the credible regime chooses a more aggressive policy.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,8,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This reflects the possibility of switching to the low credibility regime.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,9,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",A further increase in the response to deviations of inflation from target is found for the case of endogenous switching.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,10,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",In this case the interest rate is used to stabilize and to lean against a possible switch to the low credibility case.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,11,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","From a positive perspective we use a canonical policy model for the euro area and implement a regime switching version, allowing to filter for the probability of being in the respective regime."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,12,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Analysing the period between 2010 and 2024 in the euro area, a de-anchored regime is detected for the period between 2011 and 2015."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,13,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The inflation surge in 2021/2022 is accompanied by a strong increase in interest rates and is not detected as a de-anchored regime.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,14,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Furthermore, we introduce a quantitative approach to evaluate de-anchoring risks relative to a baseline or forecast."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,15,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Using stochastic simulations of the regime-switching model, we calculate a measure of these risks."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,16,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The real time exercise allows to assess the state-dependent risks of inflation de-anchoring.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,17,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","For the ELB episode in the euro area between 2015 and 2020 we find pronounced risks of de-anchoring, while for the post-covid, with a supply side driven inflation surge, the risks maintain contained, because of monetary policy moving towards an increasingly tighter stance."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,38,18,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The evolution of de-anchoring risks over time can give an indication for more forceful policy in times of increasing risks of de-anchoring. ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 37
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,0,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","In a counterfactual policy exercise, where the central bank follows a ’looking-through oil price driven inflation’ policy we show that the risks of de-anchoring increase."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,1,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","This increase is due to the central bank tolerating longer periods of headline inflation above target, if these deviations are driven by external factors."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,2,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",This result is in line with the theoretical results that a less forceful reaction to headline inflation leads to higher de-anchoring risks.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,3,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",The proposed method complements de-anchoring measures based on financial market data or surveys.
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,4,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Unlike those measures, the model-based approach provides a structural interpretation and enables the analysis of policy counterfactuals."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,5,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","Our methods also relate to the recent tendency in monetary policy to account more explicitly for uncertainty in the form of scenarios, as proposed in the Bernanke-report for the Bank of England."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,6,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.","When uncertainties around the baseline projection are illustrated by counterfactual scenarios, the calculation of de-anchoring risks around the baseline and the scenarios, can be a useful extension to inform monetary policy decisions."
ecb.wp3082~273898d46f.en.pdf,39,7,Managing the risks of inflation expectation de-anchoring,"Kai Christoffel, Mátyás Farkas",D:20250728111449+02'00',D:20250530195309Z,"This paper investigates the implications of a potential loss of credibility in the central bank’s ability to bring inflation back to target in the medium-term (”de-anchoring”). We propose a monetary policy framework in which the central bank accounts for de-anchoring risks using a regime-switching model. First, we derive the optimal monetary policy strategy, which balances the trade-off between the welfare costs of a stronger response to inflation and the benefits of preserving the central bank’s credibility. Next, we apply this framework in a medium-scale regime-switching DSGE model and develop a method to assess de-anchoring risks in real time. Using the post-COVID inflation episode in the euro area as a case study, we find that an explicit ”looking-through” strategy would have only modestly increased de-anchoring risks. These findings highlight the importance of monitoring de-anchoring risks in monetary policy design.",Future research could provide a more comprehensive evaluation of various policy alternatives with respect to the implied risks of a de-anchoring of inflation expectations. ECB Working Paper Series No 3082 38
