ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,2,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,2,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,2,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,2,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,2,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","JEL: I24, E32, J24 Keywords: Homeownership, Housing Wealth, College enrolment, Housing Boom-Bust Episodes. ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 1"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Non-technical summary For decades, college attendance in the U.S. kept increasing, reaching an all-time high in 2006 when 64% of those graduating from high school enroled in college."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","However, this trend was arrested in the wake of the housing bust of the late 2000s, with college enrolment stagnating and even declining."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",We hypothesis that the destruction of housing wealth during the bust played a role to play in the reversal of college attendance.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","In theory, a decline in house prices can affect college attendance via two channels."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","The first one is the opportunity cost channel, whereby when the economy is in a recession and housing markets are in decline, college becomes relatively more attractive."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","The second one is related to changes in housing wealth whereby during a housing bust, home equity declines sharply, raising the effective cost of sending one’s children to college."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","And while the former channel affects everyone, the latter affects homeowners but not renters."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,7,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",This makes it possible to identify the effect of the housing-leverage channel of fluctuations in house prices on college enrolment.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,8,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We use micro-census data on around 104,300 households from the American Community Survey, for which we observe both parental homeownership status and children’s college enrolment outcomes."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,9,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",We match these data with local indices on changes in house prices over time.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,10,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Our main finding is that during the housing bust of 2008-2011, and compared with those of renters, the college-age children of homeowners in areas that experienced a relatively larger decline in house prices were significantly less likely to be enroled in a higher education institution, as opposed to homeowners who experienced more modest declines in house prices."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,11,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","This effect is concentrated among homeowners with a mortgage, rather than among outright homeowners, strengthening the notion of the importance of credit constraints in determining college choice."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,12,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Moreover, the effect is stronger when the parents themselves did not go to college, suggesting that family education history can affect the elasticity of the likelihood of college enrolment to housing shocks."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,3,13,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We also find that the housing leverage effect on college enrolment is meaningful in the aggregate: at the height of the housing bust, up to 2% of the local college-age population ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 2"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,4,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",did not enrol in college due to housing frictions.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,4,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Importantly, it is observed long after the bust episode, and it translates into persistently lower income levels and lower likelihoods of working in an education-intensive industry for homeowners."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,4,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",This finding is consistent with other work that has recently documented long-lasting effects of the Great Recession.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,4,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","The returns to investment in human capital are high both individually and socially: the college premium in lifetime income is substantial, and a more educated workforce is associated with a more productive economy."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,4,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","However, the cost of college in the U.S. has been rising in recent decades, pointing to the central role that credit constraints play in college enrolment."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,4,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Our results shed new light on the long-lived adverse socioeconomic effects of housing busts and illuminates a potential trade-off between investment in real assets (housing) and investment in human capital (college education).
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,4,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Our evidence suggests that government policies aimed at reducing the cost of college should not be uniform, but should also take into account local housing price dynamics. ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 3"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",1 Introduction Do housing busts have long-term economic consequences?
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",We go to the heart of this question by studying the implications of the 2008-2011 U.S. housing boom on college enrolment.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","This question is important because while the college premium has steadily increased since the 1970s (e.g., Goldin and Katz, 2008; Athreya and Eberly, 2021), so has the price of higher education in real terms (e.g., Dynarski et al.,"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","2003), to the point where the rising cost of college has entered the debate about economic inequality and prompted government action.1 It is therefore natural to hypothesize that households’ ability to send their children to college is sensitive to shocks to their finances."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Indeed, Lovenheim (2011) and Lovenheim and Reynolds (2013) show that rising home equity during the housing boom of the early-to-mid 2000s was associated with an increase in college attendance by the children of homeowners, and especially for lower-income ones, pointing to the importance of financing constraints in education decisions."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",There is no reason to expect that the effect is not symmetric and therefore adverse shocks to home equity could significantly negatively affect college enrolment through a reduction in the housing wealth of families with college-age children.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","However, the empirical literature has not yet provided conclusive evidence to that end."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,7,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Figure 1 illustrates a significant slowdown in college attendance since the end of the housing boom.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,8,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","The percentage of 18- and 19-years-olds attending college increased between 1980 and 2006, from 44% to 64%."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,9,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","However, in the following decade this increase was not only arrested, but college attendance by that group even declined, to 63% in 2015."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,10,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Figure 2 further emphasizes the important role that the homeownership status of college-age individuals’ parents played in this development.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,11,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","While college attendance by homeowners’ children increased by more than that of renters’ children during the housing boom of the early-to-mid 2000s, attendance by renters’ children continued increasing after 2006, while that of homeowners’ children stagnated."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,5,12,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","To explain this phenomenon, we study whether decreased affordability of college be- 1For example, the Biden administration announced two rounds of student debt forgiveness between 2022 and 2024. ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 4"
