ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Using this approach, we find that up to 2% of the local college-age population, did not enrol in college during the peak years of the housing bust due to housing wealth destruction."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","To answer the second question, we compare owners and renters in the geographic localities more and less affected by the housing bust over the next decade."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We find that differences in college attendance persist beyond the housing bust, with owners still significantly less likely to be enroled in college as of 2019, one year before COVID-19 pandemic hitted."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Longer and more expensive college arrangements are affected more compared to shorter programs.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",This persistent decline in human capital accumulation translates into persistently lower income levels for homeowners compared to renters in localities more affected during the housing busts.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We show that this is not driven by different employment likelihood, but by a significantly lower likelihood of being employed in education-intensive industries in which the college premium tends to be reflected in higher wages."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,7,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Our work contributes to the literature on financial frictions and education.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,8,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Wealthy parents invest, on average, more in the human capital of their offspring than poorer ones (e.g., Becker et al.,"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,9,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Consequently, easier access to external finance increases college enrolment for credit-constrained households."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,10,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","A number of papers have demonstrated this link by looking at the effect of exogenous changes in the availability of student loans on human capital accumulation (e.g., Lochner and MongeNaranjo, 2011; Denning and Jones, 2021; Black et al.,"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,11,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Others have demonstrated a similar effect by looking at the effect of banking deregulation on college enrolment which increased the availability and reduced the cost of bank credit (Sun and Yannelis, 2016), or of unexpected positive wealth shocks as a result of lottery wins (Bulman et al.,"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,9,12,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Closest in spirit to our approach is the analysis in Lovenheim (2011) and Lovenheim and Reynolds (2013) who show that an increase in housing wealth increases significantly the likelihood of university enrolment, with the effect being strongest for lower-income ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 8"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,10,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Relative to this paper and to the rest of the empirical literature, we look at the effect of a negative wealth shock via the destruction of home equity."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,10,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Our paper also contributes to the literature on the socio-economic effects of fluctuations in house prices.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,10,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","One strand of this literature has linked the U.S. housing boom of the early-to-mid 2000s to household portfolio and labor choices, as well as to changes in the U.S. industrial structure."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,10,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Mian and Sufi (2011) provide evidence on how home equitybased borrowing during the U.S. housing boom of the late 1990s and early-to-mid 2000s was responsible for the large observed increase in housing debt among U.S. households.
