ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,36,13,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Standard errors are clustered at the PUMA × Year level. ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 35
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,37,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","8 Heterogeneity of college attendance responses to the housing bust To account for potential heterogeneity in difference between homeowners’ and renters’ responses to the housing bust, we estimate the following regression: J X j=1 θj · ∆2006,t−1 ln HPIp × Owneri,p,t,b × 1{Heteroi,p,t,b=j} Collegei,p,t,b = J X j=1 βj · Owneri,p,t,b × 1{Heteroi,p,t,b=j}+ + J X j=1 δj · ∆2006,t−1 ln HPIp × 1{Heteroi,p,t,b=j}+ + + γ′Xi,p,t,b + αb + αp,t + εi,p,t,b, (5) where 1{Heteroi,p,t,y=j} is a heterogeneity parameter: indicator variable taking value 1 if an observation on individual i falls into category j. In what follows we look at a number of such categories that can plausibly induce heterogeneity in the response of college enrolment to changes in house prices, such as homeownership tenure, parental education, as well as gender, race and ethnicity."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,37,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","8.1 Housing tenure heterogeneity We compare parents of different housing tenure, parents having a mortgage and renting parents."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,37,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We exclude outright homeowners because for them, there is no clear mapping between housing equity accumulation and housing tenure whereas it is the case for mortgagors."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,37,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We interact the key term of interest ∆2006,t−1 ln HPIp × Owneri,p,t,b with an indicator variable that a head of the family moved into the residence some years ago."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,37,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","In the ACS, housing tenure is defined in intervals, therefore we use housing tenure bins corresponding to the reported intervals in the ACS."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,37,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We combine recently moved households (i.e., those who moved in less than 1 year ago and those who moved in 1-2 years ago) and create a bin for those who moved in less than 2 years ago."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,37,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",We use those who moved in 30+ years ago as the baseline category and keep the other categories as reported in the ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 36
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Note: This figure reports − θj for each moved in bin from the following regression: Collegei,p,t,b = PJ j=1 θj ·∆2006,t−1 ln HPIp×Owneri,p,t,b×1{MOV EDINi,p,t,y=j}+PJ j=1 βj ·Owneri,p,t,b× 1MOV EDINi,p,t,y=j} + γ′Xi,p,t,b + αb + αp,t + εi,p,t,b, where 1{MOV EDINi,p,t,y=j} is parents’ income in a j-th bin of moving into the current residence."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Housing tenure variation of the college attendance response to the housing bust The estimates from this test are depicted in Figure 10.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","The evidence makes it clear that the largest effect, in terms of a decline in college attendance during the housing bust, is for the children of those parents who moved into their current residence not too recently and not too long ago."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","In the latter case, this is plausibly because for those who moved in more than 20 years ago, the mortgage has likely been close to paid off and so the decline in housing wealth is less dramatic."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","For the former group, it is possible that they are more likely to be optimistic and thus to perceive the decline in house prices as temporary."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",8.2 Parental education heterogeneity We consider the heterogeneity of children’s responses to the housing bust with respect to parental education.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","As before, our key object of interest is the difference between the responses of homeowners and renters."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,38,7,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","To gauge how this difference varies parental education, we interact the key term of interest ∆2006,t−1 ln HPIp × Owneri,p,t,b with an ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 37"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","indicator variable equal to one if the household head’s education falls in one of three categories: high school dropout, completed high school, and at least some college."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Note: This figure reports −θj for each moved in bin from the following regression: Collegei,p,t,b = PJ j=1 θj · ∆2006,t−1 ln HPIp × Owneri,p,t,b × 1{EDUCi,p,t,y=j} + PJ j=1 βj · Owneri,p,t,b × 1EDUCi,p,t,y=j} + γ′Xi,p,t,b + αb + αp,t + εi,p,t,b, where 1{EDUCi,p,t,y=j} is indicator variable of parental education falls into a j-th bin of education."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Parental education and college attendance response to the housing bust The estimation results are depicted in Figure 11.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,3,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",The evidence shows that the overall decline in college attendance comes from the children of parents who have completed high school but have not been to college.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","While establishing the exact mechanism is not possible with our data, it is plausible that those without college education know that their labor income becomes more uncertain during a recession, or that they do not value college education as much."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","8.3 Race, ethnicity, and gender heterogeneity Next, we estimate Equation (5) after including interactions of the main variable of interest with dummy variables capturing gender, race and ethnicity."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",The estimation results are presented in Table 9.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,39,7,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","We find little or no evidence that these variables affect the sensitivity of homeowners to the housing bust, in terms of college attendance. All interaction terms ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 38"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,40,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","of ∆2006,t−1 ln HPI × Owner and gender and ethnic group indicator variables are insignificant except the one that captures Hispanic origin."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,40,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","In particular, Hispanic homeowners turn out to be less sensitive to the housing bust than non-Hispanic ones, as illustrated by the negative and significant coefficient at the interaction term ∆2006,t−1 ln HPI × Owner × Hispanic."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,40,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Once again, the exact mechanism is unclear, but one potential explanation may be that both the college enrolment rate and housing value of Hispanics are lower, leading to a lower sensitivity of college enrolment to the negative housing shock."
