ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,4,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","However, we document persistent differences in employment when we look at education-intensive sectors.24 As late as 2017, homeowners living in areas that experienced the largest decline in house prices during the bust were up to 0.15 p.p."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,5,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",less likely to work in an educationintensive sector.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",This reduction in employability in high-human-capital industries – which tend to be characterized by higher wages – result in persistent differences in per-capita real family incomes between homeowners and renters in more affected localities (see Figure 18).
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,7,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Even a decade after the housing bust, family incomes of homeowners are 0.35 p.p."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,8,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",lower for every 1 p.p.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,9,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",housing price decline over 2006-2011.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,10,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","The combined evidence thus suggests that in affected regions, homeowners experience a persistent loss of human capital."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,11,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Rather than simply postponing college enrolment at 24We define these as the sectors in which over half of the respondents in the ACS who self-identify as working in them have at least some college education.
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,45,12,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","For a similar strategy, see Ciccone and Papaioannou (2009). ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 44"
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,46,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","the trough of the housing cycle and catching up later, they are less likely to have any college education as late as a decade after the bust."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,46,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","And while they not more likely to be unemployed in the years after the housing bust, they appear to be switching to more low-skill jobs and as a result, to experience a reduction in relative real income."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,46,2,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Note: The picture presents differences in employment probability between homeowners and renters in response to a 1 p.p.
