ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,60,6,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","denote an estimate is significant at the 1%, 5%, and 10% levels, respectively."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,60,7,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Standard errors are clustered at the PUMA × Year level. ECB Working Paper Series No 3083 59
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,61,0,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.","Acknowledgements We thank Matteo Benetton, Tobias Berg, Reint Gropp, Alessandro Gavazza, Ethan Ilzetzki, Stepan Jurajda, Marek Kapicka, Alexander Monge-Naranjo, David Martinez-Miera, Steven Ongena, Sebastian Ottinger, Ctirad Slavik and seminar participants at the ECB, IWH Halle, CERGE-EI, NEOMA Business School, Bank of Spain, and TBS Finance and Economics Lab members for their valuable comments and suggestions."
ecb.wp3083~c8f18b8382.en.pdf,61,1,Back to school when times are bad? The role of housing wealth,"Anna Pestova, Alexander Popov",D:20250729093058+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"College enrolment typically rises during recessions. This paper demonstrates that housing wealth destruction dampened this countercyclical effect in areas most affected by the U.S. housing bust of 2008-2011. By combining household data with a mortgage credit register and housing price data, we reveal that negative shocks to housing wealth significantly reduced college enrolment among homeowners relative to renters during this period. Up to 2% of the local college-age population did not pursue college enrolment at the height of the bust due to housing wealth destruction. The negative impact of homeownership on college education persists for a decade, contributing to persistently lower incomes among homeowners in the most affected areas.",Part of this study was conducted while Anna was a visiting Ph.D. student at Princeton University.
