ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,11,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","2023), we compute a dummy variable and Climate Lawsuits, denoted as 1 if the firm is exposed to a climate lawsuit, and 0 otherwise."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,12,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",This climate lawsuits measure has a mean value of 0.037.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,13,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Thus, 3.70% of the firm-year observations in our sample face climate lawsuits (Table 2)."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,14,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","The final dataset contains 5,264 individual bank loans issued to 329 unique worldwide firms from 2006 to 2021."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,15,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","3.4 Empirical design We empirically examine the impact of climate-related lawsuits on loan spreads using a panel fixed effects regression model, which addresses correlated omitted variable bias (Wooldridge, 2010): Log(Spread)s,i,b,j,t = α0 + β Climate Lawsuitsi,t−1 + γ Loan Controlss,t−1+ + ψ Governance Controlsi,t−1 + δ Firm Controlsi,t−1+ + ϕi + γt + θj + ωs + δb + εs,i,b,j,t (1a) The unit of observation is loan facility."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,16,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","The dependent variable, Log(Spread), is the natural logarithm of the loan spread, which is the all-in-drawn spread over the London Interbank Offered Rate (LIBOR) of the syndicated loan s for borrowing firm i operating in country b supplied by 8Lawsuit filings and court decisions are typically unforeseen events."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,17,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Information regarding legal actions generally becomes publicly available only upon their official announcement, often through a press release or press conference on the date of filing marking the first public disclosure of the claim."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,18,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Even in cases where investigations are ongoing and publicly acknowledged, the precise timing of a filing is rarely disclosed in advance."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,11,19,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Likewise, the dates of court rulings are rarely announced in advance, and the substance of judicial decisions remains unknown until formally issued ECB Working Paper Series No 3087 10"
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,12,0,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","the lead bank of the syndication j, at time (year) t. Climate Lawsuits is our main variable of interest, the firm-level climate litigation profile, measured by a dummy variable that is denoted as 1 if the firm is exposed to a climate lawsuit and 0 otherwise."
