ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,0,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",Supplementary information Case study 1.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,1,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",v. Royal Dutch Shell plc A distinctive aspect of the Milieudefensie et al.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,2,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",v. Royal Dutch Shell plc case was the unprecedented level of transparency and public involvement that characterized the period leading up to its formal filing.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,3,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","In April 2018, a full year prior to the formal initiation of legal proceedings, Milieudefensie (Friends of the Earth Netherlands) issued an open letter to Mr. Ben van Beurden, Chief Executive Officer of Royal Dutch Shell plc."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,4,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Published online16, the letter articulated the organization’s view that Shell was in violation of its legal duty of care by failing to align its corporate strategy and investment decisions with the climate objectives set forth in the Paris Agreement."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,5,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Milieudefensie contended that Shell’s ongoing contribution to global greenhouse gas emissions amounted to an unlawful act, and it urged the company to take corrective measures."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,6,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","In the aftermath of the letter, the organization launched a nationwide public campaign aimed at mobilizing citizen support."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,7,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",Dutch residents were invited to endorse the initiative by signing a petition and registering as co-plaintiffs in the prospective legal action.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,8,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","By May 2018, the campaign had garnered the support of over 10,000 individuals."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,9,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","In February 2019, six additional organizations - ActionAid Netherlands, Both ENDS, Fossielvrij NL, Greenpeace Netherlands, Wadden Sea Forum, and Jongeren Milieu Actief-formally joined Milieudefensie in issuing a second letter to Royal Dutch Shell."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,10,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","This correspondence reiterated their initial demands and explicitly warned that, in the absence of substantive corrective measures, legal proceedings would be initiated on April 5, 2019."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,11,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","By the end of March, more than 17,000 individuals had registered their intent to join the action as co-plaintiffs."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,12,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","As announced, the plaintiffs proceeded to file the lawsuit before the District Court of The Hague on the specified date."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,13,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",Shell was granted a period of three months to submit its initial response to the claim.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,14,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Following subsequent procedural developments, the District Court scheduled four days of oral hearings, which took place in December 2020."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,15,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Unusually, the Court pre-announced the date of its judgment, indicating that a decision would be rendered on May 26, 2021."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,16,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","As scheduled, the judgment was delivered and livestreamed, garnering significant attention from both national and international media outlets."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,17,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","On July 20, 2021, Shell publicly confirmed its intention to appeal the decision, and the formal notice of appeal was filed on August 23."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,18,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",It is expected that the appellate proceedings will take approximately two to three years before a final judgment is rendered17.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,19,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","On April 25, 2022, Milieudefensie issued a formal letter to Shell’s Board of Directors urging immediate compliance with the May 26, 2021, court ruling and cautioning that failure to act could expose board members to personal liability risks toward third parties."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,20,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Finally, on November 12, 2024, the Court of Appeal in The Hague issued its decision in the Shell climate case, affirming that Shell has a legal duty of care to mitigate dangerous climate change under Dutch tort law, interpreted considering international human rights and climate law."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,21,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","While the Court upheld the broader legal framework established by the lower court, it overturned the specific requirement that Shell reduce its emissions by 45% by 2030 relative to 2019 levels."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,22,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",The Appeals Court concluded that there is insufficient scientific and legal consensus to impose a fixed emissions reduction target on an individual company.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,23,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Although the Court acknowledged that Shell’s duty of care extends to Scope 1, 2, and 3 emissions, it emphasized legal and practical challenges - particularly regarding Scope 3 - in setting enforceable reduction obligations."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,24,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",The Court also noted that Shell is already pursuing its own voluntary target of a 45% reduction by 2035 compared to 2016.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,25,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","While the Court expressed concern that future fossil fuel investments could conflict with Shell’s duty of care, it found that such issues were not central to the present case and declined to impose binding emissions targets18."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,51,26,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",16https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-05-25/court-decision-to-test-shell-s-responsibility-for-climatechange 17See: https://www.foei.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/MilieudefensielegalletterShell4 −April −2018.pdf 18For more details see: https://climatecasechart.com/non-us-case/milieudefensie-et-al-v-royal-dutch-shell-plc ECB Working Paper Series No 3087 50
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,0,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","New York Attorney General Issued Subpoena to Exxon Mobil Regarding Climate Disclosures In a notable legal confrontation involving state attorneys general and Exxon Mobil Corporation, the Attorneys General Offices of New York and Massachusetts initiated securities lawsuits against the corporation, following public investigations that commenced on November 4, 2015, as reported by the Bloomberg on the subsequent day19."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,1,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","On October 24, 2018, the New York Attorney General formally filed a highly anticipated lawsuit."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,2,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","The legal action alleged that Exxon Mobil had misled investors and regulatory bodies by publicly downplaying the severity of climate change, despite internal advisories from the company’s own scientists indicating otherwise."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,3,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Although there was considerable anticipation surrounding this potential lawsuit, the exact filing date remained uncertain until its occurrence."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,4,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","The case proceeded to trial in October 2019, culminating in a 12-day judicial examination."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,5,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Ultimately, on October 12, 2019, the court determined that the New York Attorney General’s Office failed to substantiate claims that Exxon Mobil had misled investors regarding its methodologies and procedures for evaluating climate risk."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,6,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","During the New York trial, the Massachusetts Attorney General initiated another securities suit against Exxon Mobil on October 24, 2019."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,7,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","This legal action was preceded by an investigation launched by Attorney General Maura Healey on April 19, 2016."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,8,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",The Massachusetts lawsuit contends that Exxon Mobil engaged in deceptive practices that adversely affected Massachusetts investors and consumers.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,9,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Specifically, the company is accused of failing to adequately disclose climate change risks, misrepresenting its business practices concerning the use of proxy carbon costs, misleadingly advertising its products, neglecting to disclose the environmental impacts of its products on climate change, and executing greenwashing campaigns."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,10,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.",Exxon Mobil’s attempt to shift he case from state court to federal jurisdiction was thwarted by a federal district court in Boston.
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,11,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","In contrast to the New York litigation, the Massachusetts lawsuit remains pending in state court20."
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,52,12,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","19See:https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-11-05/exxon-mobil-said-to-be-probed-by-n-y-overclimate-change 20For more details, see:https://climatecasechart.com/case/people-v-exxon-mobil-corporation/ ECB Working Paper Series No 3087 51"
ecb.wp3087~24c9711946.en.pdf,53,0,The impact of climate litigation risk on firms’ cost of bank loans,"Andreas Beyer, Lorenzo Nobile",D:20250805083841+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"Using a novel worldwide dataset of 5,264 syndicated loans issued to 329 firms from 2006 to 2021, we study how climate-related litigation risk affects firm’s cost of borrowing. We find robust empirical evidence that firms targeted by climate lawsuits pay significantly higher spreads on their bank loans. These effects are more pronounced for firms with weaker environmental performance and higher ESG controversies. The results suggest that lender’s view climate litigation as a material risk factor, which is increasingly priced into debt contracts.","Acknowledgements We are grateful for comments from participants of the ECB Banking Supervision Research Seminar Series and by Klaus Düllmann, Chryssa Papathanassiou, Spyros Palligkinis, and an anonymous referee."
