raised their deposit rates by less - a pattern we interpret as banks leveraging a more valuable deposit franchise to fund longer-duration assets. Although euro-area banks as a whole avoided widespread runs, several institutions nonetheless carried substantial mark-to-market losses, suggesting latent fragilities.","Traditionally, this maturity mismatch is thought to expose banks to IRR, whereby rising interest rates decrease bank net worth as the present value of assets falls more than that of liabilities."
