ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,1,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Working Paper Series Collateral and credit Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao No 3095 Disclaimer: This paper should not be reported as representing the views of the European Central Bank (ECB)."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,1,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The views expressed are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect those of the ECB.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,2,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Challenges for Monetary Policy Transmission in a Changing World Network (ChaMP) This paper contains research conducted within the network “Challenges for Monetary Policy Transmission in a Changing World Network” (ChaMP).
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,2,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",It consists of economists from the European Central Bank (ECB) and the national central banks (NCBs) of the European System of Central Banks (ESCB).
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,2,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","ChaMP is coordinated by a team chaired by Philipp Hartmann (ECB), and consisting of Diana Bonfim (Banco de Portugal), Margherita Bottero (Banca d’Italia), Emmanuel Dhyne (Nationale Bank van België/Banque Nationale de Belgique) and Maria T. Valderrama (Oesterreichische Nationalbank), who are supported by Melina Papoutsi and Gonzalo Paz-Pardo (both ECB), 7 central bank advisers and 8 academic consultants."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,2,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","ChaMP seeks to revisit our knowledge of monetary transmission channels in the euro area in the context of unprecedented shocks, multiple ongoing structural changes and the extension of the monetary policy toolkit over the last decade and a half as well as the recent steep inflation wave and its reversal."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,2,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",More information is provided on its website. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 1
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Abstract This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, AnaCredit."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Real estate and ﬁnancial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","For the ﬁrst time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","By exploiting within-ﬁrm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we ﬁnd that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7´0.8."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, ﬁnancial, and macro conditions."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,3,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","JEL classiﬁcation: E32, G21, G33 Keywords: Collateral channel, Corporate ﬁnancing, Secured debt, Bank credit ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 2"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Non-Technical Summary Collateral plays a crucial role in bank lending.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",It reduces risk by giving banks the legal right to seize and sell a borrower’s assets if the borrower fails to repay.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Firms’ assets, like land and buildings, both facilitate production and serve as security for loans."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",This makes collateral a cornerstone of corporate ﬁnance and a powerful ampliﬁer of macroeconomic cycles.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","When asset values drop, ﬁrms borrow less, which can worsen economic slowdowns."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Yet, recent evidence from the US suggests a waning reliance on secured debt among large corporations, leaving unclear whether collateral still matters for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) operating within bank-based ﬁnancial systems."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In this paper, we aim to answer two questions: ﬁrst, to what extent does the value of pledged collateral inﬂuence loan pricing and volumes?"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","And second, how important is collateral in SME lending?"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","To answer these questions, we leverage AnaCredit, the euro-area corporate credit register that uniquely links each loan to its full set of pledged assets and records the actual value of every pledged asset."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,9,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Exploiting within-ﬁrm, within-bank, and over-time variation, we isolate the impact of collateral presence, type, and value on credit outcomes."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,10,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Our results suggest that roughly 70% of outstanding volume is collateralized, and these loans enjoy 33%-48% larger committed amounts and 10-18 basis points (bps) lower interest rates than comparable uncollateralized loans."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,11,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The type of collateral matters: ﬁnancial assets are most frequently pledged (46%) but real estate dominates total collateral value (53%).
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,12,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Real estate boosts loan quantity rather than price, while intangibles deliver both modest amount increases and lower rates."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,13,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","For the ﬁrst time, we show that a 1% increase in collateral value translates into a 2-4 bp reduction in loan rates and a 0.7-0.8 elasticity in committed amounts."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,14,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Cross-country and over-time analyses reveal that elasticities of loan amounts to collateral value vary, ranging from 64% to 89% across countries and 76% to 86% over years, reﬂecting diﬀerences in collateral composition, legal frameworks, and loan-to-value norms."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,15,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Taken together, our ﬁndings conﬁrm that collateral remains a critical determinant of SME credit outcomes."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,4,16,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Models linking asset values to borrowing should continue to incorporate collateral channels, and policymakers designing targeted guarantees or balance-sheet interventions can meaningfully inﬂuence both the cost and volume of credit to SMEs. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 3"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",I Introduction Collateral plays a central role in corporate ﬁnancing and is a key driver of macroeconomic ﬂuctuations.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In macro-ﬁnance models (e.g., Bernanke, 1983, 1999; Kiyotaki and Moore, 1997), ﬁrm assets, such as land or buildings, serve dual functions: as productive inputs and as collateral to support borrowing."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Negative shocks to asset values reduce borrowing capacity, triggering feedback loops that amplify downturns and business cycle ﬂuctuations."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","However, recent empirical studies, mainly in the US, cast doubt on the relevance of collateral in current environments."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","2024) and Lian and Ma (2021) document a declining reliance on secured debt, suggesting a diminished role for collateral."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","These ﬁndings are drawn from large US ﬁrms and may not generalize to small and mediumsized enterprises (SMEs) or bank-based ﬁnancial systems, where ﬁrms remain highly reliant on bank credit and more exposed to collateral constraints."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Understanding whether, how, and to what extent collateral still matters is thus critical for both corporate ﬁnance and macroeconomic ﬂuctuations."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In this paper, we ask two questions: (1) What is the impact of collateral value on credit conditions? ("
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",2) How important is collateral in bank-based ﬁnancial systems with signiﬁcant SME lending?
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,9,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Answering these questions has been hindered by three main challenges.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,10,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","First, granular data on the role of collateral in SME ﬁnancing is rarely available, despite SMEs’ importance and sensitivity to collateral requirements."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,11,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Second, few datasets provide the full composition of collateral types pledged."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,12,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Most datasets capture only the primary collateral, limiting our understanding of collateral heterogeneity."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,13,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Third, empirical work lacks information on the pledged value of collateral."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,14,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Collateral value is typically estimated indirectly by researchers using ﬁrm accounting data in the previous literature, rather than measured using direct and actual bank-reported values of collateral at the individual asset level.1 We overcome the three aforementioned challenges by using AnaCredit, the standardized corporate credit register for the euro area, and show that collateral plays a crucial role in shaping debt ﬁnancing."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,15,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","AnaCredit contains granular information on both loan and collateral attributes, with reporting thresholds as low as €25,000 per ﬁrm-bank relationship."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,16,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Crucially, it oﬀers a complete mapping between loans and collateral, along with the bank-reported value of each individual collateral item."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,17,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","To our knowledge, this is the ﬁrst study of the collateral channel based on actual 1Many studies rely on the price of a single asset class, such as the estimated market value of real estate."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,18,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",This value can be further instrumented by land supply elasticities.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,19,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The assumption is that real estate constitutes the majority of ﬁrm assets and is the collateral type most frequently used.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,20,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Consequently, changes in this price are assumed to directly reﬂect collateral constraints faced by ﬁrms (see, e.g., Chaney et al.,"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,5,21,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",2022). ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 4
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",collateral values rather than asset price indices.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","We approach the question, ""How important is collateral in bank-based ﬁnancial systems involving small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs)?"""
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","First, we document that collateral is widespread and materially aﬀects loan terms."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Over half of all loans are collateralized, and these account for roughly 70% of aggregate outstanding nominal amounts (ONA)."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Empirically, we ﬁnd that secured loans are associated with larger credit volumes and modestly lower borrowing costs."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Controlling for detailed ﬁrm, bank, and time-varying characteristics through high-dimensional ﬁxed eﬀects, we estimate that annualized interest rates on secured loans are 10 to 18 basis points lower than on comparable unsecured loans."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In terms of volume, pledged collateral is associated with 33% to 48% higher committed amounts, holding all else equal."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","These ﬁndings suggest that collateral alleviates lender risk and improved credit terms, even after controlling for various time-varying ﬁrm and bank characteristics, and non-random ﬁrm-bank matching."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Second, the impact of collateral depends critically on its type and features."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,9,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","The former refers to the asset category (e.g., real estate, ﬁnancial assets), while the latter captures economic properties such as tangibility and redeployability that aﬀect an assets suitability as collateral."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,10,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Our ﬁndings indicate that loans backed by real estate are associated with signiﬁcantly higher credit volumes, but do not consistently exhibit lower interest rates, which is consistent with the collateral channel mechanism described in Chaney et al. ("
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,11,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","By contrast, other assets, including intangibles, are associated with both larger loan amounts and lower interest rates."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,12,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Regarding pledged asset features, we ﬁnd that immovable collateral is associated with higher credit volumes, consistent with theoretical predictions in Hart and Moore (1994), whereas other features such as liquidity and redeployability show no relationship with price or quantity of credit."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,13,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","To assess the aggregate implications of this result, it is thus important to understand the prevalence of each of these collateral types."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,14,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","We show that ﬁnancial assets constitute the most frequently pledged collateral category, accounting for 46% of all protection contracts, followed by real estate at 26%."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,15,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In value terms, however, real estate dominates: it secures 53% of the total value of outstanding collateralized loans, compared to 34% for ﬁnancial assets."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,16,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Other asset types, including intangibles and physical movable assets play a marginal role, accounting for 7% and 5.5%, respectively."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,6,17,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","While the ﬁrst question concerns the extensive margin of collateral use, ""What is the impact of collateral value on credit conditions?"""
