ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,20,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","2022; Luck and Santos, 2023; Barbiero et al.,"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,21,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The type and characteristics of collateral also matter.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,22,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Theoretically, Hart and Moore (1994) show that durable assets increase borrowing capacity."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,23,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Eisfeldt and Rampini (2009) emphasize the role of low repossession costs, while Shleifer and Vishny (1992) highlight asset liquidity as a key determinant of debt capacity."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,24,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",We advance this literature along several dimensions.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,25,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","First, we incorporate three aspects of collateral, presence, type, and value, into our empirical framework which allows for a comprehensive assessment of collateral’s role in loan pricing and volumes."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,26,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Second, we provide novel evidence on how diﬀerent collateral types and features aﬀect ﬁrm borrowing conditions by focusing on asset tangibility, liquidity, and redeployability."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,9,27,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Third, we are the ﬁrst to quantify the collateral channel ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 8"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","using actual, asset-level collateral value, rather than relying on proxies such as real estate price indices.2 Finally, the scope of AnaCredit enables us to explore cross-country variation in the relevance of collateral."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","While we do not formally identify the sources of this heterogeneity, our results yield insights that can inform a wide range of policy discussions on credit access and ﬁnancial stability in bank-based ﬁnancial systems."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Third, our work is related to the literature on the consequences of collateralization."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","As a central enforcement mechanism, collateralization constitutes a microfoundation of ﬁnancial frictions in macro-ﬁnance."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","A large body of research highlights the importance of collateral values in shaping ﬁrm-level borrowing constraints and amplifying macroeconomic ﬂuctuations (Bernanke, 1983; Bernanke and Gertler, 1986; Kiyotaki and Moore, 1997; Chaney et al.,"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",A complementary strand of the literature explores potential misallocation introduced by collateralization.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","For instance, Bleck and Liu (2018) demonstrate how collateralization can distort credit allocation under liquidity injections."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","2024) link the collateral channel to capital misallocation, while Donaldson et al. ("
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",2021) argue that collateral requirements can inhibit asset reallocation by preventing ﬁrms from divesting pledged assets.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,9,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","A common feature of these studies is that they take the existence of collateralization as given, without examining the underlying process through which it occurs.3 We address this gap by documenting where collateral is allocated and how collateralization is achieved."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,10,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","First, we investigate how collateral is allocated across loans within a ﬁrm, thereby shedding light on whether and how assets are eﬀectively deployed to secure credit."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,11,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",This speaks directly to the problem of collateral allocation.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,12,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Second, we study the structure of collateralization itself, i.e.,how speciﬁc assets are linked to individual loans."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,13,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Our ﬁndings suggest that these mechanisms inﬂuence both the interpretation of collateral value and the terms of debt contracting.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,14,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In doing so, we provide new micro-level evidence on how the process of collateralization shapes credit outcomes."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,15,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","2The magnitude of the collateral channel in the US is usually identiﬁed through changes in real estate value on debt issuance, where the collateral value is its market value estimated by researchers."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,16,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","The impact of a $1 increase in real estate value on ﬁrm debt issuance, including secured and unsecured loans and bonds, varies from 9-10 cents in Chaney et al. ("
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,17,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",2012) to 30 cents in Campello et al. (
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,18,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","These estimates are identiﬁed using mostly publicly listed ﬁrms, who are in need for loans and willing to increase their debt as well as having suﬃcient resources at hand."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,19,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The core limitation of these studies is that there is no information on whether ﬁrms actually pledge real estate or not.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,20,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",2021) overcome this limitation by using FRY-14Q data that includes the actual pledge of real estate collateral by ﬁrms.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,21,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","With the sample covering both publicly listed and large private ﬁrms that have loans from 32 large banks, they report that a $1 increase in real estate value leads to an increase of 7-12 basis points in loan growth."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,22,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Based on our sample, the point estimate of the collateral channel is around 75 cents."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,23,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",It is estimated using a sample of SMEs that take new bank loans.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,24,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In addition, we use the actual value of individual collateral that consists of several asset types but is not limited to real estate."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,25,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",3We do not take a position on whether collateralization is endogenously or exogenously determined within a model.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,26,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Instead, our focus is on the mechanics of the collateralization process, which remains underexplored."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,27,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","While ""collateral refers to the asset serving as security for repayment, ""collateralization describes the act of assigning those assets to speciﬁc credit contracts."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,28,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",This distinction matters for understanding ﬁrm behavior and lender constraints.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,10,29,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","For instance, a ﬁrm owning two buildings and seeking both a term loan and a credit line must decide how to allocate these assets to secure each loan, highlighting the need to analyze the matching between assets and liabilities. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 9"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The remainder of this paper is structured as follows.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Section II describes the data and sample.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Section III documents facts on collateral in the euro area.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","IV empirically studies the role of collateral in credit conditions, including loan prices and quantities."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","II Data and Sample A Data Source The core dataset for our analysis is AnaCredit, the harmonized credit registry of the euro area."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","It provides conﬁdential, loan-level data on credit granted by euro area banks to enterprises, along with detailed information on collateral.4 The ﬁrst part of the paper (Section III) examines the prevalence and distribution of collateralization in the euro area, based on a cross-sectional snapshot from December 2019."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","To assess the robustness of our ﬁndings, we also report stylized facts in December 2021 and December 2023 (in Tables C3, C4, and C5).5 The second part of the paper (Section IV) examines the role of collateral for loan characteristics at loan origination."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",That analysis is based on the universe of newly originated loans during 2019-2023.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","The sample covers 19 euro area countries: Austria, Belgium, Cyprus, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Greece, Ireland, Italy, Latvia, Lithuania, Luxembourg, Malta, the Netherlands, Portugal, Slovakia, Slovenia, and Spain."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,11,9,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","B Data Structure Before describing the sample construction, it is important to highlight how diﬀerences in data structure and information availability shape our understanding of collateral."
