ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In columns (1) and (2), the outcome is the interest rate."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","The coeﬃcients on real estate are positive and signiﬁcant, suggesting it even increase loan rates relative to unsecured loans."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In contrast, the other three types yield negative, signiﬁcant coeﬃcients."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Financial assets lower rates by about 20 basis points when ILT ﬁxed eﬀects are included.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Columns (3) and (4) use committed amounts as outcome variables.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",All collateral types are positively and signiﬁcantly associated with loan size.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Real estate shows the largest eﬀects, potentially oﬀsetting its lack of a pricing beneﬁt."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,9,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","These ﬁndings conﬁrm that collateral types matter for credit terms, a distinction that would be missed without knowing the asset type."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,10,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","We repeat the analysis by year and country, with results in Tables C10 to C13."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,11,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Not surprisingly, the pricing eﬀects have substantial heterogeneity but still show some general patterns."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,12,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In Italy and France, loans backed by real estate sometimes carry higher interest rates than unsecured loans or those backed by other collateral types."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,13,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","This suggests that the pricing impact of collateral is highly context-dependent, varying by both collateral type and its temporal or geographic use."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,14,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The results are more consistent when focusing on loan quantities.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,15,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Real estate is associated with larger loan amounts across years and in France, Italy, and Spain."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,16,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","In Germany and the pooled group, ﬁnancial assets yield the largest eﬀect, particularly under ﬁrmtime ﬁxed eﬀects."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,17,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Our results caution against using real estate as a general proxy for collateral.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,18,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Doing so ignores other relevant types and overstates real estates role in reducing borrowing costs since its main beneﬁt appears to be in boosting loan volumes rather than lowering rates.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,19,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","B.2 The Role of Collateral Features Beyond examining collateral types, we also investigate the underlying economic features of collateral assets that inﬂuence their eﬀectiveness in debt contracts."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,20,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Speciﬁcally, we classify collateral according to three key attributes: immovability, liquidity, and redeployability, which are shown to drive the role of collateral."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,21,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Appendix Table A1 outlines how each of the fourteen collateral categories reported in AnaCredit maps to these features.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,26,22,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Our choice is motivated by the previous literature.
