ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,28,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",We ﬁnd a strong negative relationship between collateral value and interest rates.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,28,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","As the collateral value is in logs, the coeﬃcient can be interpreted as a semi-elasticity: A 1% increase in collateral value reduces loan rates by 2.4-4.3 basis points."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,28,8,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","At sample means, this equates to a 0.42 basis point rate reduction for a €10,000 increase in collateral value."
