ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,42,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel (a) shows the distribution based on the most recent collateral valuations from the collateral-level sample, with each bar segment representing the share of a given collateral type in the total collateral value for that country."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,42,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel (b) presents the distribution based on allocated collateral values from the loan-collateral sample, which accounts for multiple loan pledges and adjustments for enforceability."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,42,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Collateral categories are deﬁned according to the AnaCredit classiﬁcation and are described in the main text. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 41
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,43,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Table 1: Summary Statistics for Stylized Facts Sample Variable Mean p25 p50 p75 SD N Panel A: Loan sample ONA (million €) 0.111 0.001 0.016 0.061 0.384 16,390,989 Commit amount (million €) 0.215 0.010 0.040 0.135 0.682 14,393,339 Interest rate 2.947 1.000 1.943 3.608 3.532 13,825,756 Secured 0.530 0.000 1.000 1.000 0.499 16,448,152 #Collateral per loan 1.119 0.000 1.000 1.000 1.927 16,448,152 #Collateral per secured loan 2.112 1.000 1.000 2.000 2.216 8,713,550 Panel B: Collateral sample Original value (million €) 0.252 0.016 0.060 0.187 0.761 9,236,569 Latest value (million €) 0.329 0.013 0.059 0.206 1.040 11,950,006 #Loans per collateral 1.474 1.000 1.000 1.000 1.804 12,036,072 Panel C: Loan-collateral sample Allocated value (million €) 0.094 0.000 0.004 0.053 0.317 19,567,847 Allocated loan ONA (million €) 0.047 0.000 0.001 0.023 0.178 19,562,609 Notes: This table reports descriptive statistics for the three levels of data used in the analysis."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,43,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel A presents summary statistics at the loan level, including measures of loan size, pricing, maturity, and collateralization."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,43,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel B provides statistics at the collateral level, including the original and most recent valuations of collateral as reported by banks, as well as the number of loans secured by each collateral item."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,43,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel C reports statistics at the loan-collateral level, capturing the allocated value of collateral linked to a speciﬁc loan and the corresponding outstanding nominal amount (ONA)."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,43,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",All continuous variables are winsorized at the 0.5th and 99.5th percentiles to mitigate the inﬂuence of outliers. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 42
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,44,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Table 2: Collateral Presence Panel A: Loan frequency Share in total loans 53% Loan types Credit lines Finance leases Term loans Revolving credit Trade receivables 60.5% 61.4% 63.9% 35% 43% Panel B: Loan ONA Share in total ONA 70% Loan types Credit lines Finance leases Term loans Revolving credit Trade receivables 75.6% 71.1% 74% 57.6% 31.2% Notes: This table reports summary statistics on the presence of collateral across corporate loans.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,44,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel A shows the share of secured loans as a percentage of total loan counts, overall and by loan type."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,44,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel B reports the share of the outstanding nominal amount (ONA) of secured loans relative to the total ONA, again disaggregated by loan type."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,44,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Loan types include credit lines, ﬁnance leases, term loans, revolving credit, and trade receivables. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 43"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,45,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Table 3: Collateral Types Composition Frequency Loan volume Real estate 26.32% 52.80% Physical movable assets 10.54% 5.53% Financial assets 46.74% 34.63% Other assets 16.39% 7.04% Total 100% 100% Notes: This table summarizes the composition of collateral types in the collateral sample.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,45,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Real estate includes residential and commercial real estate collateral, such as oﬃces and commercial premises."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,45,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Physical movable assets comprise other physical collateral (e.g., machinery or equipment)."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,45,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Financial assets encompass currency and deposits, securities, loans, equity and fund shares, credit derivatives, ﬁnancial guarantees (excluding credit derivatives), life insurance policies, trade receivables, and gold."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,45,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Other assets include all remaining types, primarily intangible or non- ﬁnancial assets such as intellectual property."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,45,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Column (1) reports the frequency distribution of each collateral type across secured loans.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,45,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Column (2) shows the share of total outstanding nominal amounts (ONA) of secured loans backed by each collateral type. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 44
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Table 4: Collateral Value and Composition Panel A: Latest collateral value Aggregate collateral value Collateral value Secured loan ONA Value 3933.61 925.92 Share 424.83% Value composition Real estate Physical movable assets Other assets Financial assets Value 2348.94 212.82 256.59 1115.26 Share 59.71% 5.41% 6.52% 28.35% Panel B: Allocated collateral value Aggregate collateral value Collateral Secured loan ONA Value 1837.16 925.92 Share 198.41% Value composition Real estate Physical movable assets Other assets Financial assets Value 903.61 75.74 141.93 715.88 Share 49.19% 4.12% 7.73% 38.97% Notes: This table reports the aggregate collateral value and its composition across collateral types.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel A uses the collateral-level sample and reports the total value of all individual collateral items (""Collateral value"") and the total outstanding nominal amount (ONA) of the secured loans they back."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The share represents the ratio of total collateral value to secured loan ONA.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The composition breakdown reports the value and share of each collateral type relative to the total collateral value.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Panel B uses the loancollateral-level sample and reports “Allocated collateral value”, which reﬂects the value attributed to a speciﬁc loan-collateral pair as assessed by the reporting bank."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",The aggregate allocated collateral value and its associated secured loan ONA are calculated by summing the values across all loan-collateral pairs.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,6,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Shares are computed by dividing the collateral value of each type by the total allocated collateral value.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,46,7,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",All values are expressed in billion EUR. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 45
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,47,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Table 5: Summary Statistics for Regression Sample Variable Mean p25 p50 p75 SD N Interest rate 2.985 1.239 2.273 4.32 2.231 6,088,099 PD 0.041 0.005 0.013 0.032 0.102 6,088,709 Secured 0.454 0 0 1 0.498 6,088,709 Commit amount (million €) 0.227 0.013 0.031 0.102 0.82 6,088,709 Maturity (days) 840.727 91 355 1277 1199.681 6,088,709 Collateral value 0.284 0.009 0.025 0.088 10.083 2,762,553 Notes: This table presents summary statistics for the regression sample, which includes monthly new loans issued to nonﬁnancial corporations by banks between 2019 and 2023."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,47,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","The variables include the contractual interest rate (in percentage points), the one-year-ahead probability of default (PD) as assessed by the lender, a binary indicator for whether the loan is secured, the committed loan amount (in millions of euros), the contractual maturity (in months), and the total value of collateral pledged at origination (in millions of euros)."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,47,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Summary statistics for collateral value are restricted to the subsample of secured loans. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 46
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,48,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Table 6: Collateral Presence (1) (2) (3) (4) Annualized interest rate ln(Committed amount) Collateral presence -0.173** -0.099** 0.481*** 0.331** (0.073) (0.042) (0.076) (0.138) PD 0.251*** 0.663*** -0.054 0.061 (0.075) (0.127) (0.036) (0.058) Maturity -0.188*** -0.164*** 0.302*** 0.423*** (0.044) (0.058) (0.053) (0.080) Loan type dummies ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Firm-Time FE ✓ ✓ Bank-Time FE ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Bank-Firm FE ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Sector-Country-Time FE ✓ ✓ N 5,835,528 3,779,951 5,836,153 3,780,446 AdjR2 0.85 0.93 0.77 0.77 Notes: This table shows the estimation results of the following speciﬁcation: Creditf,b,i,t “βCollateral Presencei ` γ1X ` FE ` εf,b,i,t, where Creditf,b,i,t denotes either (i) the annualized loan interest rate (Columns 1–2), or (ii) the natural logarithm of the committed loan amount (Columns 3–4), for loan i extended by bank b to ﬁrm f in month t. The variable Collateral Presencei is a binary indicator equal to 1 if the loan is collateralized, and zero otherwise."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,48,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","The control vector X includes: (i) the one-year-ahead probability of default (PD) as assessed by the lender, and (ii) the natural logarithm of the loans original maturity (in months)."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,48,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",All regressions include loan type ﬁxed eﬀects and additional high-dimensional ﬁxed eﬀects as speciﬁed in the table.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,48,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Robust standard errors are double-clustered at the bank and time level. ***, **,"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,48,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","and * indicate statistical signiﬁcance at the 1%, 5%, and 10% levels, respectively. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 47"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,49,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Table 7: Collateral Types (1) (2) (3) (4) Annualized interest rate ln(Committed amount) Real estate 0.062** 0.086* 0.460*** 0.515*** (0.030) (0.044) (0.111) (0.070) Physical movable assets -0.127*** -0.073 0.397*** 0.344*** (0.046) (0.053) (0.074) (0.107) Other assets -0.163** -0.150** 0.322*** 0.406*** (0.067) (0.063) (0.069) (0.041) Financial assets -0.203* -0.129 0.385*** 0.180 (0.107) (0.086) (0.097) (0.270) PD 0.252*** 0.657*** -0.050 0.049 (0.076) (0.130) (0.037) (0.059) Maturity -0.191*** -0.166*** 0.308*** 0.426*** (0.045) (0.057) (0.053) (0.078) Loan type dummies ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Firm-Time FE ✓ ✓ Bank-Time FE ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Bank-Firm FE ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ Sector-Country-Time FE ✓ ✓ N 5,774,951 3,738,979 5,775,576 3,739,471 AdjR2 0.85 0.93 0.76 0.77 Notes: This table shows the estimation results of the following speciﬁcation: Creditf,b,i,t “β1Collateral Typei ` γ1X ` FE ` εf,b,i,t, where Creditf,b,i,t denotes either (i) the annualized loan interest rate (Columns 1–2), or (ii) the natural logarithm of the committed loan amount (Columns 3–4), for loan i extended by bank b to ﬁrm f in month t. Collateral Typei includes a set of dummy variables indicating the collateral types securing loan i. Each dummy takes the value of one if the loan is secured by a speciﬁc collateral type, and zero otherwise."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,49,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","For example, if loan i is backed by both real estate and ﬁnancial assets, then the dummies for those two types will equal one, while the dummies for physical movable assets and other assets will equal zero."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,49,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","The control vector X includes: (i) the one-year-ahead probability of default (PD) as assessed by the lender, and (ii) the natural logarithm of the loans original maturity (in months)."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,49,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",All regressions include loan type ﬁxed eﬀects and additional highdimensional ﬁxed eﬀects as speciﬁed in the table.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,49,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Robust standard errors are double-clustered at the bank and time level. ***, **,"
