ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,52,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","and * indicate statistical signiﬁcance at the 1%, 5%, and 10% levels, respectively. ECB Working Paper Series No 3095 51"
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,53,0,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.","Table 11: Collateral Presence and 2019 Feature (1) (2) (3) (4) Annualized interest rate ln(Committed amount) Panel A: Importance of collateral presence Collateral presence -0.158** -0.082* 0.463*** 0.330** (0.068) (0.044) (0.072) (0.128) Collateral presence ˆ Presence ONA -0.083** -0.058* 0.105** 0.003 (0.039) (0.032) (0.040) (0.053) Panel B: Importance of real estate collateral Collateral presence -0.175** -0.079 0.484*** 0.310* (0.075) (0.057) (0.076) (0.165) Collateral presence ˆ RE ONA 0.013 -0.050 -0.024 0.051 (0.033) (0.032) (0.033) (0.063) Panel C: Importance of collateral value Collateral presence -0.156** -0.080* 0.449*** 0.343*** (0.062) (0.044) (0.069) (0.115) Collateral presence ˆ Value LTV 0.075 0.046 -0.141*** 0.028 (0.054) (0.044) (0.046) (0.077) Panel D: Importance of real estate collateral value Collateral presence -0.173** -0.086 0.481*** 0.319* (0.072) (0.055) (0.070) (0.163) Collateral presence ˆ Value RE 0.037 -0.039 -0.070* 0.037 (0.044) (0.033) (0.037) (0.070) Loan controls ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ FirmˆTime FE ✓ ✓ BankˆTime FE ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ BankˆFirm FE ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ SectorˆCountryˆTime FE ✓ ✓ N 5,835,528 3,779,951 5,836,153 3,780,446 AdjR2 0.85 0.93 0.77 0.77 Notes: This table shows the estimation results of the following speciﬁcation: Creditf,b,i,t “βCollateral Presencei ` µCollateral Presencei ˆ Featurec ` γ1X ` FE ` εf,b,i,t, where Creditf,b,i,t denotes either (i) the annualized loan interest rate (Columns 1–2), or (ii) the natural logarithm of the committed loan amount (Columns 3–4), for loan i issued by bank b to ﬁrm f in month t. Collateral Presencei is a dummy variable that equals one if a loan i has a collateral, zero otherwise."
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,53,1,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Featurec is one of the features of a country c in December 2019 calculated from stylized facts.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,53,2,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Presence ONAc is the ratio of secured loans ONA scaled by total loans ONA shown in Figure 2b.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,53,3,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",RE ONAc is the ratio of loans ONA secured by real estate collateral scaled by loans ONA secured by all collateral shown in Figure 3b.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,53,4,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Value LTVc is the ratio of secured loan ONA scaled by all collateral values.
ecb.wp3095~0e81ee7f34.en.pdf,53,5,Collateral and credit,"Hans Degryse, Olivier De Jonghe, Luc Laeven, Tong Zhao",D:20250812145549+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of collateral using the euro area corporate credit registry, Ana-Credit. We document key facts about the importance, distribution, and composition of collateral, including its presence, types, and values. On average, 70% of credit amounts are collateralized. Real estate and financial assets are the most pledged, while physical movable assets and other intangible assets are less present. In addition, we show that the aggregate collateral value pledged to the banking sector is substantial, driven mainly by real estate in most countries. For the first time, we examine the collateral channel in bank credit using the actual value of individual collateral. By exploiting within-firm and within-bank variations for newly issued secured loans, we find that the elasticity of collateral value to loan commitment amounts is around 0.7-0.8. This collateral value elasticity exhibits substantial country and time heterogeneity, which can be explained by legal, financial, and macro conditions.",Value REc is the ratio of real estate collateral values scaled by all collateral values shown in Figure 6b.
