ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,2,7,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","JEL classification: E22, D24, H54, O38, O52 ECB Working Paper Series No 3099 1 ECB 1"
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,0,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",Non-technical summary Productivity is a key driver of long-term economic growth and living standards.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,1,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","In recent decades, however, Europe has experienced a marked and persistent slowdown in productivity growth, raising concerns among policymakers and bringing the topic to the centre of the policy debate."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,2,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Against this backdrop, EU Cohesion Policy, delivered primarily through the European Structural and Investment Funds (ESIF), represent one of the EU’s main instruments for promoting economic development, productivity, and regional convergence."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,3,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",This paper evaluates the impact of the 2014–2020 ESIF on firm-level outcomes across the EU.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,4,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Using a novel dataset that links firm-level information from the Orbis database with project-level data from the European Commission’s Kohesio dataset, the study examines (1) which firms receive EU funding and (2) how receiving funding affects their investment and productivity."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,5,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","The analysis shows that EU funding is typically directed toward relatively well-performing firms, that are less capital-intensive and face some degree of financial constraints."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,6,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Firms receiving funding increase their capital by around 15% within one year and experience a gradual productivity growth, reaching approximately 3% after four years."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,7,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","These effects are more pronounced for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) and for firms facing financing constraint, suggesting that EU funding helps to unlock investment by easing access to capital."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,8,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Moreover, the study finds that not all categories of funding yield the same outcomes."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,9,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Projects aimed at supporting “SME investment” have stronger positive effects on capital accumulation and productivity growth, while projects targeting green transition objectives show more limited gains."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,10,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",The findings highlight the important role of EU programmes in enhancing firm-level investment and productivity.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,11,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",They also suggest that targeted allocation—especially towards SMEs and financially constrained firms—can improve the policy’s effectiveness.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,3,12,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","These insights are especially relevant as the EU continues to channel investments during the ongoing Cohesion Policy programming period—and the Recovery and Resilience Facility—, while also preparing to revamp the EU budget for the 2028–2034 cycle. ECB Working Paper Series No 3099 2"
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,0,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","1 Introduction Productivity is a fundamental driver of long-term economic growth and improvements in living standards.1 While productivity performance has long been a priority for policymakers, it has recently taken centre stage in the EU policy debate amid the pronounced and persistent slowdown observed in Europe over the past three decades (Draghi, 2024)."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,1,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","In this context, assessing the effectiveness of existing policy instruments aimed at fostering investment and productivity is essential, not only to evaluate their impact, but also to inform future policy design by identifying what works, for whom, and under what conditions."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,2,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",An example of such instruments is the EU Cohesion Policy.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,3,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","While the EU’s fiscal capacity remains limited, the Cohesion Policy—operationalized through the European Structural and Investment Funds (ESIF)—stands as one of the Union’s most significant instruments for promoting economic development."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,4,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Over the past 25 years, the EU has invested approximately one trillion euros through these funds, with the objective of reducing regional disparities and promoting economic and social cohesion."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,5,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","For the current programming period (2021-2027), the EU has allocated around €392 billion for Cohesion Policy initiatives, confirming its commitment to promoting convergence and growth through this instrument.2 This paper contributes to the growing literature on the effectiveness of place-based policy by evaluating the impact of EU structural funds on firm-level outcomes, focusing in particular on firms’ investment and productivity."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,6,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","To do so, we construct a novel dataset that links firm-level information from the Orbis database with project-level data from the European Commission’s Kohesio dataset, covering the entire EU."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,7,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",We address two main questions: (1) What are the characteristics of firms that receive EU funding? (
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,8,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",2) What are the effects of receiving EU funding on firm performance?
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,9,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","To address the first question, we use a logistic regression model to identify the firm characteristics that most influence the likelihood of receiving funding."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,10,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","In addition, to prevent over-fitting and isolate the most relevant predictors, we also apply lasso penalization to select a key subset of firm-level variables."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,4,11,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","This allows us to uncover structural differences between treated and never-treated firms, and informs our subsequent identification strategy."
