ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,14,3,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",The excluded group shows the number of firms that were part of non-valid matches (mostly due to being part of one-to-many or many-to-one matches).
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,14,4,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Importantly, the treatment group used in the regression analysis is smaller than the number of valid matches reported in table 3, as some firms are dropped from the regressions due to missing values in one or more explanatory variables or insuﬀicient coverage over time."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,14,5,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",3.5 Productivity estimation One of the key variables we analyse is firm level total factor productivity (TFP).
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,14,6,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",Since TFP is not directly observable we need to estimate it from the firm level data.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,14,7,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",We follow the approach of Levinsohn and Petrin (2003) and use Orbis data to estimate the production functions separately for each country and two digit NACE sector pair.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,14,8,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",Further details are provided in Appendix A.2. ECB Working Paper Series No 3099 13
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,0,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","4 Empirical Analysis 4.1 Summary Statistics We match approximately 90,000 firms in Orbis to projects data from the Cohesion Policy."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,1,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","The total EU funding allocated to the firms in our dataset accounts for approximately 16 billion euros, which accounts for 34% of the total of 47 billion euros directly allocated to firms under the Cohesion Policy for the 2014-2020 programming period12."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,2,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Based on current Commission reporting, 47 billion had been allocated to firms by 2022, entirely under the ERDF, accounting for roughly 22% of the fund’s total allocation."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,3,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Hence, while our dataset covers a substantial share of the funds directly matched to firms, our analysis focuses on a specific component of the overall Cohesion Policy."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,4,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Consistently with reporting by the Commission, most of the firms matched (approximately 81%) and assigned to our treatment group receive funding from the European Regional Development Fund."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,5,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","However, we also find that 17% of firms receive funding from the European Social Fund and under 2% from the Youth Employment Initiative."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,6,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","This discrepancy is not unexpected, as Commission reporting is subject to ongoing updates and has not yet been finalised."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,7,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","The policy objectives under which firms receive funding are: Smarter Europe – 75% of firms, Social Europe – 19%, and Greener carbon-free Europe – 6%."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,8,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","The most common thematic objectives are: Competitiveness of SMEs – 63% of firms, Sustainable and quality employment – 15%, Research and innovation – 11%, and Low-carbon economy – 5%."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,9,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",Figure 5 illustrates a word cloud derived from the descriptions of funded projects involving firms in our treatment group.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,10,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","The visualization prominently features key terms such as “development,” “business,” “knowhow,” “permanent,” “new,” “structural,” “competitiveness,” “implementation,” “overcome,” and “diﬀiculties.”"
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,11,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","These words indicate a strong focus on promoting sustainable and enduring business growth, emphasizing structural changes, and enhancing firms’ competitiveness."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,12,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Moreover, the frequent appearance of terms such as “innovation,” “internationalisation,” “expansion,” “modernisation,” and references to “SMEs” underline the intent of funding transformative investments."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,15,13,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","These terms collectively suggest EU Cohesion funding is primarily aimed at addressing structural and operational challenges, facilitating innovation, and boosting long-term productivity and competitiveness."
