ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,0,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","funding for the following five broad categories: “Small and Medium Enterprise (SME) investment”, “SME support, Research & Development”, “Green transition”, “Social and territorial cohesion”.17 We find that while all funding categories support firm outcomes, the categories “SME investment” and “Green transition” offer the most interesting insights.18 Projects categorized under “SME investment” (Figure 11a) prominently highlight terms such as “equipment”, “production”, “capacity”, “investment,” “acquisition,” “modernisation”, and “innovation”."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,1,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","These keywords indicate a strong emphasis on capital expenditure, with funding directed towards tangible assets and production-enhancing investments aimed at improving the competitiveness, productivity, and innovation capabilities of SMEs."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,2,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","On the other hand, the “Green transition” category (Figure 11b) features frequent references to “energy”, “eﬀiciency”, “saving”, “renewable”, “replacement,” “installation” and “management”."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,3,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","These terms reflect a clear focus on sustainability, including measures to enhance energy eﬀiciency, implement renewable energy solutions, and promote sustainable management practices within firms. ("
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,4,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",a) SME investement (b) Green transition Figure 11: Word Clouds for projects pertaining to “SME investment” and “Green transition” Results suggest that funding targeted specifically towards “SME investment” is more effective in facilitating firm investment and enhancing total factor productivity (TFP).
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,5,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Consistently, this targeted funding leads to a more pronounced increase in firms’ leverage ratios."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,6,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","In contrast, funding received for “Green transition” projects has a more modest and shorter-lived effect on increasing firm capital, with no significant effect beyond year 2, when compared to other projects."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,7,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","It also has little to no effect on productivity, intangible intensity, or the leverage ratio."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,30,8,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","This suggests that adopting green practices may not necessarily yield substantial productivity gains in the short term — for instance, when operational high-emitting equipment is replaced 17The ‘SME investment” dummy is based on the descriptive data pertaining to the projects under which firms received funding, while in the previous section the “SME” dummy was based purely on the size of the firm."
