ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,38,12,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Filter outliers based on leverage ratios, dropping firm-year observations outside the 0.1st to 99.9th percentile range for: a) Capital to shareholder funds ratio, b) Total assets to shareholder funds ratio."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,38,13,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","Filter outliers based on growth rates, dropping firms in all years if: a) For firms with 0-10 employees: growth rate ≥1000% (10 times), b) For firms with 11-20 employees: growth rate ≥500% (5 times), c) For firms with 21-50 employees: growth rate ≥300% (3 times), d) For firms with 51-100 employees: growth rate ≥200% (2 times), e) For firms with 101+ employees: growth rate ≥100% (1 time), f) For firms with missing lagged employment: growth rate ≥2000% (20 times)."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,38,14,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","These conditions are applied separately for: Employment growth, Sales growth, Operating revenue growth. ECB Working Paper Series No 3099 37"
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,39,0,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",Deflate nominal variables using country-specific GDP deflators from the World Bank World Development Indicators database (we use 2015 as base year so that all the nominal values are expressed in constant 2015 EUR).
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,39,1,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",A.2 Estimating firm-level productivity We follow the approach of Levinsohn and Petrin (2003) to estimate firm level total factor productivity (TFP) as implemented in STATA by the prodest function written by Rovigatti and Mollisi (2016).
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,39,2,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.",We estimate the production functions separately for each country and two-digit NACE sector pair.
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,39,3,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","We exclude NACE sector A (Agriculture, forestry and fishing) as for this sector the production function without land as a production input is misspecified."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,39,4,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","We begin with a 3 inputs log-linear Cobb-Douglas production function: yit = β0 + βk kit + βl lit + βm mit + ωit + εit, where yit is the log of output for firm i at time t, kit is capital, lit is labour, mit is materials (or other intermediate input), ωit represents the firm’s unobserved productivity (TFP), εit is an i.i.d."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,39,5,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","The key econometric challenge is that ωit is observed by the firm when making input decisions but not by the econometrician, potentially leading to endogeneity problems."
ecb.wp3099~d0ab147fbf.en.pdf,39,6,Unlocking growth? EU investment programmes and firm performance,"Alessandro De Sanctis, Daniel Kapp, Francesca Vinci, Robert Wojciechowski",D:20250814102858+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This study evaluates the effectiveness of EU Cohesion Policy as an investment programme, employing a novel dataset that links firm-level data from Orbis with project-level information from the Kohesio database. It focuses on two key questions: (1) Which firms receive EU funding? (2) How does receiving EU funding affect firm performance? By applying a logit model and a local projection difference-in-differences approach, we provide new insights into the allocation mechanisms of EU Cohesion Policy funds and their firm-level impact. Our findings show that funding tends to be allocated to firms that already perform relatively well, and that firms receiving EU funding experience a persistent productivity increase of approximately 3% after 4 years, with smaller and more financially constrained firms experiencing relatively greater improvements. Moreover, funding targeting “SME investment” tends to enhance firm performance disproportionately more than other categories, whereas projects directed the “green transition” appear comparatively less beneficial.","The estimation is carried out in two stages, under the following assumptions: 1."
