ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,13,8,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","The most common rating is BBB+ (e250 bn), followed by A- (e163 bn) and A+ (e139 bn)."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,13,9,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","In terms of holdings, mutual funds hold less than 15% of bonds rated AA, 23% of bonds rated A, and 31% of bonds rated BBB."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,13,10,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","In contrast, insurance companies and pension funds hold a large share of bonds rated AA (50%) and A (40%), but a smaller share of bonds rated BBB (33%)."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,13,11,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","This is particularly true for bonds with a best rating of BBB-, where their share is just 23%."
