ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,34,10,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","In this case, we use the change in OAS in the week following the PEPP announcement."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,34,11,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","We assess the response from the Monday following the announcement (March 23) for 5 business days (one week), 10 business days (two weeks), and 30 business days (approximately one month)."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,34,12,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",The findings reveal a more pronounced reduction in bond spreads for bonds with higher mutual fund shares.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,34,13,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","For a 10 percentage point increase in mutual fund holdings, we observe a further 4.8 basis point reduction in bond yields when fixed effects are not included."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,34,14,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","The effects are even larger when we consider more saturated regressions, with the coefficients ranging from -48 to -72."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,34,15,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","As expected, the coefficient is smaller when we control for the bidask spreads, as it is more likely that bonds sold through fire sales experienced a widening of the bid-ask spread prior to the announcement."
