funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",A.1 Setting The fundamental settings are explained in Section 7.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,2,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","In this appendix, we will detail the additional features of the infinite-horizon model."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,3,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",State of the Economy The state of the economy is determined by the variable zt and follows the autoregressive process in log: zt+1 = ρzzt + σz t εz t+1.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,4,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",The volatility of the process σz t is time varying and depends on the state of the economy itself: σz t = ¯σz 1 + βσezt.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,5,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",This formulation results in high coupon volatility during economic downturns and low volatility during economic expansions.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,6,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","The parameter βσ determines the sensitivity of volatility to the state variable, while the parameter ¯σz scales the average volatility."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,7,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Payoff In the dynamic model, we assume that the payoff of the risky asset is: δt+1 = ¯δezt+1, (19) where ¯δ is a scale parameter.23 Assets under Management We assume that assets under management for both the bond managers and the long-term investors are fixed over time."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,8,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","This assumption indicates that bond managers and long-term investors return all profits and losses to the ultimate 23The assumption does not alter the intuition of the model but ensures that the coupon process follows a log-normal distribution, while Equation 11 represents its inverse-lognormal transformation."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,9,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Since we numerically solve for the standard deviation of returns, the inverse-lognormal transformation introduces instability in the standard deviation."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,54,10,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","This, in turn, affects the asset allocation of managers, leading to ill-defined allocations in certain states and causing instability in convergence. ECB Working Paper Series No 3101 53"
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,0,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Consequently, in the absence of fund flows, the assets under management remain unchanged."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,1,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Allocation of Investors At each time t, the long-term investors optimal allocation is still defined by Equation 13."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,2,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",The allocation of the bond manager is by solving a mean-variance problem and the optimal portfolio is still determined by Equation 12.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,3,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",Central Banks We assume that the central bank holds a quantity of bonds that are credit risky XCB t which follows the policy rule in Equation 14.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,4,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","The central bank maintains a fixed allocation, denoted as ¯XCB, and may also have a statedependent component determined by βCB."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,5,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",The central bank’s assets are normalized to one.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,6,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Notably, Equation 14 suggests that the central bank’s holdings can be negative."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,7,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Generalizing to include a non-negative holdings constraint is straightforward, given the exogenous nature of the process."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,8,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","However, we will use this process to further show that even when the central bank’s average holdings are zero (i.e., when ¯XCB = 0), asset purchases can still significantly influence risk premia if βCB < 0."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,55,9,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",This effect arises from the state-contingent nature of the policy rather than the average level of holdings.
