funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",This involves tilting purchases toward issuers with better climate performance through the reinvestment of significant redemptions expected in the coming years.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,3,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",Initial Market Reaction We examine the overall reaction of corporate bond yields to the ECB announcement by studying an aggregate corporate bond yield for Euro area nonfinancial corporations.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,4,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Subsequently, we decompose the index into an interest rate (defaultfree) component and a credit spread component."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,5,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",It is important to note that the credit spread component also encompasses the liquidity premium component.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,6,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","The construction of the aggregate index and its decomposition is detailed as follows: = X i wi,t × yi,t = X + X i wi,t × ιi,t | {z } Interest Rate Component i wi,t × ysi,t | {z } Credit Spread Component Yt |{z} Market Corporate Yield. ("
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,7,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","29) Here, yi,t represents the yield-to-maturity of bond i at time t, and wi,t denotes the notional outstanding amount of bond i as a share of the total outstanding amount of all bonds in the sample."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,8,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Additionally, ιi,t stands for the swap yield with maturity equal to the maturity of bond i, while ysi,t signifies the spread of corporate bond i in excess of the default-free rate."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,9,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Figure 2b illustrates the progression of the aggregate index, showcasing the impact of the CSPP announcement in effectively reducing corporate bond yields."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,10,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","A significant drop in bond yields occurred on the day of the announcement, followed by a more gradual decrease in the subsequent weeks."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,11,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Notably, the decline was predominantly driven by a reduction in the spread component, as evidenced by Figure D.7a."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,12,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","The decomposition also reveals the mechanism through which the transmission of corporate bond purchases unfolded, resulting in a decrease in credit spread or liquidity risk premium."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,13,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","The policy package not only impacted prices, but the period following the announcements also witnessed an increase in the volume of bond issuance by Euro area nonfinancial corporations."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,77,14,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","This observation aligns with findings in several papers in the literature, which have demonstrated the causal link between CSPP announcements and their effects on both bond yields and bond issuance volumes (Abidi and Miquel-Flores (2018); Grosse-Rueschkamp ECB Working Paper Series No 3101 76"
