ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,7,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","We also observe that in the 2014-2020 sample, monetary policy shocks exert significantly lower effects on corporate bond spreads (Figure H.2a)."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,8,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","During this period, short-term interest rates in the Euro area remained close to the effective lower bound."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,9,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","Consequently, monetary policy shocks were small in magnitude (i.e., the standard deviation of the shocks was small compared to the full sample), resulting in a muted bond response."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,10,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","This stands in contrast to the 2020-2023 sample (Figure H.2b), characterized by the recent ECB monetary policy tightening."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,11,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","In this period, we find that a 100 bps monetary policy shock (tightening) leads to a 100 bps increase in spreads 6 days from the announcement and a 150 bps increase 15 days from the announcement (note that lags are measured in business days from announcements)."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,12,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",The standard deviation of monetary policy shocks in the 2020-2023 sample period is double the one of 2014-2020.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,13,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.",One potential explanation for the significant difference between the 2014-2020 sample and the 2020-2023 sample could be associated with the intermediaries’ response to monetary policy shocks.
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,14,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","A monetary policy shock of small magnitude may not elicit a substantial reaction in the capital intermediated by mutual funds, consequently resulting in a more subdued response in corporate bond spreads."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,15,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","By contrast, the decisive tightening cycle initiated by the ECB in 2021 could prompt a portfolio rebalancing of households and intermediaries, ultimately exerting a heightened influence on the corporate bond market."
ecb.wp3101~78c61bcd05.en.pdf,90,16,Heterogeneous intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases,"Fédéric Holm-Hadulla, Matteo Leombroni",D:20250820141337+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper studies the role of financial intermediaries in the transmission of central bank corporate bond purchases to bond yields. Contrary to standard expectations, we find that mutual funds—typically viewed as price-elastic investors—amplify, rather than dampen, the effects of these interventions on bond spreads. Following the ECB’s corporate bond purchase announcements in 2016 and 2020, bonds predominantly held by mutual funds experienced significantly larger and more persistent declines in spreads compared to those held by price-inelastic investors such as insurance companies, even after controlling for a broad set of bond characteristics. Drawing on additional empirical evidence and an equilibrium asset pricing model, we show that the state-contingent nature of the policy reduces perceived market risk for procyclical investors like mutual
funds, thereby boosting demand and compressing risk premia.","In summary, Figure H.2 demonstrates that the transmission of monetary policy to spreads is highly non-linear."
