ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,10,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The dominance of geography over income extends to many countries, as shown in Table 1."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,10,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Germany, Spain, the Netherlands, and the United Kingdom, the energy ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 9"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","share of total expenditures is relatively flat across income quintiles, with Q1-to-Q5 ratios of 1.1, 1.1, 0.7, and 1.0, respectively."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","However, the energy share in these countries varies significantly across living areas, with Rural-to-City ratios of 1.4, 1.1, 1.7, and 1.4, respectively."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the United States, geography also plays a key role in determining energy consumption (8.3% in rural areas versus 5.7% in cities with populations over 1 million)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Income differences are more pronounced, with energy shares of 8.8% for Q1 compared to 4.8% for Q5."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This contrast between the United States and Europe can be attributed to transportation costs: while transportation expenses are higher for wealthier households in Europe, the opposite is true in the United States, where even the lowest-income households allocate a substantial share of their expenditures to transportation."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Therefore, geography plays a more significant role than income in explaining the share of energy and fossil fuels in household expenditures."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Accounting for this geographic dimension is crucial for understanding the distributive effects of carbon taxation, as fossil fuels constitute the majority of households’ direct emissions."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","However, carbon taxes affect not only households but also the firms that employ them."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","1.2 Firms’ direct emissions Some sectors, such as metalworking, agriculture, and transportation, have higher emissions and are therefore more affected by carbon taxes."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","These sectors are also unevenly distributed across regions and occupations, implying that both income and geography play a role in determining the firms where households are employed."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This, in turn, shapes the distribution of the indirect costs of carbon taxes."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We use administrative matched employer-employee data from France known as BTS-Salari´es.5 The BTS dataset has two advantages.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","First, it is exhaustive, containing 32 million workers per wave, providing rich demographic, geographic, and plant-level information."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Second, it has a panel version that covers the entire work history of a representative set of workers (over 3 million individuals).6 The large sample size enables us to conduct a detailed analysis by city code and to finely disaggregate employer and worker groups, which allows us to control for composition effects."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Our contribution is to merge this dataset with sectoral emissions data from the 2022 National Accounts.7 To assess workers’ exposure to a carbon tax on firms, we compute GHGs emissions per worker in each establishment of the economy."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,15,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Using sectoral-level emissions and establishments’ labor share, we construct plant-level emissions."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,11,16,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We then build worker-level 5Base Tous Salari´es – fichiers Salari´es.
