ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,19,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Untargeted moments – income composition, taxes, wealth and MPCs distributions – are presented in Appendix C. 3.1 Households Consumption heterogeneity."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,19,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We use ΛE and ΛH to match the average energy and housing share in total expenditures, and we normalize ΛC to 1 as in Comin, Lashkari and Mestieri (2021)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,19,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The parameters ϵE and ϵH are calibrated to fit the non-homotheticity of energy and housing across the income distribution and ϵC is normalized to 1.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,19,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We then add the ¯e(k) to match the observed spatial heterogeneity in energy constraints.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,19,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We normalize ¯e(Paris) = 0 and set the other ¯e(k) to match the average energy share in each city type, and γ(k) to have the right energy mix in each area, as shown in Figure 4.a. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 18"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Figure 4: Energy share in total consumption Notes: share of fossil fuel [(pF + τ h)F h] and electricity [pNN h] in total consumption expenditures [c + (pF + τ h)F h + pNN h].
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Panel a: by geographical location.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panel b: by disposable income quintile, untargeted in the model."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Source: BdF 2017 Insee survey.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We estimate the elasticity of substitution between energy and G&S consumption to σ = 0.2, using National Accounts longitudinal data from 1959 to 2021 (the data and method are described in Appendix C)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, we set the elasticity of substitution between fossil fuel and electricity to ϵh = 1.5."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Literature estimates range from 0.02 in the short-run in Hassler, Krusell and Olovsson (2021) to 2 in the long-run for Papageorgiou, Saam and Schulte (2017): we choose this value to be the same as the one selected for firms (ϵy), estimated in Fried, Novan and Peterman (2024)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Appendix F, we provide robustness check for alternative values of σ, ϵh and ϵy."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As changes in transfer, labor and capital incomes account for a large part of the distributive effects of carbon taxation, we calibrate carefully the distribution of each type of income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We fit the disposable income distribution9 (Figure 5.a), using the AR(1) persistence parameter ρz, which is set to be the same across all types."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We use the means µz(k) and variances σz(k) of the idiosyncratic productivity process for each type to match the proportion of each geographical location type within each disposable income quintile (Figure 5.b).
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Our model recovers that Parisian households are richer than average, as they account for 26% of the top income quintile but only 19% of the population."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,20,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Households living in rural areas or small cities are more equally distributed, 9From the 2021 Insee survey “Revenus et patrimoine des m´enages” (RPM 2021). ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 19"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","with over-representation in Q2, Q3 and Q4, and under-representation in Q1 and Q5."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, we set the annual discount factor β = 0.94 to match the French capital-toincome ratio10 when excluding public debt: a GDP = 4.5, and the intertemporal elasticity of substitution (IES) 1/θ to 1."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Figure 5: income distribution of households Notes: Panel a: quintile of disposable income (source: RPM 2021 Insee survey).
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panel b: share of each geographical location type within each quintile in data (solid lines, source: BdF 2017 Insee survey) and in the model (dashed lines)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Each quintile sums to 100%.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","3.2 Migration costs We use migration costs κ(k, k′) to recover the empirical probability of migrating from region k to region k′. We first compute the empirical migration matrix over a 5-year horizon.11 Specifically, we compute the probability of being in region k′ at time t + 5, conditional on being in region k at time t. We then construct a 5 × 5 migration cost matrix κ to match the empirical migration matrix, normalizing the diagonal to zero to reflect zero cost for staying in the same region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The migration probabilities in the model and in the data are shown in panels a and b of Figure 5.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The model fits the data well, and it replicates three key stylized facts."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","First, on average, 85% of households remain in their current region over a 5-year period, implying an annual migration probability of approximately 3% (1 −0.851/5)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Second, movers tend to relocate to regions of a similar type (i.e., values close to the diagonal), with very few migrations between extremes such as Paris and rural areas."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Third, very few people move to Paris, and 93% of current Parisians remain in Paris five years later (or 98.6% after one year)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",10See 2022 Banque de France report.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","11To construct this migration matrix, we use the panel dataset BTS-Salari´es 2016–2021."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We retain only workers aged between 30 and 55, with annual wages above €2,100, and who are present in the dataset throughout the 2016–2021 period."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,21,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This sample includes 1,010,559 individuals. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 20"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Figure 6: Migration matrix and migration parameters Notes: Panel a and b: probability to have migrated from k towards k′ 5 years later, with {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} = {Rural, Small, Medium, Large, Paris}."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Sources: panel data from BTS-Salaries 2016-2021.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panel c: migration cost from k to k′ in the model, as % of income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panel d: change in model probability of migrating in region k′, following a decrease in average labor income tax rate in region 1, see text."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",How do our migration costs relate to the empirical literature?
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",There are two main approaches in the literature to discipline migration costs in models.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The first is to estimate monetary moving costs directly from data or through structural modeling.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","For instance, Kennan and Walker (2011) estimate an average moving cost of $312,000, equivalent to approximately 600% of annual income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","However, this is a hypothetical cost, as it reflects the case of an individual being forced to move to a random location; in practice, households choose destinations, typically reducing the cost."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Similarly, Artu¸c, Chaudhuri and McLaren (2010) estimate sectoral migration costs ranging from 2 to 13 times the average annual wage."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Other studies, such as Bryan and Morten (2019) and Clemens, Montenegro and Pritchett (2019), employ lower values, between 15% and 50% of annual income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panel c of Figure 6 presents our migration cost matrix, expressed as a share of GDP per capita."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We highlight three important features.
