ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,22,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",This hypothetical cost is the average an individual would have to pay to move to an arbitrary ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 21
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","If we instead average over observed migration flows, we find that migrating households pay 30% on average."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Second, migration costs vary substantially depending on the origin region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Averaging across each row, i.e. P k′=k κ(k, k′)/4, we obtain the hypothetical cost of moving from region k to a randomly chosen region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","These values are 118%, 115%, 92%, 69%, and 0% for rural, small, medium, large, and Paris, respectively."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Thus, the smaller the origin region, the higher the expected cost of migration."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Third, migration costs also depend on the destination region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Averaging across each column, i.e. considering the cost of migrating to region k′ from a randomly selected origin, yields values of 15%, 10%, 28%, 50% and 289% for rural, small, medium, large, and Paris, respectively."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This suggests that the larger and more urbanized the destination, the higher the associated migration cost."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The second approach to disciplining migration costs is to estimate the dynamic response of migration probabilities following changes in tax rates, typically for highincome households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","For example, Young and Varner (2011) finds that a change in the top income tax rate leads to a 0.1 percentage point change in the migration probability of millionaires."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Similarly, Akcigit, Baslandze and Stantcheva (2016) estimates an elasticity of around 0.03 for local inventors."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In contrast, Martinez (2017) finds much higher elasticities, ranging from 3.2 to 6.5, for wealthy Swiss taxpayers."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Spain, Agrawal and Foremny (2019) shows that a 1% increase in a region’s net-of-tax rate relative to others increases the probability of moving to that region by 1.7 percentage points."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In panel d of Figure 6, we replicate this type of experiment in partial equilibrium."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Specifically, we increase λ, the average net-of-tax rate on labor income, by 1% in the rural region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,15,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We then compute households’ optimal location decisions, holding all other prices constant, and compare the resulting transition matrix to the baseline."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,16,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","For each income quintile, we examine the probability of migrating to region k′, conditional on starting in another region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,17,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As expected, we observe positive values in the first column and negative values in the others: following the tax cut, households are more likely to move to the rural region and less likely to move elsewhere."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,18,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Moreover, the change in migration probability is much larger for high-income households than for low-income ones."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,19,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",This is because wealthier individuals face lower effective migration barriers and benefit more from lower income taxes due to their higher productivity.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,20,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In summary, tax changes can induce migration, particularly among richer households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,23,21,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We will return to this mechanism in our results section, when analyzing the effects of carbon taxation—a non-region-specific policy whose impacts differ by location. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 22"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",3.3 Firms Goods and services firm.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The energy share ωy(k) is city-specific and accounts for the share of each regional firm in total emissions, as illustrated in Figure 2."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We follow Fried (2018) and set the elasticities of substitution between energy and the capitallabor bundle, and between electricity and fossil fuel, to respectively σy = 0.05 and ϵy = 1.5."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","These elasticities lie within the range of estimates from Papageorgiou, Saam and Schulte (2017): we provide robustness check for alternative values in Appendix F. The capital share is set to α = 0.31 to match the share of labor revenue wl GDP = 65% following Cette, Koehl and Philippon (2019)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The share of fossil fuel in the policy mix is set to γy = 0.86 such that electricity accounts for 33% of the regional firms’ energy mix.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, the depreciation rate is set to δ = 11.8% to match the aggregate share of investment as in Auray et al. ("
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Electricity firm and other parameters.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The electricity sector is capital intensive, so we set ζ = 0.9813 to have FN F = 1%."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We assume that electricity is produced using few fossil fuel inputs because France relies mainly on nuclear power plants and hydroelectricity from dams.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The initial price pF of the imported fossil fuel is set such that fossil fuel imports account for 4% of the GDP.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The housing supply scaling parameters {Hk=1,2,3,4} are set to obtain the population share of each region in France: 23.5%, 26.0%, 18.5%, 13.4%, and 18.6% for Rural, Small, Medium, Large, and Paris."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The last parameter H5 is set to obtain the share of housing in total wealth H/A = 0.66.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The price elasticity of housing supply is set to δH = 0.2, in the range of common values found in the housing model literature (for example 0.1 for Murphy (2018) and 0.3 for Baum-Snow and Han (2024))."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, in our main quantitative exercise, we suppose the price of fossil fuel is fixed and does not react to the domestic demand (δF = 0): this small-open economy assumption is relaxed in Appendix F. 3.4 Fiscal authority We set lump-sum transfers to T = 0.08 to match the share of transfer in each disposable income quintile, as shown in Figure 13.a."
