ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,16,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","2022), λ targets public spending ¯G at 29.3% of GDP, we set the effective VAT rate τ VAT to 22.24% and the effective capital income tax rate to 9.02%."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,17,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The resulting amount of tax paid by each group of households is shown in Figure 13.b.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,24,18,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The fit with the data is good, as we mostly miss corporate taxes in the model. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 23"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","4 Quantitative results In Section 1, we show that geography is an important determinant of energy consumption for households and firms."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Sections 2 and 3, we build a spatial heterogeneousagent model, calibrated on France."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In this section, we increase carbon taxes τ h or τ f and compute the welfare change associated with the transition."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The experiment is as follows.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We start at the initial steady state as described in Section 3.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","At t = 1, we introduce an unexpected shock to the path of τ h or τ f. After t = 1, the path is perfectly anticipated by agents."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The shock is permanent, with the final tax calibrated to reduce emissions by 10% at the final steady state."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The increase in tax is linear: the tax rises from 0 to τ final over 10 periods, and stays at τ final for t ≥10."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The carbon tax revenue, in this benchmark experiment, is used to increase public spending; we consider alternative rebating policies in Section 5."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The welfare is measured as the wealth transfer now that is equivalent to the welfare change during the transition.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","It answers the question: “what share of my income should I receive now to be indifferent between staying at the initial steady state, or experiencing the transition?”."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Formally, for each initial wealth a0, region k0 and productivity z0, we find x that satisfies the following equality: ∞ X ∞ X t=0 βtE0[U no tax i,t |a0 + x, k0, z0] = t=0 βtE0[U tax i,t |a0, k0, z0] with U = u1−θ 1−θ."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, we express the wealth equivalent by dividing x by total disposable income: WE(a0, k0, z0) = x(a0, k0, z0)/TI(a0, k0, z0)12."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Therefore, a wealth equivalent of −10% means that a household should receive a one-time lump-sum transfer equal to 10% of their current income in order to be indifferent between staying at the steady state or going through the transition with the increase in carbon tax."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Alternatively, dividing this number by the infinite sum of discount factors P∞ t=1 βt ≈15.7 gives the transfer a households should receive every year in order to be indifferent between staying at the steady state or experiencing the transition."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,15,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In this section, we describe the transmission of τ h and τ f to household welfare, categorized by income quintile and location."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,16,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We also examine the role of migration in shaping the distributive effects of carbon taxes, and highlight that the associated costs may differ between the short run and the long run."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,25,17,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","12With TI(a, k, z) = Γ ␀ z(k)w(k)¯l  + (1 −τ k)ra + T(k). ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 24"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","4.1 The distributive effects of carbon taxes The carbon tax burden varies significantly depending on location, income, and the type of tax."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Figure 7 presents the average welfare effects (in wealth equivalent, as described above) by region and income quintile for the initial distribution, for an increase of τ h only (left panel) and τ f only (right panel)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Before examining the different channels, we provide some general observations."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","First, there is a welfare cost associated with reducing emissions by 10%, as the WE is negative, assuming G is not valued by households to isolate the distributive effects of carbon taxation."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",This cost is higher for τ h (−27% of initial disposable income on average) than for τ f (−11% of initial disposable income).
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This implies that the social planner would need to compensate each individual with a one-time e10,500 transfer to make households accept the increase in τ h, and e4,250 for the increase in τ f. Alternatively, the planner should give e673 and e272 transfers every year, respectively, to make households accept the increase in τ h and τ f. Second, both taxes are regressive, as the welfare cost is higher for poorer households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The regressivity is significantly more pronounced for τ h. Third, the welfare cost varies substantially by location."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Parisian households tend to experience smaller welfare losses than other regions, regardless of income, while households in small and medium cities consistently face high losses."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We now detail the distributive effects of both taxes.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Figure 7: Average welfare effect by region and income Notes: This represents the average welfare change (one-time wealth-equivalent transfer expressed in % of households’ disposable income) for each group over the transition.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Panel a: change in τ h only.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Panel b: change in τ f only.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Carbon tax on households (τ h).
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Taxing fossil fuel consumption by households, with few indirect income effects."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,26,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As shown in the decomposition in Figure 15, the overall ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 25"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","welfare impact of τ h depends on two key factors: the direct effect of the carbon tax, and the change in housing rents pH. The direct effect of τ h is more pronounced for households with high fossil fuel consumption, i.e., rural and low-income households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Although households can substitute energy with goods and fossil fuels with electricity, the non-homotheticity of energy consumption with respect to income (ϵE) and geography (¯e) generates heterogeneous welfare costs."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Specifically, the welfare cost is equal to −29.7% of initial disposable income (WE) in rural areas compared to −23.4% WE in Paris, and −34.7% WE for the bottom income quintile (Q1) versus −19.6% WE for the top quintile (Q5)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","However, this adverse effect on rural households is partially offset by a decline in rents."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As some households relocate from small to large cities to avoid the carbon tax, housing price decreases by 6.2% in rural areas and increases by 4.6% in Paris, mitigating the geographic disparity."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Thus, while the carbon tax disproportionately burdens rural areas because of energy consumption differences, migration and housing market adjustments alleviate some of this burden."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Carbon tax on firms (τ f).
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Taxing fossil fuel consumption by firms alters their input mix and impacts households through changes in income and general equilibrium effects.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As illustrated in Figure 15, the welfare impact of τ f depends on adjustments in wages, housing rents, and the interest rate."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Since firms in rural areas are more fossil fuelintensive, the rise in fossil fuel prices reduces the demand for other inputs, particularly labor, leading to a decrease in wages of 3.9% in rural areas compared to a 1.1% decrease in Paris."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This results in welfare costs of −17% WE and −5% WE, respectively."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The decline in wages disproportionately affects lower-income households, as labor income constitutes a larger share of their total income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As with τ h, this geographic burden is partially offset by a decrease in housing rents: as households migrate from rural to urban areas seeking higher wages, pH decreases in rural areas, mitigating losses for households that remain."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Lastly, the reduction in firms’ capital demand lowers the interest rate, which mostly affects wealthier households because capital income constitutes a larger portion of their earnings."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Policy implications of EU ETS 1 and EU ETS 2.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,15,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Although carbon taxes and carbon quotas are different, our framework can give us insights into the expected effects of the European Union Emissions Trading System (EU ETS)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,27,16,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The first scheme (EU ETS 1), introduced in 2005 and targeting specific industrial sectors, is similar to our tax on firms, denoted as τ f. In contrast, the upcoming extension (EU ETS 2, also known as Phase 4), scheduled for 2027 and covering sectors not included in the initial phase – primarily goods directly consumed by households – is more analogous to our tax on households, τ h. In Figure 7, we set τ h or τ f such that, at the final steady state, total emissions are reduced by 10% compared to the initial steady state. This represents a ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 26"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","carbon tax increase by e149 per ton of CO2eq for households, and by e117 for firms.13 At the peak of the EU ETS 1 in 2023, the price of a ton of CO2 reached e100, which translates into an 8.5% decrease in total emissions in our model, assuming EU ETS 1 covers all direct emissions by firms."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","For the future EU ETS 2, the first three years will include a price containment mechanism, whereby if the price exceeds e45, additional allowances may be released."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","According to our simulations, this maximal price translates into a 3% decrease in total emissions, provided the EU ETS 2 extension covers all direct household emissions."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Therefore, assuming a price of e100 for both the current EU ETS and its extension, and assuming they cover all direct emissions from both firms and households, our model predicts a decline of 15% in total emissions, and a welfare cost equal to −28% of initial disposable income (or a one-time equivalent of −e9, 850, or −e631 per year)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Our primary objective is to quantify the redistributive effects of carbon taxes.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","To this end, we have calibrated the model using relatively low elasticities of substitution, which reflect households’ and firms’ limited short-run ability to adapt."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Appendix F, we re-run the main experiments using alternative values for key elasticities (σ, σy, ϵy, ϵh, δH)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We find that the distributional results are largely robust to changes in σy, ϵy, and δH: average welfare losses remain around −17% (in wealth equivalent terms), with rural households facing losses about 20% higher than those in Paris, and households in the bottom income quintile (Q1) experiencing welfare losses 45% higher than those in the top quintile (Q5)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",The elasticity of substitution between fossil fuels and electricity for households (ϵh) plays a somewhat more significant role.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Reducing ϵh from 1.5 to 1.3 increases overall welfare losses by 3.2 percentage points, as it becomes harder to substitute fossil energy with electricity."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The rural/Paris welfare gap also widens slightly, from 18.3% to 18.8%, due to rural households’ greater dependence on fossil fuels."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The most influential parameter is σ, the elasticity of substitution between energy and other consumption goods."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Increasing σ from 0.2 to 0.4 reduces the average welfare cost of carbon taxation by half and significantly lowers the rural/Paris disparity.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As energy becomes more easily substitutable, differences in baseline energy consumption matter less for welfare outcomes."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","These findings suggest that long-run calibrations, featuring greater flexibility and substitution possibilities, may yield different results."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,15,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","They also highlight that the political acceptability of carbon taxation could be enhanced through policies that facilitate adaptation, such as promoting electric vehicles, improving public transportation, and investing in energy-efficient housing."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,28,16,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In conclusion, due to differences in households’ energy consumption baskets for τ h 13As firms emit more and exhibit greater elasticity of substitution for clean energy, they require lower taxes to reduce emissions by the same amount. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 27"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","and firms’ fossil fuel intensity for τ f, both carbon taxes disproportionately impact rural areas and lower-income households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Migration and housing price adjustments partially mitigate the welfare costs along the geographic dimension.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the following section, we further examine the role of migration and the welfare costs over different time horizons."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","4.2 Migration and welfare In our spatial model, households can migrate subject to a migration cost κ(k, k′), which tends to smooth welfare costs between regions over time."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In this section, we examine the role of migration in shaping the distributive effects of carbon taxes."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Figure 8 illustrates household migration behavior by wealth level and how it changes under our two carbon taxes.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panel a shows that, in the initial steady state, wealthier households are more likely to migrate, due to the presence of fixed migration costs, which disproportionately constrain low-wealth households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panels b and c report changes in migration decisions during the first period of the transition, relative to the initial steady state."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Panel b, following an increase in the household carbon tax (τ h), the 20% poorest households in most regions exhibit no change in their migration probability."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Lacking the resources to afford moving costs, many of these households remain effectively “trapped.”"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Some respond by increasing savings, either to enable future migration or to buffer future consumption losses."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In contrast, middlewealth households (deciles 3 to 5) in rural and small cities often use their accumulated savings to finance relocation."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Panel c shows that the firm-level carbon tax (τ f) induces different mobility dynamics.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Since this tax operates through income effects, such as lower wages or reduced employment, it leads to a broad decline in migration possibilities across wealth groups and cities."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The exception is wealthy households, who have enough wealth to stay mobile and whose labor income is a large share of total income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,29,15,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Figures 18 and 19 in Appendix, we compare mobility matrices at different stages of the transition for both tax scenarios with the mobility matrix of the initial steady state. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 28"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Figure 8: Migration choices by wealth quantiles Notes: Panel a: probability of leaving the region by wealth decile.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Panel b and c: change in migration decisions in the first period of the transition relative to the initial steady state after an increase in τ h and τ f, respectively."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As a result of these decision rules, both taxes leads to significant, but different, migration dynamics between region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Figure 17 in Appendix illustrates population shifts between steady states across both income and geographic dimensions.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Under τ h, the increase in energy price encourages poor households to move away from rural areas and small cities, and they are replaced by richer households who can absorb the increase in prices."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the new steady state, average household income increases by 2.4% in rural areas and 1.1% in small cities relative to the initial equilibrium, but falls by 3% in large cities, highlighting interregional recomposition effects."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The migration dynamics is the opposite for τ f. As wages decline by 4% in small cities compared to only 1% in Paris, high-productivity workers migrate from small to large cities."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","They are replaced by low-productivity households for whom the wage decline has a smaller impact, as transfers constitute a larger share of their income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the new steady state, average income is 5.5% lower in rural areas and 2.8% lower in small cities relative to the initial equilibrium, but increases by 1.6% in medium cities and 4.2% in large cities."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Short-run and long-run welfare effects.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Migration shapes the geographic distributional effects of carbon taxes, but this adjustment takes time, as households need to accumulate savings to cover migration costs or wait for a favorable productivity shock."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As a result, welfare effects differ between short-run and long-run horizons."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,30,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","To approximate short-run welfare effects, we compute welfare over a finite horizon by truncating ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 29"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",the infinite sum of discounted utility.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Formally, for a given T, we solve the following equation: T X T X t=0 βtE0[U no tax i,t |a0 + x, k0, z0] = t=0 βtE0[U tax i,t |a0, k0, z0] and scale the x obtained by total income, as explained above."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Furthermore, to facilitate comparisons, we normalize the “horizon-T wealth equivalent” to have the same mean as the “infinite-horizon WE,” since welfare costs accumulate over time."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",This metric indicates the share of income required to compensate a household for the welfare cost incurred over the first T periods of the transition.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The red and yellow lines in Figure 9 represent the wealth equivalent (WE) for T = 5 years and T = 20 years, while the blue bars correspond to T = ∞. As shown, the distributive effects differ significantly between the short run and the long run."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","At T = 5, welfare losses are significantly higher for rural households than for urban ones, and much lower for poor households than for rich ones."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the short run, rural households bear the cost of carbon taxes but have not yet migrated."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Since regional population adjustments described above have not yet fully occurred, the cost of τ h and τ f is concentrated in the middle of the income distribution, as shown in the decomposition for the 5-year horizon in Figure 16 in Appendix."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This “U-shape” pattern aligns with panel b of Figure 5, which shows that rural households are concentrated in the middle of the income distribution, whereas Parisian households are concentrated at the tails."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,31,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Another reason for this Ushape is the fact that the real interest rate decreases a lot in the first periods, affecting low-income households less. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 30"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Figure 9: Welfare effect with and without migration, and at different horizons Notes: This represents the average welfare change (one-time wealth-equivalent transfer expressed in % of households’ disposable income) for each group over the transition, for different horizons or without migration."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Results of different horizons are scaled for comparison.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Panels a and c: change in τ h only.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Panels b and d: change in τ f only.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Figure 9, we conduct the same experiments as above (keeping the same target of −10% in total emissions) but restrict households from migrating (formally, we set κ = ∞)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The blue bars represent the results of our benchmark with migration, while the black dashed line reflects the scenario without migration."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We observe that, although migration does not significantly affect welfare costs across the income dimension, it substantially reduces disparities along the geographical dimension."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Without migration, rural areas face welfare costs equal to −42% WE of initial disposable income for τ h and −19% WE for τ f, compared to −30% WE and −12% WE with migration."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The opposite effect is observed in large cities: they attract households from rural areas seeking to avoid the carbon tax, which pushes housing rents up, and real wages down."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Therefore, welfare costs in Paris are significantly higher with migration than without."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",These results highlight the critical role of migration in shaping the distributional effects of carbon taxes.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,32,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In conclusion, we have shown that the cost of the carbon transition for houseECB Working Paper Series No 3104 31"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","holds heavily depends on income, geography, and the type of taxes."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Rural areas and poor households tend to experience higher losses compared to urban and wealthy households.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Migration plays a significant role in shaping and smoothing these losses across the geographic dimension.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, the population recomposition within regions occurs gradually, implying that geographic disparities are more pronounced in the short run than in the long run."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",5 Optimal transfer policies The distributive effects of carbon taxation are key for its political acceptability.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Our positive analysis in Section 4 showed that poor and rural households are more affected by carbon taxes, making them more likely to oppose them or protest, as illustrated by the Yellow Vest movement in France."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In this section, we address the normative question of the optimal use of carbon tax revenue through targeted lump-sum transfers."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Our fiscal system offers multiple ways to recycle the revenue, such as lowering existing taxes or investing in measures to mitigate incompressible energy consumption."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","However, we argue that transfers are essential for communication and political acceptability."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","By explicitly separating carbon tax revenue from the state budget, transfers make clear that the tax aims to influence behavior rather than finance public deficits."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We consider four scenarios, each targeting a 10% ex-post reduction in emissions between the initial and final steady states."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We assume both taxes are equal, i.e. τ h = τ f (in Appendix E, we also consider scenarios with τ h = τ f)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","The transfer rule in each scenario is the following14:         0 Scenario 1: Benchmark G 1 Scenario 2: Uniform T(yi, k) = CTR · µ · y−x i Scenario 3: Income        µ · y−xk i Scenario 4: Income × Geography where T is the transfer, yi the total household’s income, CTR the carbon tax revenue, and µ the scaling parameter15."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the “Benchmark G” scenario, the carbon tax revenue is used to increase public spending G (that are not valued by households), with transfers set to zero."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,14,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the 14We also computed results for the additive rule T(y, k) = (xk + y−x) · CTR · µ, but found that it yields a lower welfare than scenario 4."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,15,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Moreover, in Appendix E, we consider an alternative rule to account for progressivity."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,16,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",15Total income: y = Γ(zwl) + (1 −τ k)ra + ¯T. Carbon tax revenue: CTR = τ h(1 + τ VAT)F h + τ f(F y + F N).
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,33,17,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Scaling parameter: µ = 1/ R i y−xk i. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 32
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","“Uniform” scenario, all households receive the same transfer."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the “Income” scenario, we find the optimal value16 of x to maximize welfare, as defined in Section 4."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This scenario assumes the government knows household income and can implement a progressive transfer (if x > 0) but cannot differentiate based on location k (or is legally restricted from doing so, as in France)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, in the “Income × Geography” scenario, we optimize over five different xk, allowing the government to apply region-specific progressivity levels during the transition."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Table 2, we show the median welfare for each scenario, by location and by income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We choose the median welfare as we are interested in the political acceptability of carbon taxes.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In Appendix E, we show that we obtain the same qualitative results taking average welfare as a target, using Negishi weights, or with alternative rebate formulas."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Table 2: Median welfare change by location and income Scenario Rural Small Medium Large Paris All (1) Benchmark G −17.3 −17.4 −15.4 −15.3 −14.5 −16.1 (2) Uniform 6.4 6.7 7.9 10.3 7.3 7.3 (3) Income 7.5 7.5 10.1 13.3 10.4 9.4 (4) Income × Geography 7.5 7.9 13.4 24.1 11.8 10.1 Q1 Q2 Q3 Q4 Q5 All (1) Benchmark G −18.2 −19.1 −17.7 −15.3 −12.8 −16.1 (2) Uniform 20.3 12.5 7.21 3.0 0.9 7.3 (3) Income 66.7 26.5 6.2 −2.0 −0.7 9.4 (4) Income × Geography 94.8 31.7 7.5 −1.3 0.1 10.1 Notes: This represents the median welfare change (one-time wealth-equivalent transfer expressed in % of households’ disposable income) for each group over the transition, for different rebating policies."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Our benchmark scenario yields welfare losses, as the revenue is used to increase G that is not valued by households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","This is the most natural choice to study the distributive effects of carbon taxation, as introducing G in the utility function would directly affect inequality and distort the analysis."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Therefore, replacing this inefficient use of carbon tax revenue with a uniform transfer naturally yields a higher utility: the comparison is more relevant for the distributive effects, and the welfare ratios between areas or income quintiles."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Moreover, for our calibration, transfers are welfare-improving: a uniform transfer policy increases median welfare by 7.3% (WE)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As transfers redistribute resources from high-income households with low marginal utility to low-income households with high marginal utility, they increase aggregate utilitarian welfare (in 16Scenario 3: x = 2.15."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,34,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Scenario 4: xk = [2.07, 2.08, 2.38, 2.4, 2.27]. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 33"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Appendix E, we also use Negishi weights to neutralize the redistribution motive)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Additionally, they mitigate inefficiencies arising from the borrowing constraint by providing some insurance to low-income households, which further increases welfare."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Hence, the welfare gains stem from the model’s baseline calibration, and the relevant comparison is between our different transfer scenarios, not between T and G. Although a uniform transfer raises median welfare, an optimal progressive transfer targeting low-income households yields a 29% higher welfare gain (raising WE +9.4%), at the expense of high-income groups."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","However, as shown in Appendix Table 13, the “Income” scenario also generates welfare losses for 24.2% of households, primarily in rural areas and small cities."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",These are primarily high-income households who do not benefit from the progressive transfer yet still bear the tax burden.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We therefore introduce the “Income × Geography” scenario, which allows transfer progressivity to vary by region."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Relative to the income-only rule, this policy improves welfare by increasing progressivity in large cities and reducing it in rural areas and small towns."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",It raises median welfare across all income and geographic groups and reduces the share of households experiencing welfare losses by 10 percentage points compared to the income-only scenario.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","These gains stem from the fact that rural households are concentrated around the middle of the income distribution, whereas the lowestand highest-income households are overrepresented in large urban areas."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",Allowing for region-specific progressivity better aligns transfers with local income profiles and reduces the dispersion of welfare gains.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Figure 10 presents the 25th, 50th and 75th percentiles of the welfare gain distribution within each income quantile."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,11,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Compared to the income-only policy, the Income × Geography rule notably reduces the dispersion of welfare gains within the bottom quintile (Q1)."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,12,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As a result, incorporating geographic variation into redistribution policies raises median and average welfare gains by 7.4% (+10.1% WE) and 7.6% (+29.8% WE), respectively, relative to the optimal transfer based solely on income."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,35,13,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","While this policy does not increase median welfare in rural areas overall, it redistributes gains toward middleand high-income households within those areas. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 34"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Figure 10: Distribution of welfare gains within income quintiles Notes: This represents the 25th, 50th and 75th percentiles of the welfare gain distribution (onetime wealth-equivalent transfer expressed in % of households’ disposable income) within each income quintile over the transition, for different transfer policies."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","As shown in Figure 20 in the Appendix, our different rebating rules yield different migration and composition effects across income groups and regions."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In the “Income” scenario, many high-income households migrate from rural and small areas to medium and large cities, while lower-income households move in the opposite direction due to declining rents."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In contrast, this effect is mitigated in the “Income × Geography” scenario: since transfers are less progressive in rural and small areas and more progressive in medium and large cities, rich households from rural areas and poor households from urban regions have fewer incentives to migrate."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We show that it is possible to reduce emissions while mitigating the welfare losses associated with the green transition.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","By implementing transfers based on income and location, the share of households experiencing welfare losses can be reduced, thereby enhancing the political acceptability of carbon taxes."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","6 Conclusion In this paper, we study the distributive effects of carbon taxation with a focus on spatial heterogeneity."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Using both household-level surveys and matched employer-employee data from France, we document that rural households consume 2.8 times more fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,8,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",These patterns are consistent across other countries.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,9,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We incorporate these findings into a spatial heterogeneous agent model, featuring idiosyncratic income risks, endogenous savings, and migration choices, as well as segmented housing and labor markets, and local energy expenditure shares for both households and firms."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,36,10,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Our approach contributes to bridging a gap between spatial models, which emphasize migration decisions, and heterogeneous-agent models that analyze inequality and wealth accumulation. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 35"
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,0,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",We find that rural households bear a disproportionate burden from carbon taxation.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,1,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","In our benchmark scenario, their welfare losses are 20% higher than those of Parisian households."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,2,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Ignoring spatial heterogeneity in income-based transfer policies reduces welfare gains by 7%, a result robust to different welfare criteria and rebate schemes."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,3,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","These findings highlight a key policy implication: geographical location must be explicitly accounted for when designing carbon tax frameworks, particularly as the EU-ETS2 for household heating and transport becomes operational in 2027."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,4,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.",This work opens several avenues for future research.
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,5,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","We focus on optimal transfer policies, as they play a central role in addressing distributional concerns and enhancing political feasibility."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,6,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","However, future studies could explore alternative uses of carbon tax revenues within our framework, such as reducing distortionary taxes or financing clean technologies."
ecb.wp3104~57cada2ed7.en.pdf,37,7,Geography versus income: the heterogeneous effects of carbon taxation,"Charles Labrousse, Yann Perdereau",D:20250826080322+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"The distributive effects of carbon taxation are critical for its political acceptability and depend on both income and geographic factors. Using French administrative data, household surveys, and matched employer-employee records, we document that rural households spend 2.8 times more on fossil fuels than urban households and are employed in firms that emit 2.7 times more greenhouse gases. We incorporate these insights into a spatial heterogeneous-agent model with endogenous migration and wealth accumulation, linking spatial and macroeconomic approaches. After an increase in carbon taxes, we quantify that rural households face 20% higher welfare losses than urban households. In an optimal revenue-recycling exercise, we compare transfers targeting income and geography, and show that neglecting for geography reduces welfare gains by 7%. We conclude that carbon policies should account for spatial differences to improve political feasibility.","Finally, our findings indicate that different forms of carbon taxation generate distinct migration responses, highlighting the need for further empirical research. ECB Working Paper Series No 3104 36"
