ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Third, I study analytically the main mechanism through two channels: the insurance channel and the factor price channel."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The first captures the variation of state-dependent fiscal multipliers due to different levels of assets in steady-state, while the second primitively the fact that households face different interest rates."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",These two channels are used to explain the changes in MPCs due to higher government bonds.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",I decompose the two channels to a first order.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Decomposing changes in the iMPCs into components that depend on wealth distribution (insurance channel) and policy functions (factor price channel), I show that fiscal policy is less effective when the government issues more bonds, as this increases the real interest rate and diminishes the impact of fiscal interventions."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Changes in real interest rates emerge as the dominant channel explaining the varying state-dependent fiscal multipliers.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",I corroborate these results using different fiscal rules and isolating the tax changes.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The empirical contribution of the paper fills the gap in a growing reduced-form literature, that has shown that the effect of discretionary spending depends on fiscal space (Ilzetzki et al. ("
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2013), Cho and Rhee (2023)): they document that high public-debt ratios are associated with weaker output responses or with higher crowding-out of private demand."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Existing estimates, however, typically rely on aggregate VARs or narrative shocks without distinguishing whether the debt is held by residents or foreigners, and without tracing the micro-economic channel through which debt matters."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",This paper shows that domestically held debt is the relevant state variable.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The central contribution of the paper is to trace the entire causal chain—from higher debt to higher real rates, from lower MPCs to weaker multipliers—within a single, data-consistent framework."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,13,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Although some of the individual links are known - Woodford (1990) and Aiyagari and McGrattan (1998) describe government bonds as private liquidity, and Auclert et al. ("
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,14,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",2024) show that MPCs are sufficient statistics for fiscal transmission—no study before has shown theoretically and quantitatively how the links fit together.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,15,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Doing so matters for policy: it tells us that the obstacle to effective stimulus in high-debt economies is not crowding out of private investment per se, but the general equilibrium rise in households government bonds holdings and in the return to saving that tilts household budgets toward postponing consumption."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,16,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Taken together, the empirical and theoretical results trace the full mechanism something that, to my knowledge, has not been demonstrated in a unified framework."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,17,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The following new main contribution is theoretical and methodological.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,18,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Using sequence-space Jacobians I decompose the change in the aggregate MPC between the lowand high-debt steady states into two primitives.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,6,19,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The factor-price channel captures how real interest rates ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 5
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",alter each individual’s consumption-saving decision at a fixed asset distribution.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The insurance channel captures how the distribution of liquid assets shifts, holding policy functions constant."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",In the quantified model more than four-fifths of the fall in the aggregate MPC is accounted for by the factor-price channel.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The distributional effect is small because newly issued bonds are disproportionately purchased by households that already hold sizeable assets and therefore exhibit low MPCs, so little of the extra debt migrates to the high-MPC margin."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The concept of the fiscal multiplier, a summary statistic of the effectiveness of fiscal policy in stimulating economic activity, has been a subject of extensive research in macroeconomics."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","However, as highlighted by Ramey (2011) and Ramey (2019), there is no singular ”fiscal multiplier.”"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Instead, its magnitude can vary depending on several factors, including the type and size of policy change, economic conditions, and characteristics of the economy where the policy is implemented."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Woodford (1990) shows that public debt can act as a form of private liquidity.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",His idea challenges traditional views of government debt as a burden on future generations and instead suggests that it can serve as a valuable asset for private agents.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",He argues that government debt provides liquidity services to private agents by serving as a store of value and a means of payment.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",This liquidity function is crucial for facilitating transactions and smoothing consumption over time.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",He discusses the implications of his analysis for monetary policy.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",He suggests that central banks should consider the role of government debt in influencing liquidity conditions when formulating policy decisions.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,13,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Aiyagari and McGrattan (1998) investigate the optimal quantity of government debt within a heterogeneous agent model framework, incorporating individual income risk, borrowing constraints, and precautionary savings."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,14,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","They challenge the traditional view that government debt should be minimized, demonstrating that a positive amount of government debt can enhance welfare by providing liquidity, thereby enabling better consumption smoothing for individuals facing income uncertainty."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,15,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Their analysis highlights that the optimal debt level balances the benefits of liquidity against the costs associated with higher taxes needed to service the debt.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,16,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","From the empirical side, recently Cho and Rhee (2023), using data from 24 OECD countries, find that fiscal policy is generally ineffective in high-debt economies but effective in low-debt economies, highlighting the importance of labor market stimulation for effective fiscal stimulus."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,17,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Additionally, they show that aged economies experience negligible fiscal policy benefits regardless of debt levels, while non-aged economies benefit positively from fiscal policy in low-debt conditions but suffer negative effects in high-debt situations."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,7,18,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",2022) explores the relationship between fiscal multipliers and the proportion of public debt ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 6
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",It posits that fiscal expansions can enhance domestic economic activity but may also cause crowding-out effects if domestic consumption and investment decline due to debt acquisition.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","These crowding-out effects are mitigated when governments sell debt to foreign investors, leading to larger fiscal multipliers."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Auerbach and Gorodnichenko (2012a), McKay and Reis (2016), Ramey and Zubairy (2018) and Berge et al. ("
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2021) further explore how the effectiveness of fiscal policy can vary depending on the economic environment, including the stage of development, exchange rate regime, and openness of the economy."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",2023) explore how expansionary fiscal policy influences the liquidity premium—the difference in returns between public debt and less liquid assets.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Using an estimated HANK model, the authors show that increased public debt enhances private-sector liquidity, thereby stabilizing fixed-capital investment."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","They further quantify the long-term impact of higher public debt, finding minimal crowding out of capital but a significant reduction in the liquidity premium, which raises the fiscal cost of debt."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The study also indicates that the optimal level of public debt, which maximizes revenue, has increased to 60% of US GDP since 2010."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",2016) and Brinca et al. (
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",2021) analyze the size and variability of fiscal multipliers depending on various characteristics of the country.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",They find multipliers depend on the fraction of the population facing binding credit constraints and the economy’s average wealth level.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The study also reveals significant cross-country differences in multiplier effects due to variations in economic structures and fiscal positions.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Antunes and Ercolani (2020) find that the tightening of the household borrowing constraint over time can substantially magnify the government spending multiplier by strengthening the negative wealth effect on labor supply induced by the fiscal stimulus.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,13,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Gorton and Ordonez (2022) find that the supply of government bonds discourages information acquisition about the heterogenous underlying qualities of private safe assets, improving their safety, crowding out the creation of private safe assets, but crowding in their safety."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,14,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The optimal supply of government bonds should factor in the dual role of intraand intertemporal smoothing and their impact on the quantity and safety of private assets.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,15,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Moreover, the literature on state-dependent fiscal multipliers has emerged, aiming to elucidate how the effectiveness of fiscal policy varies under different economic conditions."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,16,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Studies by Blanchard and Perotti (2002), Mountford and Uhlig (2009), Fern´andez-Villaverde et al. ("
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,17,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2011), Ilzetzki et al. ("
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,18,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2013), Woodford (2011) and Eggertsson (2011) have contributed valuable insights into the determinants and implications of state-dependent fiscal multipliers."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,8,19,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","These works highlight the importance of accounting for economic conditions, nominal rigidities, and the zero lower bound on nominal interest rates in assessing the ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 7"
