ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,10,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The value of β1, the value of the response of output after a fiscal shock follows the 1Domestic debt is intended to be the public debt held by domestic households."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,10,13,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",2More details about the controls choice can be found inside appendix A.2. ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 9
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","estimates available in the literature.3 The value of β2, statistically significant at the 95% level, shows that for a state where the level of debt is higher, the state-dependent fiscal multiplier is smaller."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","To explain the meaning of the negative response, it is possible to substitute standard values of the debt/GDP ratio inside the formula β1h + β2h ∗dt−1."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","A level of debt/GDP of 150 % gives rise to a state-dependent fiscal multiplier of 0.41, while a lower level of debt/GDP of 50 % gives a multiplier of 0.73."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Figure 1: β2h impulse response function over 6 quarters, with 68% confidence intervals."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The interaction term shows how GDP responds to government consumption in states with low versus high debt-to-GDP ratios.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The US reacts differently to changes in government expenditure depending on the prevailing level of the debt-to-GDP ratio.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Specifically, states with higher debt-to-GDP ratios exhibit weaker GDP growth responses than states with lower debt-to-GDP ratios."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2.3 Panel Data Framework In this section, I present the results for the following panel data estimation, for a panel of OECD countries, using the data from Broner et al. ("
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2022): 3The baseline coefficient around 0.8 at a one-year horizon is squarely within the range reported by Auerbach and Gorodnichenko (2012a), Ramey and Zubairy (2018) for narrative or shock-identified U.S. government-purchase multipliers."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,11,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Those studies typically find cumulative multipliers between 0.6 and 1.0 during normal (non-recession) periods, providing a useful benchmark for the interpretation of β1h in our specification. ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 10"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","h ∑ j=0 gi,t+j × di,t−1!"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","h ∑ j=0 yi,t+j = αi,h + γt,h + β1h h ∑ j=0 gi,t+j + β2h + K ∑ k=1 δk,hXi,t−k + ϵi,t+j (2) It is a one-step IV estimation, as for the time series of the US, to recover fiscal multipliers a la Ramey and Zubairy (2018)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The state-dependent fiscal multipliers are identified through interaction with domestic debt share and the narrative shocks used are announcement of fiscal shocks from Guajardo et al. (
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Figure 2 presents the estimated impulse responses for countries classified into “Low Domestic Debt” and “High Domestic Debt” states, as well as the corresponding difference between the two."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The low-debt group is defined as those country-periods in the bottom decile of domestic debt shares, while the high-debt group is defined as those above the ninetieth percentile."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The blue line in the left panel represents the cumulative GDP response in the lowdebt state, with dashed lines indicating the corresponding 90% confidence interval."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The green line, similarly plotted with its dashed confidence band, shows the corresponding response for the high-debt state."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In each case, the horizon on the horizontal axis extends from one to four years following the fiscal shock."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","A clear pattern emerges from the left panel: at each horizon, the estimated multiplier for the high-debt state is below that of the low-debt state, implying a systematically weaker output effect of fiscal expansions when domestic debt is higher."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The difference plot on the right confirms this visually, with the black solid line lying below zero for all horizons and thus indicating that the multiplier in the high-debt state is statistically lower than the multiplier in the low-debt state at conventional levels of significance."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",These findings align with the narrative that rising domestic debt positions reduce the marginal impact of government spending on output.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,12,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","To explain why the state-dependent fiscal multiplier is smaller for higher values of debt held inside a country, and how this is related to household decisions, in the next section I build a state-of-art HANK model to understand the mechanism. ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 11"
