ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,23,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Figure 6: Distribution of assets and MPC for high debt SS.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,23,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",5.3 Aggregate responses The aggregate responses to a 1% increase of the government consumption of the steady-state will allow to understand the reaction in the economy after a shock.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,23,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The increase in public expenditure is fully financed by an increase in government debt in the same magnitude.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,23,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This means that the public debt stock, Bt is allowed to increase during the transition."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,23,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The figure 8 show how both the impact and cumulative multiplier decreases for higher level of bonds ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 22
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,24,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Figure 7: Income states and assets distribution of the 2 economies.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,24,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","An higher level of debt in the economy means, after an unexpected government shock, that the multipliers will be smaller."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,24,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In terms of size, compared to the empirical evidence, the size of the multipliers fall is smaller."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,24,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In particular there is a bigger decrease for a small amount of debt/GDP ratio, as the kink in the picture suggests."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,24,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Figure 8: Plot of impact and fiscal multipliers for different levels of public debt in the HANK economy.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,24,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Figure 9 illustrates the impulse response functions of different aggregate variables: public and private consumption, output, after-tax income, labor demand, and government debt response. ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 23"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Figure 9: Impulse response functions for government expenditure, output, consumption, after-tax income, labor, and wage, for an economy with the 80% of debt/GDP ratio."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Under the fiscal experiment explained in section 3.8, the impact fiscal multiplier is 0.81 when the level of public debt is 20 %, and it is 0.74 when the level of debt is 100 %."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The cumulative multipliers in the first 4 quarters also change and are very close to the impact multipliers.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","To understand the underlying mechanism it is crucial to focus on the marginal propensity to consume (MPC) of agents, as anticipated in the preceding sections."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","A higher level of debt translates to greater asset holdings and consequently, smaller MPCs among households."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This diminished aggregate MPC leads to a lower aggregate demand response following the shock, resulting in a weaker employment response."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The impulse response functions reveal that following an increase in government expenditure financed by public expenditure, output levels are higher in scenarios with lower debt burdens."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","After the shock, lower debt prompts agents to exhibit more pronounced adjustments in their labor response, thereby driving a more substantial change in aggregate labor demand."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","As a result, wage levels rise more sharply."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","While the interest rate and wage initially rise post-shock, the adjustment is more pronounced in contexts with lower debt burdens, showing a stronger negative impact."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This dynamic ultimately influences consumption behavior, with agents exhibiting a greater propensity to consume in instances of lower debt, amplifying the output response."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",These results show why the impact and cumulative fiscal multiplier are diminished in scenarios characterized by higher debt.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Conversely, agents exhibit less reactive behavior in situations of heightened debt, as they rely on government debt as a buffer against aggregate shocks."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,25,13,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Given their enhanced liquidity and reduced constraints, their behavioral adjustments are comparatively subdued. ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 24"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",5.3.1 Impulse Responses in the Two Regimes - Comparison Figure 10 compares the impulse responses under the two regimes.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In the deficit-financed case, government debt Bt rises sharply on impact, then gradually returns."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Taxes adjust slowly, leaving consumption higher initially but inducing a more persistent decline as taxes eventually rise."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","By contrast, in the balanced-budget scenario, there is no large change in Bt, and taxes τt Figure 10: Impulse response functions for government expenditure, consumption, after-tax income, taxes, and assets/government bonds for an economy with the 40% of debt/GDP ratio."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",At the top (first row) is the one for the balanced-budget exercise; at the bottom (second row) is the one for the deficit-financed.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",jump immediately to fund Gt.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Consumption thus drops more on impact, though the subsequent drag from increased taxes is shorter lived."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Quantitatively, the deficit-financed shock produces a larger stimulus to aggregate demand and output in the short run, at the cost of higher future taxes and greater debt dynamics."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Stock versus Flow of Public Debt.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",A key aspect of the analysis is the distinction between the stock of public debt—captured by the calibration of different steady states—and the flow of debt resulting from fiscal expansions.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,26,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In this model, the stock of debt is a predetermined state variable: different steady states correspond to different equilibrium levels of debt holdings by households, influencing real interest rates and marginal propensities to consume (MPCs) ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 25"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Figure 11: Sensitivity of the fiscal multipliers to the elasticity of the labor supply.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",These long-run levels of government debt shape the economic environment in which fiscal policy operates.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","By contrast, the flow of debt is represented by the fiscal shock itself and the associated deficit-financed response—i.e., the temporary increase in government spending and the corresponding increase in government bonds issued along the transition."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The model therefore separately identifies how the background debt environment (stock) conditions the impact of a new round of debt issuance (flow).
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This is key for understanding state-dependent fiscal multipliers: the same flow shock (fiscal impulse) yields different output responses depending on the initial stock of debt, precisely because the level of public debt influences both the interest rate and the consumption-saving decisions of households."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",This two-tier mechanism underpins the core mechanism of the paper and connects the empirical and theoretical parts in a unified framework.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",5.3.2 A Classic Mechanism Figure 11 shows a classic explanation of the mechanism from the literature on fiscal multipliers.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Impact multipliers are computed in the model for different levels of Frisch, the parameter that determines the elasticity of labor supply."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","When the labor supply is fully elastic, the multipliers decrease by more, and there is a more accentuated slope in the diminishing behavior of the multipliers."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The red line shows the same impact multipliers computed in the main exercise, as in figure 8."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","When the labor supply is inelastic, the multipliers do not change."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,27,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This confirms a channel already present in the literature, showing how labor supply elasECB Working Paper Series No 3106 26"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,28,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",ticity matters for fiscal multipliers.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,28,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","However, from this analysis, it is still not possible to distinguish the main fundamental channel driving the results."
