ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,41,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",2014) narrative fiscal shocks as instruments.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,41,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",A.2 Empirical Specification and Results A.2.1 Alternative Specification The empirical model specification is as follows: lyt+h = αt+h + β1hlgt + β2h(lst ∗dt−1) + controls + ϵt+h where ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 40
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,42,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Figure 16: β2 impulse response function over 8 horizons, with 90 % confidence intervals. •"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,42,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The horizons h of the local projection are 8 • g is instrumented by s ∗dt−1 • l = log, y = rGDP, g = government expenditure, s = Ramey shock, d = private debt/GDP ratio The set of controls is the following: 2 ∑ k=1 β3hyt−k + 2 ∑ k=1 β4hlgt−k + 2 ∑ k=1 β5h(lgt−k ∗dt)+ 2 ∑ k=1 β6h(lgt−k ∗dt−1) + 2 ∑ k=1 β7hs + 2 ∑ k=1 β8hdt−1 A.2.2 Detailed Specification and Estimation for the panel data The estimation follows a single-step instrumental-variables (IV) approach to identify the cumulative response of GDP at each horizon."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,42,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In the main specification, we define country i to be in a “Low Domestic Debt” state when its domestic debt share is below the 10th percentile of the cross-country distribution, and in a “High Domestic Debt” state when that share lies above the 90th percentile."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,42,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Let yi,t be real GDP normalized by potential, and let ∆CAPBi,t be changes in the cyclically adjusted primary balance, also normalized by potential GDP, serving ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 41"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",as the core fiscal variable.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The local projection equation at horizon h sums forward both yi,t+m and ∆CAPBi,t+m from m = 0 to m = h, thereby yielding a direct estimate of the cumulative multiplier."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This design follows the reasoning of Ramey and Zubairy (2018), who demonstrate that a single-regression approach for cumulative outcomes is numerically equivalent to summing across multiple horizon-specific regressions, while also providing a direct measure of the standard error for the total multiplier."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Countries are pooled in a panel regression with both year and country fixed effects, and controls for lagged GDP or CAPB if desired."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Heteroskedasticityand autocorrelation-robust standard errors are computed at the country level.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The identifying assumption relies on narrative fiscal shocks (from Guajardo et al.,"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2014 or analogous sources) as instruments for ∆CAPBi,t, ensuring exogeneity of policy changes."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The interaction with the highor lowdomestic-debt indicator captures the potential state dependence: the coefficient on that interaction measures how the fiscal multiplier differs when domestic debt levels are high, and the plotted IRFs in Figure 2 evaluate those multipliers at the relevant percentile thresholds."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The resulting difference in the right panel visually displays the extent to which higher domestic debt holdings diminish the output response to policy expansions.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The vector of controls Xt−k includes: • Two lags of log real GDP, {yt−1, yt−2}, to capture output persistence. •"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Two lags of log government purchases, {gt−1, gt−2}, to absorb predictable spending dynamics. •"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Interactions of those same lags with both the contemporaneous debt ratio and its lag, {gt−k × dt, gt−k × dt−1}, so that state-dependence in spending behaviour does not confound the multiplier estimate. •"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Two lags of the shock series, {st−1, st−2}, since the defence-news announcements exhibit serial correlation. •"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,13,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Two lags of the debt ratio, {dt−1, dt−2}, to control for mean reversion in debt and slowmoving fiscal trends."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,43,14,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","These controls follow the designs in Ramey (2011), Ramey and Zubairy (2018) and Broner et al. ("
