ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Truncating M generally implies that q′(I −M) ̸= 0, since the “tents” corresponding to the final columns of M (see figure 3) are incomplete."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The approach I choose is to directly solve for the multiplier matrix M, numerically computing M = A−1K, where A is the asset Jacobian, whose elements are given by Ats = ∂At ∂Zs."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",After obtaining A either directly using the methods from Auclert et al. (
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","2021), or indirectly from M using A = K(I −M)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Then, given M, form dY = M(dG −MdT) (A.21) E Formalization of the relationship between fiscal multipliers and the level of government debt."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","To formally derive the relationship between fiscal multipliers and the level of public debt in the HANK model described in the paper, I start by considering how the household sector’s consumption and savings decisions interact with government debt."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","As government debt increases, it introduces more bonds into the economy, which can alter households’ consumption behavior via their MPC."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The key mechanism at play is how higher levels of debt influence interest rates and asset holdings, which in turn affect fiscal multipliers."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",E.1 Household Optimization Problem Households maximize utility over consumption and labor.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,46,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The household problem is given by: ( c1−σ it 1 −σ −φ n1+η it 1 + η + βEtVt+1(eit+1, ait) ) Vt(eit, ait−1) = max cit,ait subject to the budget constraint: cit + ait = (1 + rt)ait−1 + eit Wt Pt Nt −τt Given the recursive structure of the problem, the consumption function ct = C(at−1, et) ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 45"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","can be derived as a function of past asset holdings at−1, labor income et, and interest rates rt."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","E.2 Intertemporal Marginal Propensity to Consume (iMPC) The marginal propensity to consume (MPC) is defined as the derivative of consumption with respect to income: iMPCit = ∂cit ∂yit (26) In the HANK framework, households face idiosyncratic risk and income heterogeneity, so the iMPC varies across households based on their asset holdings and income."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Crucially, as public debt increases and more bonds are held by households, aggregate savings rise, and the distribution of wealth shifts."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In general, wealthier households (those with more bonds) tend to have lower iMPCs due to the diminishing marginal utility of consumption: iMPC decreases as ait increases."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Thus, when government debt increases, leading to more bonds (assets) in the economy, the aggregate MPC declines."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Consider a bounded shock {dGt, dTt} to government spending Gt and taxes Tt."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The impulse response of output dY is governed by: dY = dG −M · dT + M · dY where M ≡[Mts] is the infinite matrix of partial derivatives: Mts = ∂Ct ∂Ys Solving for dY: dY = (I −M)−1 (dG −M · dT) E.3 Government Budget Constraint and Debt The government’s budget constraint is given by: τt = (1 + rt)Bt−1 + Gt −Bt.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In a deficit-financed fiscal expansion, government spending Gt increases, and this is matched by an increase in debt Bt."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,47,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",The fiscal multiplier measures the effect of this increase in governECB Working Paper Series No 3106 46
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Specifically, the fiscal multiplier is defined as the change in output (∆Y) in response to a change in government spending (∆G): Impact Multiplier = ∆Y0 ∆G0."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","E.4 Interest Rates and Asset Demand As government debt increases, the real interest rate rt adjusts."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In the model, the steady-state real interest rate r∗increases with higher debt levels because more bonds in the economy raise the demand for assets."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The steady-state asset demand function is increasing in the real interest rate: ∂A(r∗) ∂r∗ > 0, where A(r∗) is the aggregate demand for assets as a function of the real interest rate."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","E.5 Concavity of Consumption Function and Declining MPC The consumption function C(at−1, et) is concave in income due to diminishing marginal utility, which implies that the marginal propensity to consume (MPC) decreases as asset holdings increase."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Mathematically, the MPC is given by: MPC(et, at−1) = ∂C(at−1, et) ∂et."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Given that C(at−1, et) is concave in et, the second derivative of consumption with respect to income is negative: ∂2C(at−1, et) ∂e2 t < 0."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This implies that as at−1 (asset holdings) increases, the MPC decreases."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In other words, wealthier households have a lower MPC because they consume a smaller fraction of additional income: MPC(et, at−1) decreases as at−1 increases."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Formal Statement and Proof Let B be the level of government debt in a heterogeneous-agent model, potentially altering both 1."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,48,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The factor price r(B), which affects individual MPCs, and ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 47"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The wealth distribution ΨB(a, e)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Then, if the distribution does not shift significantly at first order (∂ΨB/∂B ≈0), changes in B mostly change the aggregate MPC agg(B) through the factor-price channel, i.e. via ∂r/∂B, rather than through reweighting Ψ. Proposition 5."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","I have MPCagg(B) = Z MPC(a, e; B) ΨB(a, e)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Take a total derivative with respect to B, using the product rule: d dB MPCagg(B) = Z ∂MPC(a, e; B) ∂B ΨB(a, e) + Z MPC(a, e; B) ∂ΨB(a, e) ∂B. The first term is the policy function (factor-price) channel; the second is the distribution (insurance) channel."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","If ∂ΨB(a, e)/∂B is zero at first order, the second term vanishes and only the factorprice effect remains."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Because raising B typically increases r, it thus directly lowers individual MPCs and the aggregate MPC, dominating the multiplier response to debt."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Recall from the main text that the aggregate MPC in equilibrium with debt B is MPCagg(B) = Z A×E MPC(a, e; B) ΨB(a, e) da de."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","When differentiating with respect to B, I obtain the decomposition: A×E ∂(a, e; B) A×E MPC(a, e; B) ∂ΨB(a, e) d dB h MPCagg(B) i = Z + Z ∂B ΨB(a, e) da de | {z } Policy-Function / Factor-Price Channel ∂B da de | {z } Distribution (Insurance) Channel. ("
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","27) I claim that, under certain conditions, the second integral (the “distribution channel”) is negligible at first order (i.e. of smaller order than the factor-price term)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","E.6 Formal Argument via an Envelope/Approximate Invariance Condition To see why ∂ΨB/∂B can be zero (or very small) at first order, it is convenient to think of ΨB(a, e) as the stationary distribution arising from households’ optimal saving choices under equilibrium factor prices (interest rate r(B), wages w(B)) and equilibrium tax policy (lumpsum taxes τ(B), etc.)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,49,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","I then show that as B changes slightly, the induced shift in the distribution must be of second order (or zero), provided the model setup satisfies certain smoothness and “approximate invariance” conditions. ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 48"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Step 1: Households’ Value Function & Policy Rules.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Let v(a, e; B) be the stationary (or steady-state) value function when the government debt is B. Households’ individual policy function for assets next period, α(a, e; B), and the consumption function c(a, e; B) solve the usual Bellman equation."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Because r(B), w(B), and τ(B) are smooth in B, small changes in B only induce small changes in the household’s Bellman equation."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","As a result, α(a, e; B + ∆B) = α(a, e; B) + O(∆B), c(a, e; B + ∆B) = c(a, e; B) + O(∆B), where O(∆B) denotes terms of order ∆B or higher."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Hence the policy functions (including (a, e; B)) change at first order in B. Step 2: Stationary Distribution as a Fixed Point."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The cross-sectional distribution ΨB(a, e) solves a fixed-point or stationary condition (e.g. a Fokker-Planck equation in continuous time, or a law of motion under Markov transition in discrete time)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Symbolically, ΨB = ΓB ␀ ΨB , where ΓB is the operator that maps any trial distribution into the next period’s (or next instant’s) distribution given the policy rule α(·, ·; B) and the Markov transitions for e. For small ∆B, I consider the new fixed point ΨB+∆B: ΨB+∆B = Γ B+∆B  ΨB+∆B . Because Γ B+∆B is a continuous mapping in B and ΨB is already a fixed point of ΓB, it follows (under typical Lipschitz or contraction conditions) that the change ΨB+∆B −ΨB is of order at most ∆B. In practice, it may be strictly smaller at first order if there is an “approximate invariance” or “envelope” property, as I now detail."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Step 3: The Envelope/Approximate Invariance Argument.
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In many heterogeneous-agent models calibrated to match empirical wealth dispersion, two ingredients often imply minimal shifts in ΨB when B changes: • Lump-Sum Tax Adjustments: Any additional interest cost from higher B is financed via a small, lump-sum tax ∆τ on every individual."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",Lump-sum taxes do not fundamentally re-rank households in the cross-section; they shift everyone’s budget constraint slightly. •
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,50,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Smooth Saving Motives: Agents’ saving rules α(a, e; B) adjust somewhat with r and τ, but ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 49"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",these adjustments can offset each other in such a way that the overall wealth accumulation distribution changes little (at least to first order).
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Under these conditions, one can show (technically via an “envelope theorem” argument at the distribution level) that the linear (first-order) term in ∂ΨB/∂B vanishes or is extremely small.18 Step 4: Conclusion of the Argument."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Thus, for small ∆B, I have Ψ B+∆B(a, e) = ΨB(a, e) + O ␀(∆B)2, and hence, ∂ΨB(a, e) ∂B = 0 + (possible second-order terms)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","This justifies the statement that at first order, A×E MPC(a, e; B) ∂ΨB(a, e) Z ∂B da de ≈0."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,4,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Consequently, the second term in (27) —the “distribution channel”—is negligible at first order, so the effect of changes in B on MPCagg is dominated by the first term, i.e. by changes in factor prices (the “policy-function channel”)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,5,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Factor prices (r, w) and lump-sum taxes (τ) depend smoothly on B, 2."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,6,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Households’ policy functions α(a, e; B) and MPC(a, e; B) are continuous and differentiable in B, 3."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,7,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","The stationary distribution ΨB is determined by a contraction mapping ΓB (or continuous Markov transition) in the usual way, 4."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,8,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Small changes in lump-sum tax τ(B) and factor prices r(B) generate a second-order or negligible first-order change in ΨB, e.g. by an envelope argument."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,9,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Then, to first order in ∆B, ΨB+∆B(a, e) = ΨB(a, e) + O ␀(∆B)2, so ∂ΨB(a, e) ∂B = 0 + (higher order terms)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,10,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","18In continuous-time models, one can write the stationary distribution as the solution to a differential equation (the Fokker-Planck equation)."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,11,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","A small change in (r, τ) that keeps average saving rates roughly unchanged may shift ΨB only at second order."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,51,12,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","In discrete-time Markov chain settings, a similar argument applies by approximating transition kernels in a Taylor expansion around ∆B = 0. ECB Working Paper Series No 3106 50"
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,52,0,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Consequently, the distribution channel A×E MPC(a, e; B) ∂ΨB(a, e) Z ∂B da de is negligible at first order, and the principal effect of B on the aggregate MPC comes through the policy function (factor-price) channel."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,52,1,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","F Formal Structural Derivation of Proposition 5 In this appendix, I formally derive Proposition 5, clearly establishing the dominance of the factor-price channel over the wealth-distribution channel in determining how public debt influences aggregate marginal propensities to consume (MPCs) and consequently fiscal multipliers."
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,52,2,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.",F.1 Setup and Assumptions Consider an economy populated by heterogeneous households facing idiosyncratic productivity shocks and making optimal intertemporal consumption-savings decisions. [
ecb.wp3106~d78ff7ac24.en.pdf,52,3,"Public debt, iMPCs & fiscal policy transmission",Stefano Grancini,D:20250902084546+02'00',D:20160704163336+02'00',"This paper investigates the relationship between public debt and the effectiveness of fiscal policy, presenting evidence of an inverse relationship between government debt and fiscal multipliers. To explain the results, I develop and calibrate a HANK model tailored to the U.S. economy. The model reveals that higher public debt diminishes fiscal multipliers by making households less constrained. Theoretically, I show intertemporal marginal propensities to consume (iMPCs) are sufficient statistics of public debt, influencing fiscal multipliers. Decomposing changes in iMPCs into components driven by wealth distribution and the policy function, I find that the primary factor driving variations in iMPCs is the change in interest rates due to the variation of government bonds. This highlights a novel mechanism: even in the absence of fiscal limits or crowding out, large stocks of debt can weaken fiscal stimulus through their effect on household behavior.","Household Problem] Each household solves the infinite-horizon optimization problem: v(a, e; B) = max c,a′ u(c) + βE[v(a′, e′; B)], subject to the budget constraint: a′ + c = (1 + r(B))a + w(B)e −τ(B), where: • a ∈A ⊆R denotes household assets. •"
