ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,7,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","2023): πi,t+h −πi,t = αi,h +αd,h +βh1Crisisi,t=1 +ϵi,t+h and plots the result of βh and its 90% confidence interval for each integer value of h from -5 to 5."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,7,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Another hypothesis we can consider involves the credibility of central banks.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,7,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","In the aftermath of significant inflation surges, the risk of inflation spiraling out of control and leading to a full fledged de-anchoring of expectations becomes tangible, particularly if the central bank lacks strong credibility (Blinder, 2000)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,7,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","When inflation expectations become de-anchored, the effectiveness of monetary policy interventions is severely effected."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,7,8,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","This can undermine the central bank’s ability to fulfill its critical role as the “lender of last resort”, as bank runs might swiftly escalate into runs on the domestic currency (Kaminsky & Reinhart, 1999)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,7,9,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Consequently, the economy is left without a crucial financial stability backstop (Freixas, 1999; Robatto, 2019; Albertazzi et al.,"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,7,10,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Other factors that might theoretically explain the link between inflation and financial ECB Working Paper Series No 3108 6
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,0,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","stability pertain to the conditions of the banking sector, aligning with the established bank-lending channel paradigm (Kashyap & Stein, 2000)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Specifically, it has been suggested that higher nominal rates resulting from increased inflation could impair banks, either due to the duration gap typical of their balance sheets or through other mechanisms (Agarwal & Baron, 2024)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Empirically testing the causal effects of inflation on financial stability presents a significant challenge, primarily due to the confounding influence of simultaneous interest rate hikes."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","To control for this indirect effect we exploit that, under certain conditions, countries are forced to track the monetary policy of another country."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The monetary policy reaction from an inflationary shock should therefore be experienced across multiple countries.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","By comparing countries with differing inflation outlooks but the same monetary policy, we can gauge the causal effects of inflation on financial stability."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",We extend this methodology to examine whether the effect of inflation is driven by aggregate supply or aggregate demand shocks.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","We do so by relying on a decomposition of inflation into demand and supply shocks, standard in the literature."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,8,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The underlying assumption is that aggregate supply (demand) side inflationary shocks are contractionary (expansionary).
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,9,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","To investigate the inflation-financial stability link further, we also adopt an alternative approach considering exogenous oil supply shocks as an instrument for inflation."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,10,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Finally, to deepen our understanding of the underlying transmission mechanisms, we investigate the heterogeneity of our findings across various relevant dimensions."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,11,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Our findings indicate that inflationary shocks generally increase the probability of financial crises.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,12,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Additionally, this effect holds for inflation arising from both supply and demand-side shocks."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,8,13,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","The effect is economically significant and, in some circumstances, more than offsets ECB Working Paper Series No 3108 7"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,0,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",the indirect one produced by the corresponding hike in interest rates.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The magnitude is significantly exacerbated in episodes where the mortgage-to-GDP ratio is high and wage growth is low.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","This supports the idea that a key transmission channel for this effect involves the redistributive consequences of high inflation, which makes it more challenging for households to meet their debt obligations."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Other factors also tend to amplify the impact, albeit to a lesser extent."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","These include low central bank independence, which we consider as a proxy for its credibility, and a fragile bank-funding structure."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The remainder of the paper is organised as follows.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",We investigate three key strands of literature relevant to inflation and financial instability in Section 2.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,9,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",We go on to describe the dataset we will be using in Section 3 and the methodology in Section 4.
