ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Moreover, the strong first stage results rule out the presence of weak-instrument issues (Table 5)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",5 Results 5.1 Matched pegged-base countries comparison Our results show that a rise in inflation has a direct effect on financial stability above and beyond its indirect effect on monetary policy (Table 4).
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Mirroring the pattern documented in Figure 1, a rise in inflation from t −5 to t −1 significantly predicts crises starting in t to t + 2."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",This effect on the probability of financial crisis is also visible with a narrower crisis window for crises starting exclusively in year t. The results are also robust to the chosen inflation window.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Table B.9 confirms that the key predictor of crises is that inflation rises in the 5 years before a crisis period.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Inflationary shocks may originate from either aggregate demand or aggregate supply fluctuations, with opposite implications for growth."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Therefore, to dissect the direct effect of inflation from the concomitant indirect effect of growth we must exploit our estimated structural demand and supply shocks."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,8,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Both shocks are plotted in Figure B.3, where the rise in supply constraints during the 1970s is clearly visible."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,20,9,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The results indicate that this effect holds regardless of the type of inflation (Table 4).
