ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Below we will investigate the mechanisms underlying our finding about the link between inflation and crisis probability as well as the role played by aggregate demand and supply shocks.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","For now, it is useful to stress how these findings are not in contrast with previous evidence indicating that supply-side inflation intensifies financial stress while demand-side does not, as in Boissay et al. ("
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The focus of those analyses is on the effect of the monetary policy reaction to inflation rather than on the direct effect of inflation.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Table 4: Effect of inflation after controlling for the monetary policy effect Full sample 1870-2000 crisisi,t Shock decomposition ∆4πtrilemma i,t−1 × BASEi,t 0.012*** (0.003) 0.010** (0.005) 0.005* (0.003) demand shock 0.043*** supply shock 0.040** R2 0.030 0.041 0.038 0.045 observations 1463 1262 1463 1290 sample 1870-2020 1870-2000 1870-2020 1884-2020 crisis variable crisisi,(t,t+2) crisisi,(t,t+2) crisisi,t crisisi,(t,t+2) Notes: This table shows the outcome of a linear probability model for a systemic banking crisis."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","In particular, the result from the regression of Equation 1 for varying samples and crisis variables."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","∆4πtrilemma i,t−1 × BASEi,t describes the effect of a four-year change in inflation conditional on the monetary policy, lagged by 1 year."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","crisisi,(t,t+2) is a dummy variable that equals 1 if crisisi,t equals 1 in periods t, t + 1 or t + 2."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,8,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The final column on the right compares the relative impact of demand and supply inflation jointly within the same regression.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,9,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","The demand and supply shock variables represent the 4-year sum of structural shocks (in order to reflect ∆4π) interacted with a dummy for the BASE country: 4 X etrilemma i,t−1 ×BASEi,t, where ei,t are the structural shocks for country i at time t estimated according to Equation 2."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,10,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Significance tests are performed non-parametrically from the simulated parameter estimates that are shown in Figure B.4.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,11,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","For all other regressions, country-clustered standard errors are in parentheses. *, **,"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,21,12,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","and *** indicate significance at the 0.1, 0.05, and 0.01 levels, respectively ECB Working Paper Series No 3108 20"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,0,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","5.2 Instrumented inflation As explained above, a second methodology we follow to explore the inflation and financial stability nexus relies on an IV approach."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Starting with the first stage, as expected and in line with extensive literature on this topic, we find a strong and significant negative effect of oil supply shocks on changes in inflation rates."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Namely, a decrease in the supply of oil leads to an acceleration of prices (Table 5)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","In the second stage, we find that a 1pp increase in exogenous inflation leads to a 4.6pp increase in the crisis probability in the next year."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","This is a quantitatively strong impact; for reference, over this sample, financial crisis years account for roughly 2.9% percent of observations."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","This estimate is higher than that of the matched pegged-base countries comparison, a distinction we elaborate on in Section 7."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Table 5: Instrumenting inflation with oil supply shocks First stage Second stage c ∆πi,t−1 0.046*** (0.017) ϵOIL t -0.060*** (0.012) contemporaneous and 4 lags of ∆R and ∆y Yes contemporaneous and 4 lags of ∆R and ∆y Yes R2 0.23 R2 0.046 F-Stat 23 country fixed effects Yes Prob >F 0.0001 observations 808 Kleibergen-Paap 23.59 sample 1975-2020 country fixed effects Yes observations 808 sample 1975-2020 Notes: This table shows the outcome of a linear probability model for a systemic banking crisis."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,22,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","The left panel refers to the result of the first stage of the 2SLS, defined in Equation 3 whereas the rightward panel refers to the second stage defined in Equation 4."
