ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","As a result, periods of global financial instability, such as 2007-8, cannot simply be removed from the estimation."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Utilising a longer sample, the matched pegged-base countries comparison is able to perform this sub-sample analysis and finds robustness to excluding the 2000-2020 period (Table 4)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",6 Heterogeneity and transmission mechanisms To better understand why inflationary shocks are detrimental to financial stability we may investigate cross-country heterogeneity.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","To do this, we interact instrumented inflation from Equation 4 with a number of other macro-financial variables and indicators capturing features of the domestic financial sector."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",A first channel we explore is inspired by the literature on the redistributive effects of inflation.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","According to this view, inflationary shocks may create financial vulnerabilities for households as real income is cut by the inflation tax and only gradually recovers once second-round effects kick in (Auclert, 2019)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Consistent with this view, we document that countries with previously low wage growth have a greater risk of crisis following a rise in inflation (Table 6)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,8,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",A related additional finding is that the inflation-financial instability link is intensified for countries characterised by a high level of household debt.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,9,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",This corroborates the view that inflation interferes with financial stability by weakening the solvency of the household sector.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,24,10,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Indeed, the regression results indicate that the same conclusion does not carry over to other forms of debt, including corporate and public sector debt. ECB Working Paper Series No 3108 23"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,25,0,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Table 6: The heterogeneous impact of inflation Wage growth Debt Central bank independence d ∆πit−1 0.052*** -0.008 0.044** (0.02) (0.038) (0.02) wage growthit−2 -0.001 (0.001) wage growthit−2 × d ∆πit−1 -0.001** (0.0006) public debtit−2 -0.030 (0.021) mortgagesit−2 0.057 (0.046) corporate debtit−2 0.059* (0.036) public debtit−2 × d ∆πit−1 0.007 (0.019) mortgagesit−2 × d ∆πit−1 0.084*** (0.024) corporate debtit−2 × d ∆πit−1 0.012 (0.010) central bank independenceit−2 0.021 (0.04) central bank independenceit−2 × d ∆πit−1 -0.073* (0.04) R2 0.047 0.076 0.050 observations 808 801 808 Notes: This table shows the outcome of a linear probability model for a systemic banking crisis, defined in Equation 4, where instrumented inflation is interacted with key macro-financial variables, controlling for lags of real per-capita GDP growth and changes in policy rates."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,25,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Central bank independence refers to the financial independence estimate from Romelli (2022, 2024), for which the cross-sectional median is subtracted for each year to remove the trend."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,25,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Public debt refers to the public-debt-to-GDP ratio, corporate debt refers to the corporate debt-to-GDP ratio and business loans refer to the business loans-to-GDP ratio."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,25,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Country-clustered standard errors are in parentheses. *, **,"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,25,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","and *** indicate significance at the 0.1, 0.05, and 0.01 levels, respectively."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,25,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Other factors may also play a role.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,25,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","As introduced in Section 1, it can be theorised that the adverse consequences of inflation on financial stability depend on market expectations about the ability of the central bank to rein in second-round effects, therefore preventing ECB Working Paper Series No 3108 24"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,0,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",a strongly destabilising outright de-anchoring of inflation expectations.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","There is some evidence of this channel, with a low level of central bank financial independence magnifying the inflation’s financial instability effect."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","However, it must be noted that there were no other significant interactions with other components of central bank independence in the dataset by Romelli (2022, 2024)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Finally, additional exercises look into the presence of a bank lending channel in our context."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","One relevant finding in this respect indicates stronger detrimental consequences from inflation depending on domestic banks’ funding structure, in particular for intermediaries with higher funding gaps or reliance on wholesale (unstable) funding (Table B.12)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","7 Conclusions and Policy Considerations Our analysis demonstrates that rises in inflation cause financial instability, confirmed with multiple econometric methodologies."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",The impact is also quantitatively relevant.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","A onestandard deviation shock in the change in inflation (equal to 1.9pp in our sample) explains more than half of the standard deviation the crisis probability (17%), according to our IV estimates."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,8,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",Both supply and demand-side inflation contribute to this instability.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,9,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","These results run counter to the Fisherian ideas of debt deflation.2 Concerning the transmission mechanism at work, our results are inconsistent with the 2As previously explained, Fisher proposed that declines in the price level increase the real value of debt and subsequently debtor insolvency (Fisher, 1933)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,10,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Recent empirical analyses produced results consistent with the Fisherian hypothesis (Brunnermeier et al.,"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,11,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Interestingly, such evidence refers to the experience of German firms during hyper-inflation period of 1919-1923, which epitomises financial instability."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,26,12,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","All together, we interpret this as indicating that, even if present at the micro-level, Fisherian effects may be countered by broader, opposing forces that tend to dominate. ECB Working Paper Series No 3108 25"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,0,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","view that the inflation-financial instability relation is purely driven by the economic stress associated with adverse (inflationary) energy price shocks, as demand side inflation is documented to be relevant as well."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,1,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Symmetrically, the relevance of supply-side inflationary shocks rules out that the inflation-financial stability relation can solely be explained by the financial imbalances associated with a high-inflation booming economy stimulated by financial exuberance (in line with this, lending growth does not play a significant role in our results)."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,2,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","In differencing out monetary policy responses, we are also able to confirm that the financial stability effect exists separate from simply prompting a monetary policy reaction to a spike in inflation."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,3,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Instead, our evidence is consistent with the idea that inflation impairs financial stability due to its redistributive repercussions."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,4,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Inflation erodes households’ real income, hindering their ability to meet debt obligations, especially in low-income groups where basic necessities dominate consumption."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,5,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.",This is evident in the stronger inflation-crisis link when wage growth is low or the mortgage ratio is high.3 Also other factors contribute to shape the inflation financial stability link.
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,6,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","One interesting finding is that inflation’s impact on crisis probability is intensified when central banks have limited independence, possibly reflecting heightened risks of de-anchoring and the related broad-based instability implications."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,7,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","Inflation is also particularity harmful when banks face fragile funding, larger funding gaps and higher exposure to wholesale (unstable) funding, indicating that the the inflation-induced instability activates a bank-lending channel."
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,27,8,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","3For instance, in countries with an 80% mortgage-to-GDP ratio (which corresponds to the 90th percentile of the distribution in the estimation sample but is still lower than levels reached in the more recent past in some advanced economies), a 1pp rise in inflation results in a 7pp rise in the crisis probability (Table 6). ECB Working Paper Series No 3108 26"
ecb.wp3108~6e2f292534.en.pdf,28,0,"The causal effect of inflation on financial stability, evidence from history","Ugo Albertazzi, James ’t Hooft, Lucas ter Steege",D:20250902115023+02'00',D:20250829102522Z,"In contrast to the conventional Fisherian view that inflation reduces real debt positions, we show that significant increases in inflation are strongly associated with financial crises. In the spirit of Jordà et al. (2020), countries with free and fixed ex-change rates can be compared to difference out the confounding reaction of monetary policy. Across a dataset of 18 advanced economies over 151 years, we show that the impact of inflation extends beyond its indirect effect via monetary policy. To further corroborate causality, we instrument inflation with oil supply shocks, finding that a 1pp rise in inflation doubles the probability of financial crisis from its sample average. We give evidence for the redistribution channel, where inflationary shocks directly cut real incomes, as a possible mechanism. In conjunction with recent literature on the dangers of rapidly tightening monetary policy, our results point to a difficult trade-off for central banks once inflation has risen.","In terms of policy implications, our findings, together with those available from the related literature, indicate that both higher inflation and higher rates are detrimental to financial stability."
