ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,12,17,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Our main conclusions extend to that environment. ECB Working Paper Series No 3110 11
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,0,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","state of the economy downward, such that E(X1|sp, a) < E(X1|sp)."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,1,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",This adjustment in the private sector’s expectations arises from the signaling effects associated with the policy action.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,2,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Importantly, the revision to expectations in equation (6) depends on the policy surprise captured by a −E(a|sp)."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,3,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","The time structure of this simple model allows us to precisely pin down the signaling effects of policy actions, a. Formally, signaling effects are defined as the revision to private sector’s expectations after the policymaker announces its policy action, a. Formally, E(X1|sp, a) − E(X1|sp), which can be shown from equation (6) to be as follows: E(X1|sp, a) −E(X1|sp) = k2(a −E(a|sp)). ("
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,4,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",7) The term k2(a −E(a|sp)) in equation (7) captures the signaling effects and illustrates the importance of controlling for the private sector’s beliefs before observing the policy action when evaluating the signaling effects of policy.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,5,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",The mere policy action being positive or negative in and of itself does not capture the signaling effects.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,6,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",What matters to evaluate signaling effects is whether the size of the policy action surprises the private sector negatively or positively.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,7,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","As we will show through a numerical exercise later in this section, a negative surprise – a fiscal package smaller than what the private sector had expected based on its assessment of the state of the economy, a < E(a|sp), – will deliver good news to the private sector."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,8,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",A smaller than expected policy action is interpreted by the private sector as evidence that the policymaker expects the economy to be in a better shape than what the private sector thought before observing the policy action.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,9,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","As a result, the private sector will review its expectations positively."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,10,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Illustrative Numerical Exercises We will now perform a few numerical exercises to illustrate the fundamental properties of signaling effects theory.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,11,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","First, to accurately assess the presence of signaling effects from an economic policy, it is essential to consider the prior beliefs of economic agents regarding the scale of the policy action."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,12,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","If the fiscal package exceeds expectations, the private sector may interpret this as an indication that the economy is in worse shape than anticipated, potentially leading to negative signaling effects on economic activity."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,13,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Second, increased uncertainty in the private sector about the state of the economy amplifies signaling effects."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,14,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Third, signaling effects do not necessarily reverse the impact of economic policies."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,13,15,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Often, signaling effects only dampen the overall impact of a policy intervention, making it more complex to determine their existence ECB Working Paper Series No 3110 12"
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,14,0,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","than what the literature has typically done – e.g., Campbell et al. ("
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,14,1,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","This feature aligns with our approach, which focuses on the differential effects of policy shocks with varying degrees of signaling."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,14,2,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Finally, policy actions perceived as unrelated to changes in economic conditions do not produce signaling effects."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,14,3,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","We set the policymaker’s response to economic condition, α = −2."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,14,4,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","The effect of a unitary change in the policy action, a, on the economic activity in the second period is given by the parameter γ = 0.5."
