ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,24,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",The timing of each announcement is identified from the Nikkei newspaper.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,25,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Fiscal spending consists of national and local government actual spending and fiscal investment and loans.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,26,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",The total size comprises loans from government financial institutions in addition to fiscal spending.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,27,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","To sum up, this simple signal-extraction model highlights the key properties of the theory of signaling effects."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,28,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","First, to correctly assess the existence of signaling effects of an economic policy, it is critical to control for economic agents’ prior beliefs about the size of the policy."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,29,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Second, the larger the private sector’s prior uncertainty, the more sizable the signaling effects."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,30,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Third, signaling effects do not necessarily reverse the effect of economic policies."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,31,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",A fiscal expansion can still boost output even though signaling effects are at play.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,32,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Signaling effects only dampen the expansionary effects of a fiscal stimulus.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,33,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",It is also important to underscore that a critical feature for an economic policy to have signaling effects is that the policy is understood to respond to economic conditions.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,19,34,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","If an economic policy is fully autonomous (δ = 0), it does not give rise to signaling effects. ECB Working Paper Series No 3110 18"
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,0,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Data To study the signaling effects of fiscal policy, we construct a novel dataset that integrates daily stock price data (the Nikkei 225 average stock price index), narrative accounts of fiscal announcements from Japanese press releases, and forecast data on government expenditure by professional forecasters."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,1,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",We examine the theory in the context of Japan since its distinctive institutional and legislative frameworks offer a unique setting to empirically study the significance of signaling effects.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,2,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Institutional Framework and Fiscal Announcements Fiscal announcements exhibit signaling effects if they prompt the private sector to revise its expectations about the current state of the economy.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,3,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Identifying such effects in the data requires pinpointing the exact moment when the government releases non-redundant information about the scale of a fiscal intervention.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,4,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",This is often difficult because fiscal policy announcements in many countries reflect information that is gradually revealed during the legislative process or because political negotiations can unpredictably shape spending measures during the process.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,5,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","A central challenge in our analysis, therefore, is to identify the moment when information about the size of the fiscal package becomes public and is no longer subject to parliamentary bargaining."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,6,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Japan’s legislative process is orderly and predictable, and it features the enactment of ad hoc fiscal packages introduced in response to events that threaten the economic outlook."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,7,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",This makes Japan an ideal setting for testing the signaling effects hypothesis.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,8,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Looking at the institutional details more closely, the Prime Minister’s Office of Japan announced twenty-two stimulus packages of supplementary budgets from April 09, 2009, to October 28, 2022."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,9,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Table 1 provides the main details of each of these packages.9 Overall, the packages show the following salient features."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,10,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","First, the scale of the announced fiscal packages is sizable."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,11,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","On average, the Japanese government’s general account budget ranged from approximately 90 to 100 trillion yen annually between 2009 and 2022."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,12,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","The values of the fiscal spending and the total size of the fiscal package reported in columns (3) and (4) of Table 1, respectively, show that the magnitude of the extra fiscal stimulus measures is substantial."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,13,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Second, the largest component of the fiscal packages pertains to the expenditure side of the 9Table 1 shows fiscal announcements starting from 2009."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,14,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",The full set of fiscal announcements starting from 1992 is reported in Appendix B.1.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,20,15,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Fiscal spending excludes the loans from government-affiliated financial institutions and tax deferrals from the total size of the fiscal package. ECB Working Paper Series No 3110 19
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,0,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Tax cuts were included in only four instances and before 2009 – February 1994, April 1998, November 1998, and October 2008."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,1,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","In addition, they were relatively modest in scale compared to the overall size of the announced fiscal packages.10 Since the primary component of the fiscal packages is increased government expenditure, our analysis focuses on this aspect of the budget."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,2,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Moreover, we focus on supplementary budgets, as they are specifically designed to address adverse events and are more likely to contain non-redundant information regarding the state of the economy."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,3,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","In contrast, regular budget measures usually constitute systematic responses to business cycle fluctuations or stem from political decisions, and are therefore less likely to provide further insight into the government’s evaluation of expected economic conditions."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,4,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Supplementary fiscal packages are issued irregularly, sometimes outside the opening hours of the stock market, with a posthumous formal ratification."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,5,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","To identify the moment of the public announcement of each fiscal package, we use the Nikkei newspaper – the major, realtime, economic and business outlet in Japan."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,6,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Since we are interested in fiscal announcements, we select news releases that report the statement of the Prime Minister and the size of the government intervention."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,7,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","In Japan, the legislative process for approving a fiscal measure comprises three main orderly phases."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,8,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","In the first phase (order stage), the Prime Minister instructs the Cabinet ministers to prepare a proposal for the supplementary budget or fiscal package."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,9,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","In the second phase (announcement stage), a public discussion between the government and the ruling parties reveals the approximate content of the fiscal package but leaves uncertainty around the scale."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,10,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","This second phase ends with a public announcement by the Prime Minister (or government official) on the most likely scale of the fiscal package, which is endorsed by the official approval by the Cabinet."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,11,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","In the third phase (ratification stage), the fiscal package is formally ratified by the Diet, typically without revisions since the measures have already gained support from the ruling parties and the Cabinet.11 Our analysis, therefore, will mostly focus on the second phase, which entails the first official announcement regarding the scale of the fiscal packages, to assess the existence of signaling effects."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,12,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","To study the effect of fiscal announcements on stock prices, we create a set of indicator variables that account for the days of information release in each of the three phases of the 10The size of the announced tax cuts with the total scale of the package in parentheses is as follows: 5.85trn.(15.25), 4trn.(16.65), 6trn. ("
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,21,13,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","11In fact, we have been confirmed by the Cabinet Office of Japan that all budgets during our sample period were approved by the Diet as proposed by the government. ECB Working Paper Series No 3110 20"
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,0,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","announcement – see the second column of Table 1.12 Consequently, we denote with the indicator variable I{Aorder t } the dates when the PM orders the preparation of a proposal for the fiscal package, with the indicator variable I{Asize t } the dates of the announcements on the size of the final fiscal packages, and with the indicator variable I{Aratify t } the dates of ratification by the Diet."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,1,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Table B.2 in Appendix B.1 reports the dates for the three distinct phases associated with each fiscal announcement for the full sample period 1992-2022.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,2,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","As we will show, the announcements in the second phase, which are informative about the size of the fiscal packages, are the most relevant to evaluate the signaling effects of fiscal policy."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,3,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","On the contrary, the information released during the first phase does not seem to be very relevant for the stock market."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,4,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",The ratification stage (third phase) seems to convey information regarding the timing of the implementation of the announced fiscal intervention.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,5,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","However, at this late stage, no changes in the size of the fiscal package are announced by government officials and, hence, no signaling effects can be detected at this time."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,6,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","As shown in Section 2, signaling effects rest upon the revelation of the actual size of the stimulus from which the private sector can learn about the government’s view on the state of the economy."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,7,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Revisions to Expectations of Government Spending We measure the private sector’s revisions to expectations about government spending using forecast data from the JCER ESP Forecasts.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,8,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Published by the Japan Center for Economic Research, this dataset compiles forecasts from professional economists for various macroeconomic variables."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,22,9,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Respondents provide projections for both government consumption and investment.13 Although these forecasts pertain to total government spending rather than to the size of a specific fiscal package, revisions in expectations of total government spending serve as a reliable proxy during months in which extraordinary fiscal packages are announced."
