ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,24,7,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","The successful bid to host the 2020 Olympics with the announcement of large public investment projects on September 8, 2013."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,24,8,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","The choice of Osaka as the host city for the 2025 Universal Exposition, which was accompanied by significant urban regeneration plans and infrastructure spending on November 24, 2018."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,24,9,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",The left chart of Figure 3 shows the percentage responses of the Nikkei 225 index for the three subsequent days to the fiscal announcements.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,24,10,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Specifically, we plot the cumulative sum of the residuals obtained by regressing the percentage change in stock prices on several control variables, normalizing the response on the day before the announcement to zero.17 In our exercise the fiscal announcement occurs between time zero and one (the shaded area in the figure), and the change in stock prices at time one represents the immediate response of stock prices that cannot be explained by changes in the control variables."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,24,11,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","The effect of the three expansionary fiscal announcements is positive on stock prices on average (red-solid line with circle markers), 17The data and the estimated equations are described in the next section, equation (8)."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,24,12,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",Note that we here estimate equation (8) by varying the daily horizon of the regressand from 0 to 3 days in order to obtain the dynamic responses. ECB Working Paper Series No 3110 23
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,0,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","and differences in the responses of stock prices to the separate announcements are sizable, ranging from around 3.5% in response to the winning bid of the 2020 Olympics to around 1% in the case of the Universal Exposition."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,1,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","These responses represent a preliminary benchmark, showing that stock markets responded positively to the announcements of “exogenous” fiscal packages, which are arguably free of signaling effects according to theory."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,2,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",We compare these three benchmark responses of stock prices against those of the thirty-four supplementary fiscal policy measures that the Prime Minister’s Office announced outside the regular budget cycles over the period 2009 −2022 aimed at counteracting economic difficulties.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,3,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",A list of these measures has been provided in Table 1.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,4,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",We consider the days when the Prime Minister’s office announces the size of the package (the second phase).
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,5,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",These fiscal packages are not “exogenous” and their effects on the economy may be dampened or reversed by signaling effects according to the theory highlighted in Section 2.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,6,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","The right chart of Figure 3 shows that the percentage change in stock prices to the supplementary fiscal announcements covers a wide range of values, comprising positive and negative responses, and resulting in an average change of stock prices close to zero, as can be seen by looking at the red-solid line with circle markers."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,7,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","On the first day after the announcement of the size of the packages, the response of stock prices is equally split between negative responses and positive responses."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,8,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",A similar finding emerges if one looks at the responses of stock prices on the days of the fiscal announcements.
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,9,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Unlike the three large “exogenous” fiscal announcements, these supplementary budget measures are intended to stabilize the economy in the face of a looming recession, potentially signaling the government’s expectations regarding the severity of the economic outlook to the private sector."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,10,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Consequently, the impact of these fiscal measures could potentially offset the positive response of stock prices that is observed with the three large “exogenous” fiscal news."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,11,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","While these findings only suggest the possibility of signaling effects, this preliminary analysis is helpful for identifying whether any evidence of such effects might be present in the data."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,25,12,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.",The varying responses between the two charts indicate that a more formal investigation could provide clearer insights into the existence and quantitative significance of these effects.
