ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,58,0,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","Acknowledgements We thank Efrem Castelnuovo, Fiorella De Fiore, Andreas Dibiasi, Laura Gáti, Daisuke Ikeda, Evi Pappa, Roberto Perotti, Morten Ravn, Sarah Zubairy, and participants at Barcelona Summer Forum, NBER Mid-Year Meeting on Methods and Applications for DSGE Models, SED, CFE, EEA, ESSIM, Ecosta, IAAE, 22nd Work-shop on Macroeconomic Dynamics: Theory and Applications, ETM Lancaster, WU Vienna University, Banque de France, Bank for International Settlements, Bank of Japan, Bocconi University, Bundesbank, Danmarks Nationalbank, the Cabinet Office of Japan, European Central Bank, Hitotsubashi University, IMF, NIESR, Norges Bank, Sveriges Riksbank, and the University of Tokyo for valuable comments and suggestions."
ecb.wp3110~f44ac33453.en.pdf,58,1,The signaling effects of fiscal announcements,"Leonardo Melosi, Hiroshi Morita, Anna Rogantini Picco, Francesco Zanetti",D:20250910142949+02'00',D:20250826093010Z,"Announcing a large fiscal stimulus may signal the government’s pessimism about the severity of a recession to the private sector, impairing the stabilizing effects of the policy. Using a theoretical model, we show that these signaling effects occur when the stimulus exceeds expectations and are more noticeable during periods of high economic uncertainty. Analysis of a new dataset of daily stock prices and fiscal news in Japan supports these predictions. We introduce a method to identify fiscal news with different degrees of signaling effects and find that such effects weaken or, in extreme cases, even completely undermine the stabilizing impact of the announcements.","This research was funded by the British Academy, the Grant-in-Aid for Young Scientists 19K13652 and the Nomura Foundation."
